HT_DATA.load("detail", {"4Z2uzEM0ugY":{"wy":"HN invokes it in nearly every content moderation and censorship thread as the most eloquent case for free speech.","q":{"x":"There is a brilliant speech by the late Christopher Hitchens [0] on free speech that I must have seen a dozen times.","i":23039298,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25697134,"author":"arminiusreturns","month":"2021-01","story":"Apple has threatened to ban Parler from the App Store","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Commenter rewatched Hitchens videos repeatedly","quote":"I think I have rewatched these two videos of Christopher Hitchens on the topic a good half dozen times each","text":"My point is that you are then deigning someone to have the power to decide what is tolerant and what is not, when that is not nearly as acceptable or uncontroversial as you seem to be assuming. For example: > If progressives are pushing for more freedom, more tolerance, then to oppose it you have to, by definition, advocate for less of those. The framing of this is not correct. The problem is that not all \"progressives\" are pushing for that, which then undermines the assumption that opposition is therefor automatically intolerant and therefor automatically bannable/deplatformable. Some of the real progressives I follow such as Graham Elwood and Jimmy Dore have frequently had issues with their platforms giving them a hard time (demonteizing, etc) just because their positions or even topics of conversation are controversial, and many of them frequently mention they wouldn't be surprised to be deplatformed for said reason. To me, the real crux of your position, is that by completely bucking the standard of free speech in which we have legal definitions for speech that isn't tolerable, positions like yours seem to want to expand those definitions (libel, defamation, imminent incitement to violence, etc) to a point that you must now designate some non-legal entitiy as the moral arbiter of what is intolerant/tolerable, and that is indeed a most slippery slope. I think I have rewatched these two videos of Christopher Hitchens on the topic a good half dozen times each in the last yea"},{"id":24901736,"author":"arminiusreturns","month":"2020-10","story":"Twitter still hasn't unlocked the New York Post's account","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Whole speech is quotable on audience's right to hear","quote":"bonus (the whole speech is quotable)","text":"I've said it before and I'll say it again; it's the same justification that has been used to justify moves away from democracy. \"Those poor dumb plebs, they just don't know any better, we, the elites, know whats best, so we should reduce the amount of democracy!\" [1] 1. [LINK] You say \"some moderation is required to prevent the apparent acceptance of some pretty hateful views along with conspiracy theories\", but the entire point is that then you are giving over to some other person or entity the power and ability to decide what you get to read. I think this should be rejected as a slippery slope made of ky-jelly. \"Don't take security in the false refuge of consensus and the feeling that whatever you think you're bound to be okay because you're in the safely moral majority.\" \"It is not just the right of the person who speaks to be heard, it is the right of everyone in the audience to listen, and to hear, and every time you silence somebody, you make yourself a prisoner of your own action, because you deny yourself the right to hear something.\" - Christopher Hitchens [2] 2. [VIDEO] bonus (the whole speech is quotable): \"Dr. Johnson was waited upon by various delegations of people, to congratulate him of the nobility of the quality; of the Commons; the Lords; and also by a delegation of respectable ladies of London, who tended on him in his Fleet Street lodgings and congratulated him: \"Dr. Johnson,\" they said, \"We are delighted to find that you have not included any indecent or "},{"id":23039298,"author":"dlivingston","month":"2020-05","story":"The Inevitable Coronavirus Censorship Crisis","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Brilliant speech on freedom for those who think differently","quote":"There is a brilliant speech by the late Christopher Hitchens [0] on free speech that I must have seen a dozen times.","text":"There is a brilliant speech by the late Christopher Hitchens [0] on free speech that I must have seen a dozen times. An excerpt: “The freedom of speech is meaningless unless it means the freedom of the person who thinks differently... Don't take refuge in the false security of consensus, and the feeling that whatever you think you're bound to be okay because you're in the safely moral majority. ...Bear in mind, ladies and gentlemen, that every time you violate or propose to violate the free speech of someone else, you, in potentia, are making a rod for your own back.” [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20616914,"author":"Reedx","month":"2019-08","story":"8chan goes dark after hardware provider discontinues service","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains why free speech is vital","quote":"his talk is worth watching for a strong understanding of why free speech is so vital.","text":"Christopher Hitchens yells fire in a crowded room here: [VIDEO] More importantly, his talk is worth watching for a strong understanding of why free speech is so vital."},{"id":20199984,"author":"chroma","month":"2019-06","story":"YouTube CEO Susan Wojcicki addresses hate speech controversy","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hitchens' best oration against hate speech laws","quote":"The oration by Hitchens is his best.","text":"Back in 2007 Christopher Hitchens argued that Canada's hate speech laws are not only immoral but self-contradictory. The motion: \"Be it resolved: Freedom of speech includes the freedom to hate.\" Hitchens was fully in favor of that statement.[1] His argument has three parts. First, freedom of speech isn't about the rights of the speaker. It's about the rights of people who want to listen to that person's ideas. Every time you silence someone, you are infringing upon the rights of that audience. The audience might not agree. Maybe they want to understand the speaker's ideas so they can refute them. But if you prevent the audience from hearing those views, they can't refute them, and the bad views continue to spread. Second, censoring requires delegating a censor. But if you follow the first argument, you'll see that a censor decides for you what you are allowed to read or hear. I'm an adult and can decide that for myself, thank you very much. Third, if you are going to censor hate speech, then you must censor every major religious text. The bible and the quran contain passages encouraging hatred of homosexuals, subjugation of women, and taking of slaves. By our standards, they are utter abominations and any impartial application of a hate speech law would see these books banned. The oration by Hitchens is his best. He starts off by yelling \"Fire!\" to the audience, and explains just why the analogy of yelling fire in a crowded theater is a terrible one. (Mainly that it was coine"},{"id":16354719,"author":"0x7f800000","month":"2018-02","story":"Is America having second thoughts about free speech?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Eloquent defense of free speech","quote":"Christopher Hitchens summarized my own opinion when he gave this speech, and he did it far more eloquently than I ever could","text":"First, the First Amendment protects the speaker from being punished by the State for his speech. That's it. It does not protect the speaker from losing their job, or social ostracism, or anything else. Second, no one is rationally a \"free speech absolutist,\" which is an absurd position. For example, no one thinks their doctor has a right to lie to them, and that deliberate lies by a doctor to a patient must not be punished by the state. Third, Christopher Hitchens summarized my own opinion when he gave this speech, and he did it far more eloquently than I ever could, so I'll just link to the speech here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32680720,"author":"jesushax","month":"2022-09","story":"Backsliding on free expression around the world needs to end","stance":"rec","why":"Hitchens' deeper philosophical case for free speech","quote":"Here's a good talk from Hitchens on the topic","text":"That is the lowest possible bar for free speech, which as a philosophical principle goes way further. Here's a good talk from Hitchens on the topic: [VIDEO] I'm not a free speech person, but since I think you're unwittingly falling for a strawman I think I can help. Contrapoints also made a really good response (against) hitchens you can find here: [LINK]"},{"id":27670094,"author":"Shosty123","month":"2021-06","story":"YouTube reinstates Right Wing Watch","stance":"rec","why":"Hitchens on protecting offensive speakers","quote":"I like Christopher Hitchen's take on matters like these","text":"I like Christopher Hitchen's take on matters like these: \"And one person gets up and says, “you know what, this holocaust, I’m not even sure it happened. In fact, I’m pretty certain it didn’t. Indeed, I begin to wonder if the only thing is that the Jews brought a little bit of violence on themselves.”—That person doesn’t just have a right to speak, that person’s right to speak must be given extra protection. Because what he has to say must have taken him some effort to come up with. Might contain a grain of historical truth; might, in any case, give people to think why do they know what they think they already know. How do I know I know this except I’ve always been taught this and never heard any thing else? It’s always worth establishing first principle….don’t take refuge in the false security of consensus and the feeling that, whatever you think, you’re bound to be okay because you’re in the safely, moral majority\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":25673929,"author":"tdons","month":"2021-01","story":"Facebook Indefinitely Suspends Trump","stance":"rec","why":"Hitchens addresses the fire example at the start","quote":"If you have a few minutes, check the beginning of this talk [1] about free speech.","text":"Christopher Hitchens (a Brit, can we still call them European?) would disagree with you. If you have a few minutes, check the beginning of this talk [1] about free speech. He treats the exact \"fire\" example at the very start of the talk :-) [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18245659,"author":"walru","month":"2018-10","story":"Are Black Holes Actually Dark Energy Stars?","stance":"rec","why":"Warning against false security of consensus","quote":"I would much rather live in a world where this person may be heard than likewise.","text":"I would much rather live in a world where this person may be heard than likewise. \"Don't take refugee in the false security of consensus.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":33856097,"author":"jakeogh","month":"2022-12","story":"The War on General Purpose Computing","stance":"reference","why":"Hitchens clip cited in support of separating speech from violence","quote":"","text":"That's ludicrous grouping the first (free speech) with the other two violent things. Quite a bit of effort goes into blurring these lines. It's not good. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29232612,"author":"arminiusreturns","month":"2021-11","story":"YouTube takes down independent court livestreams","stance":"reference","why":"Hitchens speaking on freedom of speech","quote":"bonus: Hitchens on freedom of speech,","text":"This is why everyone should read RMS's Right to Read story. Its not just about censoring the output, they want to censor our inputs too! While the RMS parable is less about censorship and more about drm its still relevant. [1] Unfortunately there are a lot of people who are so bought into the tribalistic dialectic that, in the more recent version, they heard about all the censoring, they saw the people being censored were \"those evil others\" and said \"yeah thats ok with me!\", failing to understand the lack of principle would/will come for them next. The beauty of it all is that I think the oligarchs are overplaying their hand to the point they are going to force a pendulum swing back the other way at some point. The counter-point to that is that with techno-tyranny at some point the oligarchs are going to be able to stop the pendulum swing. 1. [LINK] bonus: Hitchens on freedom of speech, [VIDEO]"},{"id":25665200,"author":"dark_glass","month":"2021-01","story":"Twitter appears to have removed the president's last two tweets","stance":"reference","why":"Hitchens literally yells fire in a crowded room","quote":"Christopher Hitchens literally yelled \"Fire\" in a crowded room [0].","text":"To add to the sibling comments, Christopher Hitchens literally yelled \"Fire\" in a crowded room [0]. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25503459,"author":"tw25502454","month":"2020-12","story":"Laura Poitras: I Am Guilty of Violating the Espionage Act","stance":"reference","why":"Hitchens on the fire in a crowded theatre ruling","quote":"Related to the Espionage Act specifically, here is an excerpt from Christopher Hitchens [1]","text":"> Congress shall make no law ... abridging the freedom of speech, or of the press; ... The rule of law is a myth, a fig leaf hiding the fact that we are ruled by the whims of people in power. Edit: Shadowbanned. Neat. Related to the Espionage Act specifically, here is an excerpt from Christopher Hitchens [1]: \"Everyone knows the fatuous verdict of the greatly overpraised Justice Oliver Wendell Holmes who, asked for an actual example of when it would be proper to limit speech or define it as an action, gave that of shouting \"Fire!\" in a crowded theatre. \"It's very often forgotten what he was doing in that case [2] was sending to prison a group of Yiddish-speaking socialists -- whose literature was printed in a language most Americans couldn't read -- opposing President Wilson's participation in the first World War, and the dragging of the United States into this sanguinary conflict, which the Yiddish-speaking socialists had left from Russia to escape. \"In fact, it could be just as plausibly argued that the Yiddish-speaking socialists ... were the real fire fighters, were the ones who were shouting \"Fire!\" when there really was a fire in a very crowded theatre indeed.\" [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":24675979,"author":"859a48cd782e","month":"2020-10","story":"An Unprecedented Attack on Press Freedom","stance":"reference","why":"Hitchens references WWI dissent law in opening","quote":"Christopher Hitchens references that in the opening of his defense of free speech","text":"> a century-old law passed in order to criminalize dissent to US participation in WWI Christopher Hitchens references that in the opening of his defense of free speech: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29235755,"author":"AussieWog93","month":"2021-11","story":"YouTube takes down independent court livestreams","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"see how wrong he was about Religion and why they were censorious","text":">Hitchens on freedom of speech, [VIDEO] Interesting to watch this with hindsight and see how wrong he was about Religion and why they were censorious."}],"r":[["PhNUjg9X4g8",1],["GKYCA3UsmrU",1],["PHe0bXAIuk0",1],["OKY6BGcx37k",1],["oAHbLRjF0vo",1],["15RTC22Z2xI",1],["4Q7FTjhvZ7Y",1],["d1EB1zsxW0k",1],["24R8JObNNQ4",1],["VscdPA6sUkc",1],["-2ZTmuX3cog",1],["np_ylvc8Zj8",1],["tlThdr3O5Qo",1],["GiYO1TObNz8",1]]},"-k3mVnRlQLU":{"wy":"HN commenters love Cliff Stoll's earnest, playful personality and point to this as a delightful look at his unusual warehouse.","q":{"x":"Cliff is an amazing person. Just a joy!","i":24267125,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":39159461,"author":"fsckboy","month":"2024-01","story":"The KGB, the Computer and Me – The Cuckoo's Egg Story (1990) [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Must-see Numberphile video with Cliff Stoll.","quote":"but it's a must see","text":"this numberphile/Cliff Stoll video is on a different topic, klein bottles... or maybe more the travelling salesman/knapsack problem, but it's a must see. Cliff is a cool guy (whose geewhiz excitement I can only take in small doses but) he's worth following [VIDEO]"},{"id":24267125,"author":"Balgair","month":"2020-08","story":"Being OK with not being extraordinary","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Cliff is a joy to watch.","quote":"Cliff is an amazing person. Just a joy!","text":"Cliff is an amazing person. Just a joy! Every time I see him on Numberphile , my life is made better. [LINK] [VIDEO] P.S. Cliff, we know you're on HN. Please continue being yourself, it seems like a really great person to be!"},{"id":12244413,"author":"joshschreuder","month":"2016-08","story":"Ask HN: What book have you given as a gift?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Cliff Stoll is delightful to watch.","quote":"He is such an enjoyment to watch.","text":"I thought I recognised the name... turns out Cliff is the guy from the Numberphile videos who loves klein bottles. He is such an enjoyment to watch. [VIDEO]"},{"id":39257511,"author":"superice","month":"2024-02","story":"Acme Klein Bottle","stance":"rec","why":"Playlist of Numberphile videos with Cliff Stoll.","quote":"here is a playlist of videos Numberphile did on Klein Bottles","text":"If you don't happen to have the time to visit him, here is a playlist of videos Numberphile did on Klein Bottles, his basement warehouse, the intrinsic curvature of pizza, and a whole bunch of other interesting things: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35241845,"author":"jacknews","month":"2023-03","story":"How a Berkeley Eccentric Beat the Russians – Then Made Useless, Wondrous Objects","stance":"rec","why":"Remote controlled mini-forklift retrieving Klein bottles from crawlspace.","quote":"He keeps already-made klein bottles in the crawlspace under his house","text":"Bah they forgot to mention the best bit. He keeps already-made klein bottles in the crawlspace under his house, and built a remote controlled fpv mini-forklift to retrieve them on demand: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25812813,"author":"garaetjjte","month":"2021-01","story":"Running a fake power plant on the internet for a month","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining RC forklift storing Klein bottles under his house.","quote":"Everything this guy does is hilarious and entertaining.","text":"Everything this guy does is hilarious and entertaining. See later talk about this event: [LINK] And he drives RC forklift under his house to store Klein bottles he's selling! [VIDEO]"},{"id":20247333,"author":"jacobolus","month":"2019-06","story":"Get an Acme Klein Bottle","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the Klein bottle operation.","quote":"To learn about the operation, watch the Numberphile video","text":"To learn about the operation, watch the Numberphile video, “The man with 1,000 Klein Bottles UNDER his house” [VIDEO]"},{"id":10557774,"author":"newman314","month":"2015-11","story":"Cutting a Klein Bottle in Half [video]","stance":"rec","why":"How Cliff Stoll stores Klein bottles under his house.","quote":"You should also watch the related video about how he stores Klein bottles under his house. It's fascinating.","text":"You should also watch the related video about how he stores Klein bottles under his house. It's fascinating. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29619609,"author":"JshWright","month":"2021-12","story":"4D Toys: Klein Bottles","stance":"reference","why":"Shows his pretty unique warehouse.","quote":"His warehouse is pretty unique","text":"No discussion of Klein bottles would be complete without mentioning Cliff Stoll's Acme Klein Bottle company. [LINK] His warehouse is pretty unique: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27686905,"author":"lopis","month":"2021-06","story":"A foreign seller has hijacked my Amazon Klein bottle listing","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as 2015 source on Klein bottle ordering.","quote":"","text":"So in 2015 [1] you said you ordered enough bottles for about 10 years, but you're already running out? That's quite the success story you have there! [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25402513,"author":"jacobolus","month":"2020-12","story":"Earnestness","stance":"reference","why":"Example of earnest and playful person.","quote":"Cliff Stoll is the most earnest person I have talked to in the past year, and also one of the most playful and fun.","text":"> Earnestness, for example, is pretty far away from my conception of \"playful\" or \"flirty\" or even \"fun\". Cliff Stoll is the most earnest person I have talked to in the past year, and also one of the most playful and fun. See e.g. [VIDEO] I think you have a non-standard personal definition of “earnest”. As I understand the words, earnestness and playfulness are completely orthogonal/unrelated attributes."},{"id":24277245,"author":"loco5niner","month":"2020-08","story":"Being OK with not being extraordinary","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the little warehouse full of Klein bottles under his house.","quote":"","text":"> Of course, these days, he's the best dang Klein bottle glass blower in the game. And of course, he hires out the manufacturing. That's why he has (an awesome, little) warehouse under his hour full of them. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16148224,"author":"R_haterade","month":"2018-01","story":"CES Was Full of Useless Robots and Machines That Don’t Work","stance":"reference","why":"Example of Cliff Stoll's crawlspace warehouse.","quote":"","text":"It's been implemented to some degree by Cliff Stoll for his klein bottle business. Or rather, he started a warehouse in his crawlspace. Same difference... [VIDEO]"},{"id":14015945,"author":"EvanAnderson","month":"2017-04","story":"Acme Klein Bottle","stance":"reference","why":"Features the forklift in question.","quote":"Numberphile did a video a couple of years ago that featured the forklift in question","text":"Numberphile did a video a couple of years ago that featured the forklift in question: [VIDEO] Cliff is a really neat guy. I met and talked to him briefly once (at Dayton Hamvention, 20ish years ago). He's a really, really nice guy."}],"r":[["2JM2oImb9Qg",1],["k1kndKWJKB8",1],["EACkiMRT0pc",1],["7Hk9jct2ozY",1],["gB9n2gHsHN4",1],["FkKPsLxgpuY",1],["qJZ1Ez28C-A",1],["cidchWg74Y4",1],["KTzGBJPuJwM",1],["iph500cPK28",1],["Og6cTlwBTrk",1],["qhbuKbxJsk8",1],["86gyW0vUmVs",1],["n8m7lFQ3njk",1],["Ygty9HxhFK4",1],["FJJTYQYB1JQ",1],["re96UgMk6GQ",1],["bAxRYrpbnuA",1]]},"8Z685OF-PS8":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as a clear explanation of why data-centric networking fits how the internet is actually used, and as a core talk on content addressing.","q":{"x":"This video explains very clearly how this is the case","i":3873007,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":16840892,"author":"contingencies","month":"2018-04","story":"Ask HN: What is your favourite tech talk?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Insights on network effects and historical tidbits.","quote":"Watching this now. It's great.","text":"Watching this now. It's great. Thanks! Not only are there some nice restatements of powerful high-level insights in to network effects in general (which I am considering with respect to physical logistics requirements in our current business), but some great historical tidbits too. The resounding rejection of packet switching by period communications experts at [VIDEO] is awesome. Oh how the mighty have fallen!"},{"id":3873007,"author":"runeks","month":"2012-04","story":"Free Speech and DDOS Attacks","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains data-centric networking versus location-centric TCP/IP.","quote":"This video explains very clearly how this is the case","text":"The reason we haven't solved DDoS attacks is because we're working on solving the problem at the wrong level. We're trying to solve the wrong problem. This video explains very clearly how this is the case: [VIDEO] Short(er) explanation: we no longer use the internet primarily for the purpose of communicating between two parties. But the infrastructure of the internet forces us to obtain data by having a conversation with a supposedly trusted party, even though a line of communication is not really what we want; we want a specific piece of data (an internet page, the latest exchange rate of USD to EUR, the ISO for the daily image of Ubuntu 12.04, etc.). It doesn't matter where we get it from (location is irrelevant), it only matters that it's the right data. Our use of the internet today is all about data. Yet, the infrastructure of the internet (TCP/IP) is location-centric; not data-centric. Data is shuttled between computers based, not on the data, but on the location of the computers. This creates the possibility of DoS attacks. The TCP/IP protocol is designed to efficiently route packages from a source to a destination, and a DoS attack simply uses this feature maliciously. When someone is hit by a DoS attack, the routers aiding in this are just following the rules of TCP/IP: routing packages to a destination. That's their job. What we need is a data-centric infrastructure. An infrastructure where data is not obtained via a connection to some location , but an infrastructu"},{"id":2207903,"author":"pohl","month":"2011-02","story":"A father knows best: Vint Cerf re-thinks the Internet in Stanford talk","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent Google tech talk on rethinking networking.","quote":"(an excellent google tech talk)","text":"I wasn't able to pull anything out of this article. Perhaps I read it too quickly. Did anyone get a sense of what sort of re-thinking he has in mind? Something content-centric, perhaps? [LINK] [VIDEO] (an excellent google tech talk) [LINK]"},{"id":17625339,"author":"some1else","month":"2018-07","story":"Ask HN: Where can one learn about the history of the internet and the protocols?","stance":"rec","why":"Van Jacobson explains telephony and networking paradigms, proposes content addressing.","quote":"proposes a content-addressing based replacement for the IP protocol","text":"[VIDEO] This April 2006 Google Tech Talk by Van Jacobson of PARC proposes a content-addressing based replacement for the IP protocol. But before doing so, he explains what shaped the telephony and the current networking paradigms."},{"id":9666202,"author":"gwu78","month":"2015-06","story":"Re: Proposed Statement on \"HTTPS everywhere for the IETF\"","stance":"rec","why":"Van Jacobson on authenticating content rather than connections.","quote":"Van Jacobsen gave a good talk on this at Google","text":"\"authentication of the content, not the connection\" Popular websites that hold themselves out as businesses, i.e. \"exclusive\" sources of content (often generated by users, go figure), they have no reason to support this concept. Because then it does not matter where the user gets the content. But they might have reasons to support TLS. One could argue users want authentic content (signed content), not authentic \"websites\" (single sources for content trying to serve too many users, all at the same time). Or maybe many users do not know the difference? Van Jacobsen gave a good talk on this at Google: [VIDEO] There is another thread on the HN front page right now about a Blackhat conference talk on x86 CPUs. There is another talk on that page about how TLS relies on the trustworthiness of internet routing. What is the point of securing connections when you have no control over routing? Instead of relying on securing \"connections\", I think schemes that send out \"encrypted blobs\" with the hope they arrive at the proper destination make more sense. Encrypting blobs is not something for which an \"authority\" is needed. This is something over which the user can retain full control without involving third parties. As it should be. TLS might have encryption that works well enough to \"secure a connection\" but if I am not mistaken it still has no reliable way to verify an endpoint (recipient) is the one you want it to be. Some people call that \"authentication\". I'm not even sure that TLS "},{"id":2558217,"author":"dmuino","month":"2011-05","story":"Netflix Now The Largest Single Source of Internet Traffic In North America","stance":"rec","why":"Van Jacobson presentation on content centric networking.","quote":"Van Jacobson gave a very informative presentation at Netflix about it","text":"As I mentioned elsewhere Netflix does use Level3, Akamai, and Limelight. So the traffic is mainly coming from CDNs. I'm not sure if by \"content-aware routers\" you mean something like Content Centric Networking: [LINK] If that's the case it'd be up to the ISPs to push that technology to their infrastructure. Van Jacobson gave a very informative presentation at Netflix about it a couple of months ago. It seems you can watch a similar presentation he gave at Google on youtube: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["bKGhvKyjgLY",1],["lw6TaiXzHAE",1],["ISAKc9gpGjw",1],["XDM3MqHln8U",1],["OO18F4aKGzQ",1],["RIctzAQOe44",1]]},"yL_-1d9OSdk":{"wy":"Beloved as a humor masterpiece and often cited as required viewing on what not to do in an academic talk.","q":{"x":"The presentation is truly a humor masterpiece.","i":35746568,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":40715360,"author":"Joel_Mckay","month":"2024-06","story":"A long guide to giving a short academic talk (2022)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows common presentation errors; should be required viewing for academics.","quote":"This should be required viewing for every academic author.","text":"Or one can quickly learn how to achieve clear presentations by indexing the common errata: \"Chicken chicken chicken\" (Yoram Bauman) [VIDEO] This should be required viewing for every academic author. =3"},{"id":35494956,"author":"fbdab103","month":"2023-04","story":"Trashcan – web based service for indexing scientific papers, or other documents","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Beautiful academic satire, low quality recording, great ending Q&A.","quote":"Do not miss the audience question at the end. Beautiful academic satire.","text":"That chicken paper always gets a chuckle from me. In case you have never seen the video[0]. Unfortunately, it appears to have been recorded on a potato. Do not miss the audience question at the end. Beautiful academic satire. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35746568,"author":"jerf","month":"2023-04","story":"Chicken Chicken Chicken: Chicken Chicken [pdf]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Humor masterpiece satirizing standard academic presentations, funny at every level.","quote":"The presentation is truly a humor masterpiece.","text":"If you've read a lot of papers and/or attended conference presentations, you will find that you almost know what he is saying in the presentation version: [VIDEO] . The same charts in every presentation; up and to the right, my thing higher than previous thing, here's some boxes and lines that really mean nothing in this context, some standardized statistical distribution with my dots scattered about... there's a fairly standard presentation that one could give with almost just a few made up payload words for your thing and it would apply to almost anything. I particularly remember in one of my machine learning classes where we had to give project presentations, the degree to which you could almost substitute the slide decks for each other and it would have been hard to notice. It doesn't mean that they have no content necessarily, merely that there is some ground to riff on here. I particularly like the presentation as one of the few bits of humor I know where you can make a two year old laugh and you can make a veteran professor laugh, and everyone in between, yet they are all laughing about different things at incredibly different levels of intellectual sophistication. The presentation is truly a humor masterpiece."},{"id":18406454,"author":"jerf","month":"2018-11","story":"A History of the Sentence \"Buffalo buffalo buffalo buffalo buffalo.\" (1999-2015)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Video presentation of the paper, better than reading it; funny commentary.","quote":"It is probably the better way to approach the paper, due to the importance of timing in comedy","text":"It may not be the original video, but a video presentation of it is available at [VIDEO] by the original author, contrary to the link's claims. It is probably the better way to approach the paper, due to the importance of timing in comedy and the fact that paper presents so much to you in a way that it can't control the timing, though unfortunately the video resolution is quite low. Plus the Q&A is pretty good. It is an amusing combination of video that is a trenchant commentary on the excessive similarity of the vast bulk of hard science papers, and a video that my four year old enjoyed back in the day. That puts your standard \"family friendly\" comedy movie to shame in terms of how much comedy ground it is capable of covering."},{"id":45814034,"author":"Joel_Mckay","month":"2025-11","story":"Learning to read Arthur Whitney's C to become smart (2024)","stance":"rec","why":"Teaches better presentations by demonstrating what not to do.","quote":"Doug Zongker's presentation similarly instructs people how to make better presentations to peers by demonstrating what not to do","text":"\"Still it's amusing how Whitney's style seems to personally offend people\" So it was for the lols after all... Notably, a Lisp interpreter written in Lisp is a common assignment for second year CS students. In a production setting, there is little reason to misappropriate resources for such toys. Bud Haggert still teaches smart people something very important about communication with interdisciplinary crowds: [LINK] Doug Zongker's presentation similarly instructs people how to make better presentations to peers by demonstrating what not to do: [VIDEO] Best of luck =3"},{"id":33674167,"author":"JamesCoyne","month":"2022-11","story":"I quit my programmer job to become a chicken","stance":"rec","why":"Good presentation of the same material as the paper.","quote":"Doug also gives a really good presentation on the same material","text":"Yeah, the paper provides good insight into the phenomenon. Doug also gives a really good presentation on the same material: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5970508,"author":"baggers","month":"2013-07","story":"Show HN: Chicken chicken chicken – chicken chicken programming language","stance":"rec","why":"Presentation detailing the project's motivations and implementation.","quote":"Do check out the presentation","text":"Do check out the presentation ( [VIDEO] ) as he goes into a fairy bit of detail on the motivations and implementation of the project. Fairly moving actually"},{"id":35747956,"author":"feyman_r","month":"2023-04","story":"Chicken Chicken Chicken: Chicken Chicken [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as a source in an AI-generated explanation of the paper.","quote":"","text":"Almost like a test from my POV (non-American) ;). From Bing/ChatGPT: The purpose of this paper is not clear from the current web page context, which consists of mostly the word \"chicken\" repeated in various ways. However, based on web search results, it seems that this paper is a *humorous presentation* by Doug Zongker, a computer scientist from the University of Washington¹. He created this paper to mock the format and style of scientific papers and presentations, and to demonstrate how little content and meaning can be conveyed by using jargon and graphs³. The paper was presented at the AAAS humor session in 2007¹³, and has been viewed online by many people who find it amusing²³. Source: Conversation with Bing, 4/28/2023(1) Chicken Chicken Chicken: Chicken Chicken - isotropic.org. [LINK] Accessed 4/28/2023. (2) Chicken chicken chicken - YouTube. [VIDEO] Accessed 4/28/2023. (3) Chicken, Chicken, Chicken | The Pioneer Woman | Food Network. [LINK] Accessed 4/28/2023."},{"id":47807502,"author":"teddyh","month":"2026-04","story":"IETF draft-meow-mrrp-00","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"(Also available in video format","text":"Disappointingly, this draft does not reference previous important and influential work in this field: < [LINK] >. (Also available in video format: < [VIDEO] >)"},{"id":37962881,"author":"fghorow","month":"2023-10","story":"The Shortest Papers Ever Published","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"the (in)famous \"chicken\" talk[1]?","text":"Hmm. I'm wondering how the short lengths of these papers might cause them to have lower information-theoretic entropy than the (in)famous \"chicken\" talk[1]? [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27149837,"author":"userbinator","month":"2021-05","story":"Enigma: GPT-2 trained on 10K Nature Papers: Can you spot the difference?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"(Related","text":"Some of the fake ones are hilarious: The chicken genome (the genome of a chicken that is the subject of much chicken-related activity) is now compared to its chicken chicken-to-pecking age: from a genome sequence of chicken egg, only approximately 70% of the chicken genome sequences match the chicken egg genome, which suggests that the chicken may have beenancreatic. (Related: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":24691987,"author":"netsharc","month":"2020-10","story":"A ritual for your product increases satisfaction and spending","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"The simplistic text and the 2 random graphs in the middle made me think of [VIDEO] ... Apparently each iProduct packaging requires a few seconds to get the top off, building up anticipation (e.g. [LINK] )"},{"id":22943689,"author":"acqq","month":"2020-04","story":"The Relative Weirdness of Birds","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"The paper about which is written there is: [LINK] and, to warn anybody expecting more, ends with: \"Acknowledgements We thank Big Bird from Sesame Street for comments on the manuscript. Several trained monkeys transcribed videos.\" Also: \"Published Date: April 01, 2020\" In short: meh. Doesn't even appear to be funny, especially especially when \"covered\" on April 15, 2020, for my understanding of what is actually funny: [VIDEO] ."},{"id":13502030,"author":"jerf","month":"2017-01","story":"Five-month-old babies know what’s funny","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"you can try bonding once again over the collegiate-level telling of that joke","text":"'My son's in college now, and I still haven't forgotten when he was not yet walking, less than a year old, and we sat on the couch cracking each other up every time I said the word \"chicken.\" Who knows why it was funny?' If you'd like, you can try bonding once again over the collegiate-level telling of that joke: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["vs41JrnGaxc",1],["S2xHZPH5Sng",1],["t-O-Qdh7VvQ",1],["oI_X2cMHNe0",1],["XEVlyP4_11M",1],["bvim4rsNHkQ",1],["yY9GAyJtuJ0",1],["bHIhgxav9LY",1],["zwAD6dRSVyI",1],["pTn6Ewhb27k",1],["r6Rp-uo6HmI",1],["RIctzAQOe44",1],["gB9n2gHsHN4",1],["bL6hp8BKB24",1],["CaasbfdJdJg",1],["kPRA0W1kECg",1],["cefnmjtAolY",1],["ar9WRwCiSr0",1],["GFMyMxMYDNk",1],["2Vrhk5OjBP8",1]]},"cCOL7MC4Pl0":{"wy":"HN treats it as the best primer on the event loop, praising its entertaining, easy-to-grasp visual explanations.","q":{"x":"actually making it both entertaining and easily grokable!","i":18217867,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":25032014,"author":"phist_mcgee","month":"2020-11","story":"How does the event loop work in JavaScript? [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best event loop primer with a likeable, knowledgeable speaker.","quote":"The best primer for the event loop I have found is here","text":"The best primer for the event loop I have found is here: [VIDEO] The speaker (Jake Archibald) has that rare ability to be both likeable and relatable, while also being incredibly knowledgeable and enthusiastic. I wish a lot more talks were like this!"},{"id":18217867,"author":"wattengard","month":"2018-10","story":"Ask HN: What are some of the best technical talks you've heard?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Entertaining and easy-to-grasp explanation of the JavaScript event loop.","quote":"actually making it both entertaining and easily grokable!","text":"Jake Archibald talking about the javascript event loop and actually making it both entertaining and easily grokable! [VIDEO]"},{"id":18219482,"author":"olavgg","month":"2018-10","story":"Ask HN: What are some of the best technical talks you've heard?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Visual explanation of how the event loop works.","quote":"Awesome video for every javascript developer.","text":"Jake Archibald's \"In the Loop\", Visual explanation of how the javascript event loop works! Awesome video for every javascript developer. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37875431,"author":"mananaysiempre","month":"2023-10","story":"Populating the page: how browsers work","stance":"rec","why":"Superb talk on how the browser schedules tasks.","quote":"see Jake Archbald’s superb talk[1]","text":"> It basically flickers between tasks very rapidly to simulate concurrency. That’s usually used a description of preemptive multitasking, like what you get running a modern OS on a single core (on multiple cores it’s also this but on each core). Every once in a small while, the current task is made to inhale chloroform, another task is chosen to wake and takes its place. Pro: you can’t hang any given task from a single thread; con: shared memory is very difficult to impossible, as you never know when you’ll be interrupted. Browser JavaScript and UI interactions instead use cooperative multitasking, which you’ll also find in classic Mac OS, 16-bit Windows, basically every embedded system ever, and languages like Python and Lua. A task has to explicitly call out to the scheduler to hand off execution resources to the next one. Pro: as task switching only happens at clearly visible points, many of the horrors of shared memory are diminished; con: you can and will hang the UI (or other outside-world interactions) if you accidentally write an infinite loop or just do too much CPU-bound work without yielding (as any developer using one of the aforementioned systems knows). For how this works in the browser environment specifically, see Jake Archbald’s superb talk[1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36598055,"author":"cassepipe","month":"2023-07","story":"How to think about `async`/`await` in Rust","stance":"rec","why":"Helped a C developer grasp async in JS.","quote":"And also that talk on the event loop","text":"Coming from C and having used thread and being totally ignorant to javascript, this article really helped me grasp async in js by clearing away implicit misconceptions I held : [LINK] And also that talk on the event loop : [VIDEO]"},{"id":33596401,"author":"luuuzeta","month":"2022-11","story":"Ask HN: Do you recall any book or course that made a topic finally click?","stance":"rec","why":"One of several videos that helped async JS finally click.","quote":"I think it's finally clicking after watching these YouTube videos","text":"I've been struggling with wrapping my head around asynchronous programming with callbacks, promises and async/await in JS, however I think it's finally clicking after watching these YouTube videos and creating a document where I explain these concepts as if I'm teaching them to someone else: * Philip Roberts's What the heck is the event loop anyway? - [LINK] * The Story of Asynchronous JavaScript - [LINK] * JavaScript Callbacks, Promises, and Async / Await Explained - [LINK] * Async Javascript Tutorial For Beginners (Callbacks, Promises, Async Await). - [LINK] * Jake Archibald: In The Loop - setTimeout, micro tasks, requestAnimationFrame, requestIdleCallback, - [VIDEO] Edit... I've been rewatching these videos, reading the MDN docs, the Eloquent JavaScript book, javascript.info, blogs about the subject, etc. This further proves you shouldn't limit yourself to a single resource, and instead fill up the laguna with water from different sources if you will."},{"id":17467065,"author":"mxschumacher","month":"2018-07","story":"Ask HN: What you wish you'd known before getting into JavaScript?","stance":"rec","why":"One of two great talks for understanding the event loop.","quote":"Here are two great talks to help you","text":"Be sure to understand the event loop. Javascript as an asynchronous language often comes back to bite beginners. Here are two great talks to help you: [VIDEO] [LINK] Your life will be easier if you learn to use Chrome Dev tools (or the Firefox equivalent) early. In the beginning, you don't have to worry about browser compatibility - embrace modern JS (ES6+)!"},{"id":37077631,"author":"ativzzz","month":"2023-08","story":"Ask HN: Should I move away from JavaScript based skillset because of saturation?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of deep JS knowledge worth having.","quote":"the browser event loop","text":"I think there's never been a better time to be a JS developer. JS is now everywhere. It runs on browsers, it runs on servers, it runs on mobile devices. You can use it as a scripting language if you really hate python and ruby. You can compile it to webassembly. There's game engines that use JS. Typescript is great as JS projects grow larger and more complex (if you can get over the fact that reading complex types is stupid) So the more and more JS devs are coming in, the more we need JS experts who really understand how stuff works at lower levels. Like for example the browser event loop - [VIDEO] Being an expert that deeply understands a tech stack will never go out of style"},{"id":19803192,"author":"memco","month":"2019-05","story":"Release Notes for Safari Technology Preview 81","stance":"reference","why":"Talk called out Safari's rAF behavior later fixed.","quote":"Just rewatched this talk on Monday","text":"Just rewatched this talk on Monday from last year where Safari was called out for the rAF behavior that was fixed here. Cool to see this change. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["8aGhZQkoFbQ",2],["4LqZdkkBDas",1],["svEuG_ekNT0",1],["snAhsXyO3Ck",1],["s254IPHXgVA",1],["Y4Gt3Xjd7G8",1],["spUNpyF58BY",1],["ZmWREtcDVBE",1]]},"ML4tw_UzqZE":{"wy":"HN repeatedly recommends the series for its extraordinary craftsmanship and its credible demonstration of how the mechanism could have been made.","q":{"x":"Highly recommend for anyone who is fascinated by the care that goes into handmade mechanical devices.","i":18446721,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":31381192,"author":"gus_massa","month":"2022-05","story":"The Antikythera Mechanism Episode 11 – Inscribing the Back Plate – Part 1","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Reconstruction series with ancient-style tools; a time sink.","quote":"I highly recommend this serie of videos about building a reconstruction of the The Antikythera Mechanism","text":"This is a new episode after more than a year. :) I highly recommend this serie of videos about building a reconstruction of the The Antikythera Mechanism with tool similar to the ancient tools. [Warning: It's a huge time sink] Complete playlist: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20523879,"author":"abainbridge","month":"2019-07","story":"The “greased sled” theory of Stonehenge","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Credible theory of how the mechanism could be made.","quote":"I highly recommend this youtube series about how the Antikythera mechanism could have been made.","text":"If you want some archaeological engineering theories that aren't obviously wrong, I highly recommend this youtube series about how the Antikythera mechanism could have been made. [VIDEO] It's the complete antithesis of this pig fat hogwash."},{"id":18446721,"author":"btown","month":"2018-11","story":"Missing Piece of Antikythera Mechanism Found on Aegean Seabed","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Handmade reproduction of Antikythera mechanism with extraordinary care.","quote":"Highly recommend for anyone who is fascinated by the care that goes into handmade mechanical devices.","text":"Non-paywall link: [LINK] This reminds me of one of my favorite YouTube channels, Clickspring: an inspiring clockmaker and machinist who's currently documenting a full reproduction of the Antikythera Mechanism: [VIDEO] . Highly recommend for anyone who is fascinated by the care that goes into handmade mechanical devices."},{"id":38614704,"author":"nialo","month":"2023-12","story":"Earliest Carpenters: 476,000-year-old log structure discovered in Zambia","stance":"rec","why":"Clockmaker builds replica using only ancient Greek tools.","quote":"I want to share this very cool youtube series of an Australian clock maker creating a replica of the Antikythera device","text":"In case anyone here hasn't seen it, I want to share this very cool youtube series of an Australian clock maker creating a replica of the Antikythera device using only tools believed to be available to the ancient Greeks: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26444638,"author":"neatze","month":"2021-03","story":"Scientists solve another piece of the puzzling Antikythera mechanism","stance":"rec","why":"Builds the mechanism with ancient tools and methods.","quote":"he actually attempts to use ancient tools and methods to build Antikythera Mechanism.","text":"Any one interested, see Clickspring[1], he actually attempts to use ancient tools and methods to build Antikythera Mechanism. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26470049,"author":"mattbillenstein","month":"2021-03","story":"Scientists Have Unlocked the Secrets of the Ancient 'Antikythera Mechanism'","stance":"rec","why":"Watchmaker reconstructs mechanism with traditional techniques.","quote":"A watchmaker has made a video series on youtube about reconstructing the mechanism using some traditional techniques","text":"A watchmaker has made a video series on youtube about reconstructing the mechanism using some traditional techniques: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26542397,"author":"wincy","month":"2021-03","story":"YouTube and Patreon Still Aren’t Paying the Rent for Most Creatives","stance":"rec","why":"Deep-domain creator who deserves more income.","quote":"Or Clickspring, the guy who made the Antikythera Mechanism [1].","text":"If deep domain knowledge paid off on Youtube people like Ben Eater would be a millionaire from Youtube at this point[0], his videos are so cool and great at explaining computing. Or Clickspring, the guy who made the Antikythera Mechanism [1]. But honestly even for me being a software engineer and someone who considers himself interested in this sort of stuff, I look at these pages sometimes out of wonder but I often end up watching comedy videos and stuff like that on Youtube. The algorithm recommends it, and I watch. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":42433469,"author":"floxy","month":"2024-12","story":"The Antikythera mechanism – 254:19 ratio","stance":"reference","why":"Asks whether the series was ever finished.","quote":"Does anyone know if Clickspring ever finished making a copy of the mechanism?","text":"Does anyone know if Clickspring ever finished making a copy of the mechanism? Seems like the last update was from ~2 years ago. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":26435676,"author":"jdefelice","month":"2021-03","story":"A Model of the Cosmos in the Ancient Greek Antikythera Mechanism","stance":"reference","why":"Playlist recreating the Antikythera mechanism.","quote":"Clickspring has been recreating the Antikythera[1]","text":"Clickspring has been recreating the Antikythera[1] and findings[2] went into a research paper.[3] [1] Playlist: [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":23471225,"author":"qohen","month":"2020-06","story":"Ask HN: Building a Computer in Jesus's Time","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how the mechanism is supposed to have worked.","quote":"There are videos on YouTube showing how it is supposed to have worked","text":"Perhaps not exactly what you have in mind, but there is the Antikythera mechanism [0][1], the first known analog computer, dating back to about 100-200 BCE which exhibits gear-work that one doesn't see for another 1500 years or so. The device calculates the phases of the moon and the motion of the known planets and more for any date over a 76 year cycle. It takes into account the retrograde motion of Mars, can calculate the dates of eclipses, etc. There are videos on YouTube showing how it is supposed to have worked, e.g. [2]. [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["XehNK7UpZsc",1],["Qct6LKbneKQ",1],["7JUs4c99-mo",1],["5TFDG-y-EHs",1],["MiUHjLxm3V0",1],["fU9hR3kiOK0",1],["UQuIVsNzqDk",1],["0bcZb-SsnrA",1],["dKx1wnXClcI",1],["jcKRGpMiVTw",1],["s86-Z-CbaHA",1],["zUyH3XhpLTo",1],["kRlhlCWplqk",1],["gxAaO2rsdIs",1],["R2paSGQRwvo",1],["LmlAYnFF_s8",1],["r-TLSBdHe1A",1],["ph8LyNIT9sg",1]]},"79fvDDPaIoY":{"wy":"HN recommends Cantrill talks generally for their mix of industry history, technical depth and entertaining delivery, and this one is a favorite for storage and data integrity threads.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend any talk you can find of Bryan Cantrill. A wonderful mix of industry history and great delivery.","i":15975691,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":15975691,"author":"kbenson","month":"2017-12","story":"Xenserver 7.3: Changes to the Free Edition","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Mix of industry history and great delivery","quote":"I highly recommend any talk you can find of Bryan Cantrill. A wonderful mix of industry history and great delivery.","text":"Bryan Cantrill says it best, I think[1]: \"If you had to explain the Nazis to someone who had never heard of WWII but was an oracle customer, there's a very good chance you would actually explain the Nazis as an Oracle allegory.\" There are more choice quotes in someone else's HN comment about Bryan Cantrill on Oracle.[2] I highly recommend any talk you can find of Bryan Cantrill. A wonderful mix of industry history and great delivery. 1: [VIDEO] 2: [LINK]"},{"id":13853193,"author":"gbrown_","month":"2017-03","story":"Silent Data Corruption Is Real","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Manta talk with ZFS history and typical Cantrill rant","quote":"I recommend checking out this video [1] from Bryan Cantrill.","text":"If you have corruption in RAID 1 how do you know which copy is good? On a slight tangent ZFS will checksum data and store that checksum in the block pointer (i.e. not with the data itself) so it can tell which of the copies is correct. The same extends to RAID 5 and RAID 6, although with RAID 6 you can intelligently work out which block might be bad. However that is assuming the block devices are returning consistent data and you are the one talking to the block devices. If the disks were sat behind a hardware RAID controller and the controller was the one you'd be hard pressed to identify the source of the data corruption. The checksumming in ZFS comes to the rescue here again. I recommend checking out this video [1] from Bryan Cantrill. It's about Joyent's object store Manta but features a fair bit of ZFS history. Also it features the usual rant level that one can come to expect from a Bryan Cantrill talk which I quite enjoy. There are plenty of other videos available on ZFS. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12233149,"author":"gbrown_","month":"2016-08","story":"Btrfs RAID 5/6 Code Found to Be Very Unsafe and Will Likely Require a Rewrite","stance":"rec","why":"Cantrill on reliable software not being unstable for long periods","quote":"Bryan Cantrill expresses this[1] in a manner I certainly agree with.","text":"Bryan Cantrill expresses this[1] in a manner I certainly agree with. Software that is designed/ intended to be reliable should not go through large periods of instability only to be written off as \"prepubescence\". [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":10040429,"author":"Flenser","month":"2015-08","story":"“Stop reverse engineering our code”","stance":"rec","why":"Cantrill's colorful Oracle allegory rant","quote":"You might enjoy this talk by Bryan Cantrill","text":"You might enjoy this talk by Bryan Cantrill, where he describes the Oracle acquisition of Sun: [LINK] > \"what you think of Oracle is even truer than you think it is. There has been no entity in human history with less complexity or nuance to it than Oracle\" > \"this company is about one man and his alter ego and what he wants to inflict upon humanity\" Edit: And how could I forget this: [VIDEO] > \"and then, the Nazis invaded\" > \"for a while I tried to not go to Nazi allegory when talking about Oracle but I actually think that it does a dis-service to not go to Nazi allegory because if I don't use Nazi allegory when referring to Oracle there's some critical understanding that I have left on the table\""},{"id":8571529,"author":"bcantrill","month":"2014-11","story":"SmartDataCenter and Manta are now open source","stance":"rec","why":"Introduction to Manta from its creator","quote":"you may want to check out my FutureStack presentation from last year.[1]","text":"If anyone's interested in a presentation introduction to Manta (presumably similar to the one you saw), you may want to check out my FutureStack presentation from last year.[1] And if you have 90 minutes to kill and are in the mood for the true origin story of Manta, see my video from NYC DevOps in January.[2] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23635543,"author":"ahnick","month":"2020-06","story":"Show HN: Splitgraph - Build and share data with Postgres, inspired by Docker/Git","stance":"reference","why":"Cantrill on data being hard to move","quote":"IIRC Bryan Cantrill said something similar about data when Joyent was developing their object storage system Manta","text":"Personally I think I'm more drawn to the dotmesh approach ( [LINK] ), but the one problem data has is as it gets massive it becomes really hard to move it around and I guess that's where trying to layer git like workflows on top of it become intractable. It's like data has it's own gravity and often times it is just easier to bring other things to the data, rather than the other way around. IIRC Bryan Cantrill said something similar about data when Joyent was developing their object storage system Manta ( [VIDEO] ergo, perhaps the Splitgraph approach will meet with better success."},{"id":15886728,"author":"masklinn","month":"2017-12","story":"Larry Ellison allegedly tried to have a professor fired for benchmarking Oracle","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"if you have to explain the Nazis to someone who had never heard of World War 2 but was an Oracle customer","text":"And remember, > Do not fall into the trap of anthropomorphising Larry Ellison. You need to think of Larry Ellison the way you think of a lawnmower. You don't anthropomorphize your lawnmower, the lawnmower just mows the lawn, you stick your hand in there and it'll chop it off, the end. You don't think 'oh, the lawnmower hates me' -- lawnmower doesn't give a shit about you, lawnmower can't hate you. Don't anthropomorphize the lawnmower. Don't fall into that trap about Oracle. — Brian Cantrill ( [LINK] ) And > I actually think that it does a dis-service to not go to Nazi allegory because if I don't use Nazi allegory when referring to Oracle there's some critical understanding that I have left on the table […] in fact as I have said before I emphatically believe that if you have to explain the Nazis to someone who had never heard of World War 2 but was an Oracle customer there's a very good chance that you would explain the Nazis in Oracle allegory. — also Brian Cantrill ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":48377394,"author":"soraminazuki","month":"2026-06","story":"Larry Ellison: \"Citizens will be on their best behavior because we’re recording\"","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Yes, we should talk to Poland about it, it was bad.","text":"He also said later: > For a while, I tried not going into Nazi allegory when talking about Oracle, but I actually think it does a disservice to not go into Nazi allegory because if I don't use Nazi allegory when referring to Oracle, there are some critical understanding that I have left on the table. There's an element of the story you can't possibly understand. If you had to explain the Nazis to someone who had never heard of World War 2, but was an Oracle customer, there's a very good chance that you would actually explain the Nazis in Oracle allegory. So, it's like: \"Really, wow, a whole country?\" \"Yes, Larry Ellison has an entire country.\" \"Oh my god, the humanity! The license audits!\" \"Yes, we should talk to Poland about it, it was bad.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":48105423,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2026-05","story":"If AI Writes Your Code, Why Use Python?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"The reason why Java is such a terrible choice now is not technical, it's the Lawnmower Nazi argument: [LINK] masklinn on Dec 9, 2017 | parent | context | favorite | on: Larry Ellison allegedly tried to have a professor ... And remember, > Do not fall into the trap of anthropomorphising Larry Ellison. You need to think of Larry Ellison the way you think of a lawnmower. You don't anthropomorphize your lawnmower, the lawnmower just mows the lawn, you stick your hand in there and it'll chop it off, the end. You don't think 'oh, the lawnmower hates me' -- lawnmower doesn't give a shit about you, lawnmower can't hate you. Don't anthropomorphize the lawnmower. Don't fall into that trap about Oracle. — Brian Cantrill ( [LINK] ) And > I actually think that it does a dis-service to not go to Nazi allegory because if I don't use Nazi allegory when referring to Oracle there's some critical understanding that I have left on the table […] in fact as I have said before I emphatically believe that if you have to explain the Nazis to someone who had never heard of World War 2 but was an Oracle customer there's a very good chance that you would explain the Nazis in Oracle allegory. — also Brian Cantrill ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":44274384,"author":"dkbrk","month":"2025-06","story":"UK unis to cough up to £10M on Java to keep Oracle off their backs","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Yes, Larry Ellison has an entire country","text":"I actually think that it does a disservice to not go to Nazi allegory, because if I don't use Nazi allegory when referring to Oracle there is some critical understanding that I have left on the table; there is an element of the story that you can't possibly understand. In fact, as I have said before and I emphatically believe, if you had to explain the Nazis to somebody who had never heard of WWII but was an Oracle customer, there's a very good chance that you actually explain the Nazis in Oracle allegory. So, it's like: \"Really, wow, a whole country?\"; \"Yes, Larry Ellison has an entire country\"; \"Oh my god, the humanity! The License Audits!\"; \"Yeah, you should talk to Poland about it, it was bad. Bad, it was a blitzkrieg license audit.\" [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["vvZA9n3e5pc",1],["36myc8wQhLo",1],["-zRN7XLCRhc",1],["KrksBdWcZgQ",1],["IQZ_bQbxSXk",1],["6chLw2aodYQ",1],["aP0Y1uAA-2Y",1],["nXaxk27zwlk",1],["coFIEH3vXPw",1],["fE2KDzZaxvE",1],["9QMGAtxUlAc",1],["YKuRkGkf5HU",1],["5Vt8zqhHe_c",1],["fzBRYsiyxjI",1],["SFv8Wm2HdNM",1]]},"pOmXiapfCs8":{"wy":"HN commenters bring it up in threads on propaganda, influence operations and platforms like TikTok, saying it feels more relevant than ever.","q":{"x":"I feel like everyone should watch this 1985 interview of an ex-KGB agent[1]. It's more relevant today than ever before","i":42757637,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":42757637,"author":"imiric","month":"2025-01","story":"TikTok goes dark in the US","stance":"strong_rec","why":"1985 ex-KGB interview explaining sociopolitical state of the West","quote":"I feel like everyone should watch this 1985 interview of an ex-KGB agent[1]. It's more relevant today than ever before","text":"I don't get how more people don't realize this. Yes, all domestic media has also been corrupted by various agencies that wish to psychologically manipulate the masses. Some of this manipulation is to get you to buy things, while other wants to get you to think, act or vote a certain way. The difference is that when a foreign adversary has the ability to do the same, it becomes a matter of national security. Allowing that adversary to also control the platform itself is beyond unsafe. The tricky thing is that the US built these tools, and opened them up for everyone to use. This libertarian position is what will ultimately be its downfall. They can't just go and block access to these tools for everyone outside the US, or heavily regulate them, as it will cause an internal uproar, but that is what they must do in order to survive this war. China is in a much better position in this conflict since the government has total control over the media its citizens consume (barring the rampant use of VPNs, which they can shut down at any point). They have no external but massive internal influence. I feel like everyone should watch this 1985 interview of an ex-KGB agent[1]. It's more relevant today than ever before, and explains the sociopolitical state of not just the US, but of many western countries as well. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35563867,"author":"tenpies","month":"2023-04","story":"Hackers claim vast access to Western Digital systems","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Soviet defector detailing infiltration of academia","quote":"It's impossible to watch this interview and not tick the boxes.","text":"Personally, I buy much more into the theory that the fissures in society, are a result of infiltration by Marxists into academia, as was detailed by the Soviet defector and KGB agent Yuri Bezmenov. It's impossible to watch this interview and not tick the boxes.[1] Putin is just the Russian leader who happens to be around when the fruits of those labours are paying off. --- [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29658986,"author":"post_from_work","month":"2021-12","story":"Buying Influence: How China manipulates Facebook and Twitter","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Ex-KGB defector's 1985 interview on subversion of public opinion","quote":"Watch this interview with Yuri Bezmenov","text":"Watch this interview with Yuri Bezmenov: [VIDEO] Then think about all the \"Culture War\" problems we are dealing with in American for the past ~10 years."},{"id":38485627,"author":"bmitc","month":"2023-12","story":"Federal judge blocks Montana's TikTok ban before it takes effect","stance":"rec","why":"Russian agent explaining how ideological warfare works","quote":"Here is a Russian agent explaining how it works","text":"In general, that is a quite naive understanding. Today's warfare is primarily executived via electronics, economics, and ideology. Here is a Russian agent explaining how it works: [VIDEO] . Adam Curtis' documentary Hypernormalism also covers this. By the way, this extends to all social media. Whether it's a state or a state's own people, the danger remains the same."},{"id":36790704,"author":"imiric","month":"2023-07","story":"Yes, There Is a Cure for Bullshit","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I mostly agree with what you said, but critical thinking can indeed be taught in elementary education. Children can learn from an early age to question the reality presented to them, to apply analytical reasoning to arrive at their own conclusions, and perhaps most importantly in this golden age of bullshit, to be extremely critical of the information they consume online, from mainstream media sources, and even their peers. Not all children will pick up this practice equally and carry it through their adult life, but it's important that they're presented with these crucial tools that can help them be less susceptible, though not invulnerable, to information warfare. Unfortunately, education is mostly based around memorizing and regurgitating facts, rather than teaching people critical thinking skills. To be clear, we're all vulnerable to IW in varying degrees. Thinking critically at the very least makes people more skeptical of the information they consume, and lowers the chance they would spread it further and \"infect\" others. There's no easy protection against IW or a quick cure from the effects of it. It takes at least a couple of decades to educate a new generation of people to slow down the bleeding, and for the oldest \"infected\" generation to die out. But first the nation's government must realize that they are indeed a victim of IW, which is difficult to do because there's no direct link that points to it since it's an operation that spans decades, and because of the c"},{"id":35365418,"author":"imiric","month":"2023-03","story":"","stance":"reference","why":"Ex-KGB agent explaining psyops decades ago","quote":"An ex-KGB agent explained this well way back in 1985","text":"> Don't worry about the US. It is still the greatest country and will remain so for at least 50 years. That's a shortsighted and very optimistic viewpoint, dripping with typical American pride. You're underestimating just how much damage foreign propaganda and misinformation can do and has done to your great country. Trump getting to power, and still being a strong contender for 2024, mass delusion and rising popularity of conspiracy theories (QAnon, pizzagate, etc.), record-high social unrest and division, racial tensions, protests, marches, insurrections, etc. And this is just starting to unravel. The ironic thing is that all that great technology built by the US, allowing everyone to join, connecting the world and whatnot, has been the perfect weapon of information warfare. You've essentially built the weapons, and handed them to your enemies, and are somehow still debating if that's the case, and what to do about it. This confused state is indicative of the effects of psyops, where the afflicted adversary doesn't understand the situation they're in, or how they got there. An ex-KGB agent explained this well way back in 1985[1]. The East certainly has an advantage, considering they've been doing this for decades now, and Western influence on their own population is close to zero, because of their isolation from the internet. Banning TikTok is a good defensive step, but it's too little, too late, to reverse the damage already done, and to stop the bleeding. The US would hav"}],"r":[["QNznD9hMEh0",1],["pSHj5UKSylk",1],["GOGru_4z1Vc",1],["izxXGuVL21o",1],["wJyUtbn0O5Y",1],["L0KuAx1COEk",1],["7Hk9jct2ozY",1],["-J_xL4IGhJA",1],["JvBT4XBdoUE",1],["2KSahAoOLdU",1],["oP3c1h8v2ZQ",1],["xdfeXqHFmPI",1]]},"OmJ-4B-mS-Y":{"wy":"Recommended to people asking how to self-learn math as a quick bird's-eye overview of the field.","q":{"x":"I suggest this \"Map of mathematics\" as a starting point that gives you a reasonable birds-eye view of the field.","i":21350402,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":31547583,"author":"lcordier","month":"2022-05","story":"Ask HN: How to learn math from zero for adults?","stance":"rec","why":"Good overview of where you can go in mathematics.","quote":"This is a good map of Mathematics to get an overview of where you can go...","text":"This is a good map of Mathematics to get an overview of where you can go... [VIDEO] Then Giles McMullen-Klein has an awesome recommended list for data science (your mileage may vary). [LINK]"},{"id":24553416,"author":"mathnmusic","month":"2020-09","story":"Why books don't work (2019)","stance":"rec","why":"A map showing connections between math concepts.","quote":"Every subject needs a similar map.","text":"> shift this burden to the medium This is beautifully expressed. Knowledge consists of connections across concepts but those connections are rarely shown explicitly in textbooks. Even wikipedia, while allowing you to traverse the connections back and forth easily, does not do a great job of giving you the bird's eye view of a subject or a field. Check out this \"map\" of mathematics: [VIDEO] Every subject needs a similar map. Besides visualizing the connections, we'd also need interactivity to the topic that helps with forming new mental models - which is the core aspect of learning. Jupyter notebooks, ObservableHQ, Mathigon are some of the good projects working on this aspect. Disclaimer: I am building the open-source project [LINK] which is attempting to build the complete map of knowledge/skills. So I too am biased against books and in favor of digital/interactive tools."},{"id":22331304,"author":"AareyBaba","month":"2020-02","story":"A Map of Mathematics","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining 10-minute map of mathematics.","quote":"This is a much more entertaining map of mathematics in about 10 minutes","text":"This is a much more entertaining map of mathematics in about 10 minutes [VIDEO] For a history of mathematics NJ Wildberger's lectures are easy to listen to. [LINK]"},{"id":21350402,"author":"AareyBaba","month":"2019-10","story":"Ask HN: Are there books for mathematics like Feynman's lectures on physics?","stance":"rec","why":"Birds-eye view of the field of mathematics.","quote":"I suggest this \"Map of mathematics\" as a starting point that gives you a reasonable birds-eye view of the field.","text":"I suggest this \"Map of mathematics\" as a starting point that gives you a reasonable birds-eye view of the field. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16562340,"author":"NiklasMort","month":"2018-03","story":"Ask HN: How to self-learn math?","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of all areas of mathematics as a starting point.","quote":"I'd start with this video to get an overview of all the topics and areas that mathematics entails","text":"I'd start with this video to get an overview of all the topics and areas that mathematics entails (some might be unknown to you) [VIDEO] . Then you go ahead and research a bit what sounds interesting to you and then you might google that topic and add \"foundations\" to that google search. It's just that most school/university math is heavily focused on analysis and algebra but there is so much more!"},{"id":25923345,"author":"lamename","month":"2021-01","story":"A Map of Mathematics","stance":"reference","why":"Wide-scope map of mathematics.","quote":"The educational youtube channel Domain of Science has a pretty wide-scope Map of Mathematics","text":"The educational youtube channel Domain of Science has a pretty wide-scope Map of Mathematics, depending on your background [VIDEO]"},{"id":16773443,"author":"sigi45","month":"2018-04","story":"Berkeley offers its data science course online for free","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a knowledge-graph style map of math topics.","quote":"Something like 'The Map of Mathematics'","text":"This is a general issue i have seen as well. We live in 2018 and there is no open source course for everything. Instead there are probably 10-30k universities who have similar courses and professors who give the same lecture every year. They get paid often enough by countries to create and do those courses. In germany most of our unversities are paid by all of us germans anyway. And what do you find online? Always the starter verion like 101 computer science or videos with bad audio or video, no proper exercises, no solution helper etc. Nothing. You have to go to different sites to sometimes pay or sometimes not. there are no local locations to meet up with people. There should be a global initiative for global free and open access learning. Sponsored and supported by companies and countries. Build upon a core of a knowledge graph based on topics or 'snippets of knowledge'. Like for example: math -> add -> sub Something like 'The Map of Mathematics' ( [VIDEO] ) And when you wanna get the global accepted math 101 level, you have to take specific topics / snippets. And those snippets can than be filled with different people who are making a lecture for that topic and you can choose whom you like more or who is better in explaining it to you. What do i do instead? I ask around for the lecture scripts because they are always behind a simple password protected area or have multiple links to different pages of different universietses who offer different courses for free as videos f"},{"id":16562401,"author":"RossBencina","month":"2018-03","story":"Ask HN: How to self-learn math?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I agree with other comments that \"learn maths\" is too broad. You can take a university degree in maths and still be just at the beginning of \"learning maths.\" I recommend refining your goal somehow: perhaps to learn math related to certain applications that you're interested in, or learn math in a certain area (e.g. high-school algebra, geometry, probability, discrete math, graph theory, calculus, pure math, abstract algebra, topology, etc). If you have not mastered high-school algebra and other pre-calculus subjects, you should start there; most other maths subjects will assume that you know these things. Calculus takes up a lot of space in upper high-school and early university courses -- but if you're a developer there may be other subjects that are more immediately useful to you (e.g. discrete math, linear algebra). I set out to \"learn maths\" (that's verbatim what it says on my personal Kanban board). In the end I took some university classes. For me they provided the structure and teachers to help me learn. Also, there is a difference between having an idea about what some math-thing is, and being able to pass an 3 hour closed-book exam in that topic. I agree that Khan Academy is a good learning resource that will provide structure to your learning: [LINK] Purplemath is another good resource: [LINK] YouTube is full of videos of people running through problems on any conceivable topic. Definitely search there for help. Once you've worked your way through the high school p"}],"r":[["35Idb_lCU4o",1],["S9JGmA5_unY",1],["vBiIDwBOqQA",1],["dW8Cy6WrO94",1],["LJ4W1g-6JiY",1],["cTsEzmFamZ8",1],["IAF8DjrQSSk",1]]},"bY2FlayomlE":{"wy":"Pointed to as the in-depth talk on exception mechanics and code size for embedded C++.","q":{"x":"In particular, his 2019 presentation [2] describes different error handling techniques","i":45094428,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45094428,"author":"inetknght","month":"2025-09","story":"How is Ultrassembler so fast?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Deep dive into exception mechanics and code size","quote":"the whole presentation was excellent in my opinion","text":"> I don't have enough time to watch these kinds of presentations. Then let me pick and share some of my favorites that I found enlightening, and summarize with some information that I found useful. By far, the most useful one is Khalil Estell's presentation last year [0]. It's a fairly face paced but relatively deep dive into exception mechanics. At the end, he advocates for a new tool that would audit a program to determine what exceptions could be thrown. I think that's a flipping fantastic idea for a tool. Unfortunately I haven't seen any progress toward it -- if someone here knows where his tool is, or a similar tool, please reply! I did send him an email a few months ago inquiring about it, but haven't received a reply. Nonetheless, the whole presentation was excellent in my opinion. I did see that he had another related presentation at ACCU this year [4] with a topic of \"C++ Exceptions are Code Compression\" (which I totally can believe -- I've seen it myself in binary sizes), but I haven't seen his presentation yet. I'll watch it later today. Just about anything from Herb Sutter is good. I don't like that he works for Microsoft, but he does great stuff for C++, including the old Guru of the Week series [1]. In particular, his 2019 presentation [2] describes different error handling techniques, some difficulties and pitfalls in combining libraries with different error handling techniques, and leads up to explaining why std::expected came about. He does pontificate a lot "},{"id":47330831,"author":"daemin","month":"2026-03","story":"Practical Guide to Bare Metal C++","stance":"rec","why":"Explains bloat in exception handling for bare metal","quote":"he mentions that a lot of the bloat in exception handling is including printf","text":"I just thought I'd mention that Khalil Estell is doing some work regarding exceptions in embedded and bare metal programming. In the first of his talks about this topic ( [VIDEO] ) he mentions that a lot of the bloat in exception handling is including printf in order to print out the message when terminating the program. For those interested he has another presentation on this topic [LINK]"},{"id":45783042,"author":"neonz80","month":"2025-11","story":"Async/Await is finally back in Zig","stance":"rec","why":"Exception overhead likely less than manual error handling","quote":"There was an interesting talk about C++ exceptions in smaller firmware at CppCon last year","text":"There was an interesting talk about C++ exceptions in smaller firmware at CppCon last year: [VIDEO] Basically, the overhead of exceptions is probably less than handling the same errors manually in any non-trivial program. Also, it's not like these table doesn't exist in other languages. Both Rust and Go have to unwind."},{"id":45088545,"author":"mpyne","month":"2025-09","story":"How is Ultrassembler so fast?","stance":"rec","why":"Reducing exception code size on embedded platforms","quote":"A specific presentation I'd point to is Khalil Estell's presentation on reducing exception code size on embedded platforms","text":"A specific presentation I'd point to is Khalil Estell's presentation on reducing exception code size on embedded platforms at [VIDEO] But honestly you'd get vast majority of the benefit just by skimming through the slides at [LINK] With a couple of symbols you define yourself a lot of the associated g++ code size is sharply reduced while still allowing exceptions to work. (Slide 60 on)"},{"id":42952302,"author":"bluGill","month":"2025-02","story":"Software development topics I've changed my mind on","stance":"rec","why":"Low-level technical look at how exceptions really work","quote":"Khalil Estell has some great work on that.","text":"Khalil Estell has some great work on that. [VIDEO] is one link - very low level technical of what is really happening. He has other talks and papers if you search his name."},{"id":46184552,"author":"vodou","month":"2025-12","story":"The C++ standard for the F-35 Fighter Jet [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Listed alongside the ACCU talk on exceptions and code size","quote":"","text":"Need to check it out. I guess you mean these: - C++ Exceptions Reduce Firmware Code Size, ACCU [1] - C++ Exceptions for Smaller Firmware, CppCon [2] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45096750,"author":"inetknght","month":"2025-09","story":"How is Ultrassembler so fast?","stance":"reference","why":"Notes a later talk is a rehash of this one","quote":"","text":"Update: it looks like link [4] is just a rehash of his talk from last year's cppcon [0]. [0]: [VIDEO] [4]: [LINK]"}],"r":[["xbBdIG--b58",1],["ARYP83yNAWk",1],["ZQFzMfHIxng",1],["2EWejmkKlxs",1],["B90_SNNbcoU",1],["ASXJgvy3mEg",1],["WUvTyaaNkzM",1],["zBkNBP00wJE",1],["rvLFEh7V18A",1],["YnWhqhNdYyk",1]]},"zUyH3XhpLTo":{"wy":"Recommended for correctly predicting the features of the real black hole image and clearly explaining the lensing physics behind it.","q":{"x":"Good video that correctly predicted the image and describes why it looks the way it does","i":19624358,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":19624325,"author":"turshija","month":"2019-04","story":"Unveiling the first-ever image of a black hole [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains how the black hole image was predicted before release.","quote":"is one of my favorite videos in a long time","text":"Related video, made by Veritasium yesterday, is one of my favorite videos in a long time. He explained how the prediction of this image was made (before the image got released) and the video is great and fun to watch. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28331068,"author":"gus_massa","month":"2021-08","story":"First image of a black hole: a CNRS researcher had simulated it as early as 1979","stance":"rec","why":"Cardboard 3D simulation and clear explanation of black hole appearance.","quote":"There is a nice 3d cardboard simulation and clear explanation in a video by Veritasium","text":"If the black hole is not spinning, it should look the same from every angle. A spinning black hole is deformed (like the spinning Earth.) Anyway, we are not seeing the black hole (because it looks just like a black circle in front of a black background). The interesting part of the image is a disk of material spinning around it. There is a nice 3d cardboard simulation and clear explanation in a video by Veritasium [VIDEO]"},{"id":21112376,"author":"sudhirj","month":"2019-09","story":"Simulation of what a massive black hole would look like","stance":"rec","why":"Clear explanation of why the accretion disk looks warped.","quote":"There's a clearer explanation of why the disk looks that way and how the gravitational warp makes those light paths","text":"There's a clearer explanation of why the disk looks that way and how the gravitational warp makes those light paths [VIDEO]"},{"id":21116203,"author":"sova","month":"2019-09","story":"Simulation of what a massive black hole would look like","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the lensing effect, made before the image was released.","quote":"Veritasium made an excellent youtube video explaining how this effect works (before the image was released!)","text":"Veritasium made an excellent youtube video explaining how this effect works (before the image was released!) [VIDEO]"},{"id":20373483,"author":"kaslai","month":"2019-07","story":"A Ring of Controversy Around a Black Hole Photo","stance":"rec","why":"Understandable explanation of the physics behind the black hole image.","quote":"Derek explains the physics in a reasonably understandable manner","text":"Derek explains the physics in a reasonably understandable manner in this video on his Youtube channel: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19624358,"author":"JumpCrisscross","month":"2019-04","story":"Unveiling the first-ever image of a black hole [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Predicts the black hole image and explains why it looks that way.","quote":"Good video that correctly predicted the image and describes why it looks the way it does","text":"Good video that correctly predicted the image and describes why it looks the way it does [1]. TL; DR The dark area is the entire surface of the event horizon, including the side facing away from us, plus some more due to photons missing the event horizon \"directly\" being drawn in. One side is brighter due to its being Doppler boosted. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19625580,"author":"delecti","month":"2019-04","story":"Unveiling the first-ever image of a black hole [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Predicts the features later seen in the actual black hole image.","quote":"This video predicts pretty well all of the features we expected to find","text":"If you mean the brightness asymmetry, it's because the accretion disc is spinning. This video predicts pretty well all of the features we expected to find, and it lines up with the actual image. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19629201,"author":"tempestn","month":"2019-04","story":"Unveiling the first-ever image of a black hole [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed explanation of why light bends around a black hole.","quote":"This video explains in much more detail","text":"The black hole itself is spherical, but the light we're seeing around it isn't projected by the event horizon; rather, it's light from behind/around the black hole that isn't blocked. This video explains in much more detail: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19626914,"author":"codesternews","month":"2019-04","story":"Unveiling the first-ever image of a black hole [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how scientists predicted the black hole's appearance.","quote":"","text":"It's really amazing that human mind can predict and tell the things without even seeing it. How come we even know and predicted the radius and things far away in galaxy without even seeing it. It's just amazing. Wow it just amazes you that scientist even have predicted the radius of thing and how it work etc.[1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19630626,"author":"brandonjm","month":"2019-04","story":"Unveiling the first-ever image of a black hole [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Explains the asymmetry of the black hole image.","quote":"See Veritasium's video [1] on why it looks like it does","text":"> Then the data was compiled and rendered into an image A digital camera is just an array of tiny sensors that detect how much light hit them and a computer takes those values and renders an image from them. > you really think it's that bright? If you take a photo in a dark room with increased exposure then the resulting image is brighter than what you see with your own eyes. This was taken over a long period of time, effectively a long exposure. If you were close enough to see it at the scale you are seeing it on your computer screen it would probably be far brighter. > How about orange? As mentioned in another comment on this thread, it's just the colour scheme used in the output to show the brightness differences. The accretion disk is not necessarily orange, this photo is essentially greyscale mapped to a black -> orange -> white scale. This is just an array of relatively large sensors with very high exposure gathering information from something very far away and combining that data in greyscale. > I wonder why it's not symmetrical? See Veritasium's video [1] on why it looks like it does, in short, the effects of the black hole and our angle to the accretion disk. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["jcKRGpMiVTw",1],["s86-Z-CbaHA",1],["kRlhlCWplqk",1],["gxAaO2rsdIs",1],["R2paSGQRwvo",1],["LmlAYnFF_s8",1],["ML4tw_UzqZE",1],["MQyxG4vTyZ8",1],["KkpqA8yG9T4",1]]},"AGCmPLWZKd8":{"wy":"HN brings it up in any thread about German or European rail as a data-driven, entertaining analysis of why trains are late.","q":{"x":"There was this really great talk on the Chaos Communication Congress some time back","i":29192078,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":36079768,"author":"btschaegg","month":"2023-05","story":"It will take years to get Deutsche Bahn back on track","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great talk analyzing Deutsche Bahn punctuality, English translation","quote":"Obligatory mention of a great talk on the topic","text":"Obligatory mention of a great talk on the topic: \"David Kriesel: BahnMining -- Pünktlichkeit ist eine Zier\" (2019) English translation: [VIDEO] Edit: Note: And that was still with better numbers than lmm posted."},{"id":29192078,"author":"sdoering","month":"2021-11","story":"How trains could replace planes in Europe","stance":"strong_rec","why":"English-translated talk on German train punctuality","quote":"There was this really great talk on the Chaos Communication Congress some time back","text":"There was this really great talk on the Chaos Communication Congress some time back on the punctuality of German trains. I searched for it and even found the English voice over translation [1]. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25091871,"author":"DiogenesKynikos","month":"2020-11","story":"Virgin Hyperloop Has Invented the World's Crappiest High-Speed Rail","stance":"rec","why":"Analysis of why German trains are delayed","quote":"The was a CCC talk about why the Deutsche Bahn has so many delays","text":"The was a CCC talk about why the Deutsche Bahn has so many delays: [1]. It turns out that East Germany has great on-time performance, probably because all the infrastructure is new. But overall, Germany has to invest more in upkeep of its existing infrastructure. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35029664,"author":"dav_Oz","month":"2023-03","story":"One rail ticket for all cities in Germany – 49 euro per month","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Don't expect German \"Pünktlichkeit\" on German railways, though.[0]","text":"Don't expect German \"Pünktlichkeit\" on German railways, though.[0] The Austrian and especially the Swiss railroad systems are far superior. Since the \"Bahnreform\" of 1994 the infrastructure progressively deteriorated from the passenger's perspective. One hard fact: about 20% of the railroad network has been dismantled since 1994 (44.600km to 33.400km [1]). Way too much. Reasons for this are a confluence of excessive bureaucracy, brutal management style, powerful industry interests (think e.g. \"Autobahn\"), lobbying. Today's \"Deutsche Bahn\" as an entity is split into eight distinct divisions [2]; DB Schenker (global logistics and freight transport company) is by far the most profitable of the bunch and single-handedly lifts the whole DB group out of the red on the regular basis, it accounts for about 50% of the total revenue.[3] [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":32221879,"author":"DiogenesKynikos","month":"2022-07","story":"Deutsche Bahn’s Meltdown and High-Speed Rail","stance":"reference","why":"Scraped Deutsche Bahn API data revealing how lateness is counted","quote":"David Kriesel presented an analysis of the on-time performance of the Deutsche Bahn","text":"At Chaos Computer Club a few years back, David Kriesel presented an analysis of the on-time performance of the Deutsche Bahn, based on a systematic scraping of their API.[0] One of the surprising things he explained: if a train never arrives at a station, it's not late, according to the Deutsche Bahn. 0. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31809644,"author":"netsharc","month":"2022-06","story":"The integrated timetable of Switzerland","stance":"reference","why":"English-translated presentation on German rail","quote":"translated presentation from German","text":"German Rail did that: translated presentation from German: [VIDEO] (with a translator that sadly breathes into the mic...)"}],"r":[["2wZ1pCpJUIM",1],["RmIgJ64z6Y4",1],["9WcHsT97suU",1],["eyll-bBZzyk",1],["pL3Yzjk5R4M",1],["7FeqF1-Z1g0",1],["ezVk1ahRF78",1]]},"Ecy1FBdCRbQ":{"wy":"HN points to it in lock-security threads as a good example of engineering meeting real-world exploitation, where flaws unrelated to the headline mechanism get found.","q":{"x":"The interaction of engineering and \"use\" by the Lock Picking Lawyer","i":31880787,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31880787,"author":"harrisi","month":"2022-06","story":"Enclave: An Unpickable Lock","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Engineering versus real-world exploitation of a lock design","quote":"The interaction of engineering and \"use\" by the Lock Picking Lawyer","text":"The interaction of engineering and \"use\" by the Lock Picking Lawyer ( [VIDEO] ) was fascinating - \"use\" here really meaning \"exploiting\". It's a problem many here are aware of, either by over-engineering things intended for use by non-engineers, or designing things to be used by customers when the designer isn't intimately familiar with the use. In this case it was sort of somewhere in between. I'm currently an operator of heavy machinery in a factory making tools for the wafer industry, although my main career is in software development. I'm actively working with the tools and software used to get a better understanding of the disconnect between engineering and operations. It's been a great way to consider how to improve tools in ways that aren't just \"better\" from a software/engineering standpoint. Also, holy cow. I've watched all of your videos multiple times. You are truly an inspiration. Thank you, and apologies for the fanboying."},{"id":31168122,"author":"yccs27","month":"2022-04","story":"High-Security Mechanical Locks (2020)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"LPL tries to pick and bypass novel anti-picking mechanisms","quote":"Together they give a whole new understanding of lock design and picking methods.","text":"On the topic of \"novel, non-pin-tumbler mechanisms\": I can recommend the videos by Stuff Made Here [1][2], where he designs two novel anti-picking lock mechanisms, and the related video [3] where LockPickingLawyer tries to pick and bypass them. Together they give a whole new understanding of lock design and picking methods. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31880302,"author":"binarymax","month":"2022-06","story":"Enclave: An Unpickable Lock","stance":"rec","why":"Related prior art with hardening suggestions from LPL","quote":"an excellent set of videos","text":"This looks very similar to the design[0] by Stuff Made Here (and collaborative hardening [1] suggestions by Lock Picking Lawyer)…an excellent set of videos. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31883696,"author":"mizzao","month":"2022-06","story":"Enclave: An Unpickable Lock","stance":"rec","why":"LPL exploited a flaw unrelated to the unpickable mechanism","quote":"He picked a lock with the same concept by swighton (Stuff Made Here), but exploited a flaw that had nothing to do with the mechanism.","text":"For others who might not have seen it, here's what the Lockpicking Lawyer had to say about that. He picked a lock with the same concept by swighton (Stuff Made Here), but exploited a flaw that had nothing to do with the mechanism. [VIDEO] I think one problem here is that the more complicated you make a locking mechanism, the more you suffer by increasing the attack surface with other potential flaws or just the lock being physically weaker (i.e. smashable). Kinda like how the most advanced cryptography is usually broken because someone made an error in the complexity of implementing it."},{"id":28400776,"author":"infogulch","month":"2021-09","story":"O.mg Cable","stance":"rec","why":"LPL tests unpickable lock designs and finds easy bypasses","quote":"Made for interesting videos on both sides","text":"Since this has generated some discussion on locks and picking, there's been some interesting developments on \"unpickable locks\" that sidestep the tolerance problem by decoupling setting the pins from testing them. I.e. pins are tested all at once after they are physically decoupled from the key & keyway, eliminating state space reduction attacks (aka picking one pin at a time) leaving only brute force. One such effort features locks made by Stuff Made Here sent to Lock Picking Lawyer. According to LPL the locks are theoretically sound and he did not attempt to pick them, but these particular implementations had a couple (easily fixable) bypasses. Made for interesting videos on both sides: Stuff Made Here describes the design in detail: TWO Unpickable (?) Locks for Lock Picking Lawyer! - [LINK] Lock Picking Lawyer reviews them and performs some bypasses: [1299] Unpickable Locks From Stuff Made Here - [VIDEO]"},{"id":31880307,"author":"mgdlbp","month":"2022-06","story":"Enclave: An Unpickable Lock","stance":"reference","why":"LPL was given unpickable prototypes a year earlier","quote":"LPL was actually challenged a year ago with unpickable prototypes made by an engineering YouTuber","text":"LPL was actually challenged a year ago with unpickable prototypes made by an engineering YouTuber, Stuff Made Here: [VIDEO] (see also description)"},{"id":29367539,"author":"ryzvonusef","month":"2021-11","story":"Saintcon: Lock Picking Lawyer Keynote","stance":"reference","why":"LPL's response to Stuff Made Here's unpickable lock","quote":"here is some to-and fro between two youtube channels about such locks","text":"are you perhaps talking about the forever lock? [LINK] while difficult, it can be undone ----- making a one-off \"unpickable\" lock is possible, here is some to-and fro between two youtube channels about such locks: Stuff Made Here: [LINK] [LINK] Lock Picking Lawyer: [VIDEO] But things from installation issues, to making sure tolerances are maintained while making the locks on a production line, mean that there are always some gaps left in a mass produced and installed lock."},{"id":31882381,"author":"femto","month":"2022-06","story":"Enclave: An Unpickable Lock","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"A convenient case of having a lawyer when you need one!","text":"The power point says \"Public Use or On Sale\" counts. That could be interesting, given that the lock was given to a member of the public, the Lock Picking Lawyer, for a public picking. A convenient case of having a lawyer when you need one! [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["2A2NY29iQdI",3],["5MxH1sfJLBQ",1],["j5v8D-alAKE",1],["Dp-mQ3HxgDE",1],["p8u_k2LIZyo",1],["htYh-Tq7ZBI",1],["Y9FZ4igNxNA",1],["pgoetgxecw8",1],["ne99laPUxN4",1],["Ll6-eGDpimU",1],["_rBO8neWw04",1],["_7vPNcnYWQ4",1],["PmlRbfSavbI",1]]},"h2I8AoB1xgU":{"wy":"Recommended as a gripping look at how hard Mars entry, descent and landing is.","q":{"x":"Here's a video that blows my mind every time I watch it.","i":23562863,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23562863,"author":"perryizgr8","month":"2020-06","story":"NASA rushing to complete Mars launch before planet moves out of range","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Complex autonomous landing sequence that had to work first time","quote":"Here's a video that blows my mind every time I watch it.","text":"> Oh, it has to land like the Falcon 9 as well, except hovering and lowering itself on a tether, like the Air Cav for Mars. Here's a video that blows my mind every time I watch it. [VIDEO] Such a complicated landing sequence, and needs to work correctly the first time, all autonomously. And it worked!"},{"id":15093927,"author":"niftich","month":"2017-08","story":"Water Found Deep Inside the Moon","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Dramatic coverage of Curiosity's landing sequence","quote":"I highly recommend 'Curiosity's Seven Minutes of Terror'","text":"For a dramatic coverage of this exact subject, I highly recommend 'Curiosity's Seven Minutes of Terror' [1] from NASA JPL. [1] [LINK] or on Youtube: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25706351,"author":"rossdavidh","month":"2021-01","story":"NASA's Curiosity rover: 3k days on Mars","stance":"rec","why":"NASA engineers discuss the landing before it happened","quote":"Somewhat over-the-top but still cool discussion of this by actual NASA tech folks","text":"Somewhat over-the-top but still cool discussion of this by actual NASA tech folks, made before the landing actually happened: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18536914,"author":"sathackr","month":"2018-11","story":"How to Land on Mars","stance":"reference","why":"Curiosity landing as the coolest Mars landing","quote":"Curiousity was by far the 'coolest' and still astonishes me that we pulled it off.","text":"1. About 6 months [LINK] 2. Out of ~50 missions, about 20 have failed. [LINK] 3. About 8 successful landings [LINK] Curiousity was by far the 'coolest' and still astonishes me that we pulled it off. [VIDEO] 4. No."}],"r":[["08mwXICY4JM",1]]},"rRi8LptvFZY":{"wy":"HN cites it as the go-to example of a company defending its trademark, and many also find it funny.","q":{"x":"it's hilarious and worth watching","i":38809093,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38809093,"author":"compiler-guy","month":"2023-12","story":"Don't Say Velcro (2017)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hilarious ad campaign that made the trademark stick.","quote":"it's hilarious and worth watching","text":"If you've never seen the video ad that went along with this campaign, it's hilarious and worth watching, as is the follow up. I love the good humor about the whole thing. And it has made their campaign stick in ways that other similar campaigns have not. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":32673219,"author":"Stratoscope","month":"2022-09","story":"YKK zippers: Why so many designers use them (2012)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hilarious Velcro ad best watched with sound up.","quote":"It's the funniest thing I've seen in a long time.","text":"That is a very cute page. I like their attitude. And the video at the top of the page is absolutely wonderful. It's the funniest thing I've seen in a long time. Watch it with good headphones or speakers and the sound up. Direct link: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22764947,"author":"_jal","month":"2020-04","story":"'Call of Duty' wins First Amendment victory over use of Humvees","stance":"rec","why":"Velcro's lawyers amusingly explain proper trademark usage.","quote":"in a surprisingly straightforward, honest and amusing way","text":"Here is Velcro's lawyers telling you to call those other brands 'hook and loop' in a surprisingly straightforward, honest and amusing way. [VIDEO]"},{"id":42630054,"author":"cosmotic","month":"2025-01","story":"Oracle Will Not Voluntarily Withdraw JavaScript Trademark","stance":"reference","why":"Explains trademark defense from Velcro's perspective","quote":"This video explains it from Velcro's perspective","text":"JavaScript can refer to many different similar languages, the runtimes, the standard library, etc. People using JavaScript without getting permission are potentially infringing on Oracle's trademark. Many companies with trademarks tenaciously defend the trademark to protect it from being revoked. This doesn't appear to be the case with JavaScript. After usage becomes widespread, a company risks losing their trademark because they did not actively enforce the use of the trademark. This video explains it from Velcro's perspective [VIDEO]"},{"id":42689351,"author":"andiareso","month":"2025-01","story":"Live London Underground / bus maps taken down by TfL trademark complaint","stance":"reference","why":"Velcro lawyers defending the brand against genericization","quote":"the lawyers of the popular \"hook and loop fasteners\" Velcro constantly try and defend their IP so it doesn't become generic.","text":"This is the thing. Most people don't know that in order to keep your brand, you have to continually use it and defend it. In a similar vein, the lawyers of the popular \"hook and loop fasteners\" Velcro constantly try and defend their IP so it doesn't become generic. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40370285,"author":"ian-g","month":"2024-05","story":"Adobe Photoshop Source Code (2013)","stance":"reference","why":"Velcro campaign made commenter aware it's a trademark.","quote":"I'm very aware now that Velcro is a trademarked name.","text":"Much more effectively, Velcro's been trying the same thing: [VIDEO] It still won't work in the long run, but I'm very aware now that Velcro is a trademarked name."},{"id":39346375,"author":"SushiHippie","month":"2024-02","story":"You Can Call the Super Bowl the \"Super Bowl\"","stance":"reference","why":"Velcro ad telling people not to genericize the name.","quote":"","text":"Is this something like velcro? Where they created an ad to tell people to stop using the name velcro because they'll lose the trademark otherwise? [VIDEO]"},{"id":39346826,"author":"SushiHippie","month":"2024-02","story":"List of Generic and Genericized Trademarks","stance":"reference","why":"Velcro ad as example of defending a trademark.","quote":"I remembered the Velcro advertisement [1]","text":"After reading the post about Super Bowl [0] I remembered the Velcro advertisement [1] and wondered what else are genericized trademarks. I think everyone is aware that Velcro, Kleenex, post-it are trademarks, even though they are used for off-brand products colloquially. In Germany people also use Tempo, Tesa, Edding and so on, but most are aware that these are brand names. But I was surprised to find out that names like escalator, cellophane, flip phone, even dry ice, trampoline, dumpster were once brand names! [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] Past submissions: List of Generic Trademarks - [LINK] - Nov 2021 (0 comments) List of Generic and Genericized Trademarks - [LINK] - Feb 2021 (3 comments) List of generic and genericized trademarks - [LINK] - Sep 2017 (0 comments) Brand Names Which Become Generic Names [Wikipedia] - [LINK] - Jan 2014 (0 comments)"},{"id":21285880,"author":"frosted-flakes","month":"2019-10","story":"LegoOS: A Disseminated, Distributed Operating System","stance":"reference","why":"Velcro's ad asking people to say hook and loop.","quote":"It's not unreasonable for them to try to protect their trademark.","text":"They don't want their trademark turning into a noun (or a verb), just like Velcro doesn't want their trademark to be used to refer to \"hook and loop\" [1]. It's not unreasonable for them to try to protect their trademark. 1 - [VIDEO]"},{"id":45472829,"author":"Naracion","month":"2025-10","story":"OpenAI's H1 2025: $4.3B in income, $13.5B in loss","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"[VIDEO] Video description, from the Velcro brand YouTube channel: Our Velcro Brand Companies legal team decided to clear a few things up about using the VELCRO® trademark correctly – because they’re lawyers and that’s what they do. When you use “velcro” as a noun or a verb (e.g., velcro shoes), you diminish the importance of our brand and our lawyers lose their insert fastening sound. So please, do not say “velcro shoes” (or “velcro wallet” or “velcro gloves”) - we repeat “velcro” is not a noun or a verb. VELCRO® is our brand. #dontsayvelcro"},{"id":41557980,"author":"OptionOfT","month":"2024-09","story":"Oracle, it's time to free JavaScript","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"VELCRO(r) has this song about it","text":"Does Oracle actually defend it? Isn't the rule behind a trademark that you actually need to enforce it? VELCRO(r) has this song about it: [VIDEO] Edit: as I watched the video again I finally caught on the fact that RollerBlades are a brand of inline-skates. In Belgium we called them inline-skates. It's interesting to see how brands-as-identifiers change based on language and countries. My Walloon friend calls tape Scotch, but I don't."}],"r":[["ZLWMQLMiTPk",2],["TLm7Q92xMjQ",1],["ywWBy6J5gz8",1],["nYg6jzotiAc",1],["YNTARSM-Fjc",1],["kAG39jKi0lI",1],["8S0FDjFBj8o",1],["GJVSDjRXVoo",1],["7TqqdOcX4dc",1],["sTu3LwpF6XI",1]]},"vu-h5y6tK34":{"wy":"Linked by the author and others in LibrePCB threads as the best explanation of its design rationale.","q":{"x":"Here's a video from FOSDEM where Urban (the main author) explains why he started LibrePCB","i":23061954,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23061954,"author":"dbrgn","month":"2020-05","story":"LibrePCB 0.1.4 Released","stance":"rec","why":"Author explains motivation and library concepts","quote":"Here's a video from FOSDEM where Urban (the main author) explains why he started LibrePCB","text":"Here's a video from FOSDEM where Urban (the main author) explains why he started LibrePCB and how the library concepts are different from other similar tools like KiCAD: [VIDEO] (Slides: [LINK] ) Also, previous HN threads: [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":21676434,"author":"dbrgn","month":"2019-12","story":"LibrePCB 0.1.3 released, now with DRC","stance":"rec","why":"Why LibrePCB was started and how libraries differ from KiCAD","quote":"Talk by Urban that explains why he started LibrePCB, and how the library concepts are different from other similar tools like KiCAD","text":"Before people start asking: Comparison with KiCAD and Eagle: [LINK] Talk by Urban that explains why he started LibrePCB, and how the library concepts are different from other similar tools like KiCAD: [VIDEO] (Slides: [LINK] ) If you want to try it: [LINK] Previous HN discussion: [LINK]"},{"id":18573629,"author":"dbrgn","month":"2018-11","story":"LibrePCB: First stable release published","stance":"rec","why":"Explains core design concepts and rationale of LibrePCB","quote":"It explains some of the core design concepts and the rationale for them.","text":"There's also an \"Intro to LibrePCB\" talk recording from FOSDEM this year: [VIDEO] It explains some of the core design concepts and the rationale for them. In case you don't have time to watch the video, here are the slides: [LINK]"},{"id":18305144,"author":"dbrgn","month":"2018-10","story":"LibrePCB: First release candidate","stance":"rec","why":"Explains LibrePCB's new library management concepts","quote":"You should take a look at my talk at FOSDEM","text":"U. Bruhin (the primary author of LibrePCB) answered this question on Reddit ( [LINK] ): ---Quote:--- Hi! LibrePCB has some very new concepts, especially for library management. You should take a look at my talk at FOSDEM: [VIDEO] (Slides: [LINK] ) As these are extremely different to KiCad's concepts, it would be very difficult to contribute that to KiCad because they need to provide backward compatibility. And KiCad already has a lot of legacy code, so it would become even worse when changing very fundamental concepts. It's very questionable if KiCad developers even would accept such fundamental changes - actually I'm pretty sure that they wouldn't agree with some of LibrePCB's concepts. So the only way was to create a completely new project. And LibrePCB 0.1.0 is only the beginning - I have many more ideas how an EDA tool should look like. With a standalone project I'm much more flexible to implement new features than if every feature would need to be contributed to KiCad."},{"id":26623530,"author":"raphlinus","month":"2021-03","story":"Thoughts on Rust bloat (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Example of text-based format designed for version control","quote":"LibrePCB (see ) who devised their own text based format specifically to make version control more friendly","text":"It's something we thought about, but I think it's somewhat orthogonal to the question of serialization - you still need code to go back and forth between the representation in the database and the native data structures. See [LINK] for a deeper discussion of issues around a file format for a font editor. One of the biggest current pain points is dealing with merges in version control (usually git). I think there are ways of dealing with that in an sqlite-based file format, but I worry it would require some custom tooling, especially to make sure invariants aren't violated. Another interesting example is LibrePCB (see [VIDEO] ) who devised their own text based format specifically to make version control more friendly."},{"id":23062655,"author":"singron","month":"2020-05","story":"Horizon – a free and open source electronic PCB design package","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why an alternative to existing tools was wanted","quote":"this LibrePCB talk explains why they wanted an alternative","text":"I haven't used any of these before, but this LibrePCB talk explains why they wanted an alternative: [VIDEO] Horizon docs have this explanation: [LINK]"}],"r":[["AxCo2bE9Gtk",1]]},"z_hm5oX7ZlE":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a technically jaw-dropping talk, and it is the standard reference whenever the Night Watch gigapixel images come up.","q":{"x":"This was the first talk I immediately felt the urge to share with multiple co-workers & friends.","i":29654848,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29551163,"author":"rocmcd","month":"2021-12","story":"Rijksmuseum to Stage the Largest Vermeer Exhibition in 2023","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Incredible imaging work on Rembrandt's Night Watch.","quote":"Incredible work being done there.","text":"I can’t hear about the Rijksmuseum without thinking of the presentation Rob Erdmann did at Pycon on how they imaged Rembrandt’s Night Watch: [VIDEO] Incredible work being done there. I’m glad to see they’re continuing to make headlines."},{"id":29654848,"author":"oqb23","month":"2021-12","story":"Ask HN: Best Talks of 2021?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Technically jaw-dropping talk on Python for heritage imaging.","quote":"This was the first talk I immediately felt the urge to share with multiple co-workers & friends.","text":"Robert Erdmann's keynote at PyCon: [VIDEO] The talk centers around Erdmann's use of python in \"combining materials science, computer science, and imaging science to help the world access, preserve, and understand its cultural heritage\". The technical complexity and nuance that went into the project was truly jaw-dropping. This was the first talk I immediately felt the urge to share with multiple co-workers & friends."},{"id":27617153,"author":"stefanpie","month":"2021-06","story":"The Night Watch: The Missing Pieces","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fascinating technical details of digitizing the Night Watch with Python.","quote":"Fascinating talk with tones of technical details.","text":"Robert Erdmann also gave a keynote talk at PyCon US 2021 about how they used Python for most of digitization and processing of the Night Watch (aka Operation Night Watch). Fascinating talk with tones of technical details. Link: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41609196,"author":"cyberlimerence","month":"2024-09","story":"Ultra high-resolution image of The Night Watch (2022)","stance":"rec","why":"Technical aspects of the Night Watch imaging.","quote":"For anyone interested in technical aspects of this, I recommend watching Pycon talk","text":"For anyone interested in technical aspects of this, I recommend watching Pycon talk [1] from Robert Erdmann. I bookmarked this couple of years ago. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29779534,"author":"erdmann","month":"2022-01","story":"Ultra High Resolution Photo of Night Watch (2022)","stance":"reference","why":"Author's own keynote on how the image was made.","quote":"I gave a PyCon keynote [1] last year about how this was done.","text":"Glad to see this getting exposure on HN. I gave a PyCon keynote [1] last year about how this was done. I've also put the image online in my own viewer [2], which has a few additional features: it encodes the viewing location in the URL and it allows for multi-pane synchronized views [3]. (Please be nice to my server.) I'm happy to answer questions here or on Twitter (@erdmann). [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":30066688,"author":"easytiger","month":"2022-01","story":"717 Gigapixel Image of Rembrandt's Nightwatch","stance":"reference","why":"Seminar by the author on how it was done.","quote":"It's a £43k camera on a huge automated gantry system controlled by python software","text":"Seminar on how it was done here by the author [LINK] [VIDEO] 97 rows × 87 cols (8 439) @Hasselblad H6D-400c MS images Size: 925 000 px × 775 000 px (717 GP) Res: 5 µm (5080 ppi) It's a £43k camera on a huge automated gantry system controlled by python software"},{"id":30076593,"author":"zerd","month":"2022-01","story":"717 Gigapixel Image of Rembrandt's Nightwatch","stance":"reference","why":"Mentions each 100mp image is around 600MB.","quote":"In this video [1] he mentions that each 100mp image is around 600MB","text":"In this video [1] he mentions that each 100mp image is around 600MB, which aligns with the size of the 4-shot multi-shot mode of the H6D-400C MS. 8439 photos 579MB would be around 4.7TB in raw format. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"}]},"ZsRRCnque2E":{"wy":"HN people treat it as the canonical C64 talk and rewatch Steil's 'Ultimate' series regularly.","q":{"x":"His “Ultimate Guide” talks are great and I rewatch them at least once a year.","i":43772743,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":43772743,"author":"msarnoff","month":"2025-04","story":"How MOS 6502 Illegal Opcodes Work (2008)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great talk series rewatched yearly.","quote":"His “Ultimate Guide” talks are great and I rewatch them at least once a year.","text":"Michael’s blog is one of my favorites. His “Ultimate Guide” talks are great and I rewatch them at least once a year. Ultimate Guide to the C64: [VIDEO] Ultimate Guide to the Game Boy: [LINK] Ultimate Guide to the Apollo Guidance Computer: [LINK]"},{"id":28554745,"author":"anyfoo","month":"2021-09","story":"The Ultimate Commodore 1541 Disk Drive Talk [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Low-resolution recording still enjoyable.","quote":"was very sufficient for me to watch and enjoy the talk back in the day.","text":"Not exactly what you're asking for, but [VIDEO] while being 480p and rather noisy, was very sufficient for me to watch and enjoy the talk back in the day. The presented material does not suffer much on the relatively low resolution."},{"id":2771411,"author":"psykotic","month":"2011-07","story":"The story of FCopy for the C-64","stance":"rec","why":"Michael Steil's talk on the Commodore 64.","quote":"Obligatory link to Michael Steil's The Ultimate Commodore 64 Talk","text":"> Back in the 80s, the Commodore C-64 had an intelligent floppy drive, the 1541, i.e. an external unit that had its own CPU and everything. I remember by the time I got a floppy drive for my C64 it was twice the price of a new C64, $400 vs $200. It was a real luxury item in those days. Obligatory link to Michael Steil's The Ultimate Commodore 64 Talk: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["HyzD8pNlpwI",2],["FyKRubB5N60",1],["5TFDG-y-EHs",1],["ar9WRwCiSr0",1],["yHXx3orN35Y",1],["GtQdIYUtAHg",1],["xx7Lfh5SKUQ",1],["2SdGkkp1aq8",1],["R35UuntQQF8",1]]},"hs3piaN4b5I":{"wy":"Recommended as a short, clear rundown of one-dimensional versus two-dimensional layout.","q":{"x":"Here's a great explanation from Jen Simmons that really helped me understand the differences","i":17619608,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17619608,"author":"ljoshua","month":"2018-07","story":"Show HN: Layoutit – An interactive CSS Grid generator","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Jen Simmons explaining differences and strengths of flexbox and grid.","quote":"Here's a great explanation from Jen Simmons that really helped me understand the differences","text":"Here's a great explanation from Jen Simmons that really helped me understand the differences and respective strengths and weaknesses of each: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22825278,"author":"JamesSwift","month":"2020-04","story":"Ask HN: Resources to learn basic UI layout design for React apps?","stance":"rec","why":"Good rundown of what flexbox and grid do and do not do.","quote":"This[1] is a good rundown of what the two do/dont do.","text":"Grid is awesome, and I highly recommend learning what it can do for you. That said, they serve different purposes. However, between the two of them they address 97% of my needs on the frontend. This[1] is a good rundown of what the two do/dont do. [1] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":22825294,"author":"JamesSwift","month":"2020-04","story":"Ask HN: Resources to learn basic UI layout design for React apps?","stance":"rec","why":"Rundown of overlap and unique strengths of flexbox and grid.","quote":"See this for a good rundown","text":"The two occupy a venn diagram of functionality. They do some stuff the same, but they also both do unique things the other doesnt. See this for a good rundown: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17159933,"author":"Deimorz","month":"2018-05","story":"Best Practices with CSS Grid Layout","stance":"rec","why":"Shows the difference between one-dimensional flexbox and two-dimensional grid.","quote":"all the Layout Land videos are great if you want to look more into CSS grid","text":"They serve different purposes, and you'll often want to use both for different pieces of the layout. Flexbox is for laying things out in one dimension, Grid is more for two-dimensional ones. This video might help show the difference (and all the Layout Land videos are great if you want to look more into CSS grid): [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["kZRE7HIO3vk",1],["p_iZr2-Coqc",1]]},"ZLWMQLMiTPk":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a delightful sequel to the original ad, brought up in threads on trademarks and the original campaign.","q":{"x":"it's hilarious and worth watching","i":38809093,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":42631197,"author":"Stratoscope","month":"2025-01","story":"Oracle Will Not Voluntarily Withdraw JavaScript Trademark","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Delightful Velcro follow-up video, slightly more NSFW than the original.","quote":"They also made a followup video that is just as delightful","text":"That is one of my favorite videos! Content advisory: there is one swear word in it that is bleeped out. Near the end they mention some other trademarks, and adding to the fun they bleep out those too - and even put CENSORED overlays on the singers' mouths. They also made a followup video that is just as delightful (and slightly more NSFW): [VIDEO] And here is a fun article about some other companies' friendly alternatives to the traditional C&D letter: [LINK]"},{"id":38809093,"author":"compiler-guy","month":"2023-12","story":"Don't Say Velcro (2017)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hilarious Velcro ad campaign video and its follow-up.","quote":"hilarious and worth watching, as is the follow up","text":"If you've never seen the video ad that went along with this campaign, it's hilarious and worth watching, as is the follow up. I love the good humor about the whole thing. And it has made their campaign stick in ways that other similar campaigns have not. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38806075,"author":"FrustratedMonky","month":"2023-12","story":"Don't Say Velcro (2017)","stance":"rec","why":"Velcro's follow-up video to its lawyer-sung ad.","quote":"There was a follow up video, also good","text":"Great. Hope this is real. I'd be impressed if the real lawyers did this. There was a follow up video, also good [VIDEO]"},{"id":34576135,"author":"Semaphor","month":"2023-01","story":"Spaghetti-Tree Hoax","stance":"reference","why":"Velcro's feedback response video poking fun at German pronunciation.","quote":"(of course they are making fun of German, Klettverschluss is so easy to say!)","text":"Thank you, this was great. There’s also a feedback response video [0] (of course they are making fun of German, Klettverschluss is so easy to say!), and a behind-the-scene video [1] where they show you their two actual lawyers that somehow were roped into participating ;) [0]: [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK]"}],"r":[["rRi8LptvFZY",2],["TLm7Q92xMjQ",1],["ywWBy6J5gz8",1],["nYg6jzotiAc",1],["YNTARSM-Fjc",1],["kAG39jKi0lI",1],["8S0FDjFBj8o",1],["GJVSDjRXVoo",1],["7TqqdOcX4dc",1],["rUg_ukBwsyo",1],["O-2tpwW0kmU",1],["sTu3LwpF6XI",1]]},"bfN2JWifLCY":{"wy":"Commenters point to it for the Swedish insider perspective on lockdowns, with some later calling its statements prescient.","q":{"x":"Here's an interesting interview with a senior epidemiologist partly responsible for Sweden's unconventional strategy","i":22912393,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":28269454,"author":"jansan","month":"2021-08","story":"The Sweden experiment: how no lockdowns led to mental health, healthier economy","stance":"rec","why":"April 2020 interview that proved prescient in hindsight","quote":"In hindsight one can call his statements prophetic.","text":"Here is an interview with Prof Johan Giesecke from April 2020. In hindsight one can call his statements prophetic. [VIDEO] They had a clear plan, unlike other governments like Germany, who still do not have a plan how to get out of this mess."},{"id":23131564,"author":"dcanelhas","month":"2020-05","story":"Getting Back to Work","stance":"rec","why":"Insight into Sweden's policy reasoning","quote":"A good bit of insight here","text":"Sweden has a rather different welfare system, typical population density, income distribution, typical household demographic, urban planning and culture regarding social interactions, compared to many places that would use it as an example for what to do in this crisis. I think following the available evidence, interpreted by experts is the way to go. Ultimately the argument for Sweden's policy seems to be less based on cost saving than general wellbeing. A good bit of insight here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23202823,"author":"benjaminwootton","month":"2020-05","story":"How Apocalyptic Is Now?","stance":"rec","why":"Unherd interviews with epidemiologists praised as top crisis journalism","quote":"Unherd.com have had some of the best journalism of the crisis so far.","text":"Unherd.com have had some of the best journalism of the crisis so far. In particular some of the interviews with epedimiologists: [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":23311245,"author":"K0nserv","month":"2020-05","story":"Stockholm Won't Reach Herd Immunity in May, Sweden's Chief Epidemiologist Says","stance":"rec","why":"Interview with former Swedish state epidemiologist","quote":"","text":"I'm Swedish but I live in Scotland and I've been watching the Swedish approach to COVID with great interest. I remain fairly sceptical but I also thought this interview[0] with Johan Giesecke who was the previous state epidemiologist and is advising both WHO and the current state epidemiologist interesting. In particular it seems like Sweden messed up the response in elderly care which is something that everyone is admiting to. I do think it's too early to say whether Sweden's approach is as bad as it currently looks, likely this is not something that can be measured and compared to other nations for years. 0: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22912393,"author":"9nGQluzmnq3M","month":"2020-04","story":"Rt: Effective Reproduction Number of Covid-19","stance":"rec","why":"Interview with Swedish epidemiologist on lockdowns and protecting the vulnerable","quote":"Here's an interesting interview with a senior epidemiologist partly responsible for Sweden's unconventional strategy","text":"Here's an interesting interview with a senior epidemiologist partly responsible for Sweden's unconventional strategy: [VIDEO] TL;DW: Preventing transmission in the long term is impossible and the actual fatality rate is likely on the order of 0.1%, so general lockdowns are pointless and being driven mostly by political considerations (something must be done and this is something). Just protect the elderly and weak."},{"id":22945628,"author":"salimmadjd","month":"2020-04","story":"The epidemiologist behind Sweden’s controversial coronavirus strategy","stance":"rec","why":"Longer interview giving Sweden's perspective on lockdowns","quote":"worthwhile listening to their perspective","text":"There is a lengthier interview by UnHeard with Professor Johan Giesecke (another experienced epidemiologist) of Sweden on YouTube [0]. It’s worthwhile listening to their perspective. One of the comments he made was that no lockdown would work in democratic countries and the real test will be fatality rates between Sweden and others in 1 year. He believed it’ll be very little difference between those countries that have implement the lockdown and those that haven’t I had watched the simulation of covid19 infection by 1blue3brown[1] and some of his modeling appeared to me (maybe I misunderstood them) to confirm Swede’s point of view. Ultimately the same number of people got infected albeit in a “flatter” rate. However one counter point by Johan Giesecke was that these flattening the curve is motivated by the assumption of a static capacity of ER rooms to deal with this pandemic and he pointed out they have increased capacity. Overall I found listening to his side very informative, at least it gave me a different view of looking at from their view. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":22988953,"author":"rishirishi","month":"2020-04","story":"Seattle’s leaders let scientists take the lead, New York’s did not","stance":"rec","why":"Epidemiologist's perspective arguing lockdowns were a mistake","quote":"You may find this epidemiologist's perspective to be interesting","text":"As I see it, lockdowns are not effective and were a massive mistake. You may find this epidemiologist's perspective to be interesting: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23414861,"author":"cinntaile","month":"2020-06","story":"Anders Tegnell defends Sweden's virus approach","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Giesecke's herd immunity claims","quote":"","text":"While it is correct that they have never explicitly mentioned herd immunity, it is at least somewhat implied. Quite a few claims were made with regards to what percentage of the population in Stockholm have already had the disease, at one point Johan Giesecke even claimed 50% when mass serology testing becomes available.[0] Although we haven't really heard from him much lately. A study by the Swedish health organization was retracted recently as well, they were too optimistic about their immunity claims and it was based on a flawed study.[1] They have since put out a correct version. The most recent numbers say Stockholm is at about 7,3%.[2] This article is actually a pretty good representation of how they have been communicating. Something similar to flock immunity is implied while not actually being flock immunity. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":23148653,"author":"salimmadjd","month":"2020-05","story":"Elon Musk reopening Tesla factory despite Alameda County order","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as a counterargument to lockdowns","quote":"","text":"I have to side with Musk here. I want to see what everything Tesla is doing to protect their employees and if they are following a subset of the below guidelines that I've listed. Temperature check at entrance, mask requirement, strict hand sanitization, etc. Then let Musk commercialize the process of opening up businesses. Some background: I followed Corona (COVID19 now) from late January very closely and read most published papers. I strongly supported the \"flattening of the curve\" and encouraged the lockdown and we practiced/and practice strict social distancing. That being said, hearing countering arguments from various scientist (2013 Noble prize winner [0]). The Swedish epidemiologist [1]. That the lockdown makes no sense unless we are going to have a vaccine when we come out of the lockdown. Remember, the initial lockdown was mostly argued for flattening the curve (in addition to maybe it worked in China lets follow them). If you look at the curves (flattened vs. spiked) they have near the same areas, meaning those original scientist who argued for it, never argued the lockdown was going to reduce the net infection by COVID but to slow it down to help the health care system gear up and also not get overwhelmed (lack of rooms, ventilators, etc.) That being said, we can copy some smart and common sense stuff from places like Taiwan (I've spoken to people who live in Taiwan and life is almost back to normal but they just wear masks in public) that has allowed them open up"}],"r":[["jeghGhVdt9s",1]]},"cjuGCJJUGsg":{"wy":"Cited to illustrate false balance and the trouble with giving all sides equal weight.","q":{"x":"There is a nice visual example of this made by John Oliver on his show","i":32222419,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32222419,"author":"consp","month":"2022-07","story":"The audacious PR plot that seeded doubt about climate change","stance":"rec","why":"Visual illustration of climate consensus versus false balance","quote":"There is a nice visual example of this made by John Oliver on his show","text":"There is a nice visual example of this made by John Oliver on his show to illustrate this to a more understandable level: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30265036,"author":"BLKNSLVR","month":"2022-02","story":"You cannot police misinformation in a fair, reproducible, and representative way","stance":"rec","why":"Oliver illustrates false balance in climate coverage","quote":"demonstrated beautifully by John Oliver","text":"Exactly, and demonstrated beautifully by John Oliver: [VIDEO] An aim at 'balance' is part of the problem because it's not an aim at facts or truth. Aiming at balance is the media twisting debate to produce maximum outrage, and therefore increase 'consumer engagement'. Aiming at balance is politicians providing themselves supposed reasons to maintain the status quo or move in counter-factual directions."},{"id":10341965,"author":"lbenes","month":"2015-10","story":"Gains in Antarctic ice might offset losses","stance":"rec","why":"John Oliver on false balance in climate debate","quote":"While looking for the clip I found this gem","text":"Watch Fox new much? Climate deniers love this argument. Jon Stewart answers your question here: [LINK] While looking for the clip I found this gem: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36493123,"author":"latexr","month":"2023-06","story":"AI is killing the old web","stance":"reference","why":"Oliver shows why weighting contrasting opinions equally misleads","quote":"John Oliver illustrated the problem in 2014, using climate change as the example.","text":"> This website should neither compete with nor replace regular journalism. That’s good in theory, but people will use it like that anyway. We’re living in an era of rampant misinformation. Provable truths we’ve know for thousands of years are being called into question (the shape of the Earth). Even people who should know to do their own research (see recent case of lawyers using ChatGPT) are taking AI output as unquestionable truth. Your website won’t eliminate bias, it will only give people more sources to cherry pick. > Hence, I try to highlight different view points along with contrasting opinions Which exacerbates the issue. If we can prove the Earth is a sphere and 99% of people understand that, making it seem like the “contrasting opinion” is equally valid and deserves the same weighted consideration makes the problem worse, not better. John Oliver illustrated the problem in 2014, using climate change as the example. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30265172,"author":"throwamon","month":"2022-02","story":"You cannot police misinformation in a fair, reproducible, and representative way","stance":"reference","why":"Shows problem of representing all sides equally","quote":"And if you want true representativity, you get problems like this","text":"There aren't only \"two sides\", as much as American conditioning may have you believe. And if you want true representativity, you get problems like this: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["15_-hgsX2V0",1],["GoW8Tf7hTGA",1],["HhGuXCuDb1U",1],["lPgZfhnCAdI",1]]},"d6DBKoWbtjE":{"wy":"HN points to it as proof that government-provided housing can be attractive, in housing and homelessness threads.","q":{"x":"See this video for what government provided housing could be.","i":28714337,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":38721326,"author":"toomuchtodo","month":"2023-12","story":"US homelessness up 12% from 2022, hits highest level since 2007","stance":"rec","why":"Vienna shows government can build housing","quote":"Vienna is a great example.","text":"Government can build if private industry will not. If you argue \"we can only fix this with the private sector and private investment\", welp, we tried that and we're plain ol' fucked at the moment. Vienna is a great example. [LINK] (Youtube: Vienna's Radical Idea? Affordable Housing For All) [VIDEO] (Youtube: Does Vienna Have the World's Best Council Housing?)"},{"id":38117223,"author":"toomuchtodo","month":"2023-11","story":"The US Housing Market Has Become an Impossible Mess","stance":"rec","why":"Vienna as a great housing example","quote":"Vienna is a great example.","text":"Vienna is a great example. [LINK] (Youtube: Vienna's Radical Idea? Affordable Housing For All) [VIDEO] (Youtube: Does Vienna Have the World's Best Council Housing?)"},{"id":28714337,"author":"malermeister","month":"2021-10","story":"Klimaticket: All public transport in Austria with a single ticket for 1095 EUR/y","stance":"rec","why":"Shows what high-quality government housing could be","quote":"See this video for what government provided housing could be.","text":"That's just not true. The problem is that government housing is so terrible that sleeping on the street is preferable to some. Public housing in Austria is high quality, cheap and plentiful, with two-thirds of Viennese residents living in it. Coincidentally, homelessness is a non-issue in Austria compared to the US (based on my experience living in the Bay for 10 and in Austria for 20 years). See this video for what government provided housing could be. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37914547,"author":"toomuchtodo","month":"2023-10","story":"If the Economy Is So Strong, Why Are Consumer Stocks Tanking?","stance":"reference","why":"Shows social housing need not look bleak","quote":"It does not have to be this way.","text":"> If we do that you'll have two social classes. You'll have the price fixed low income housing that looks like it belongs in a Command & Conquer game It does not have to be this way. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36523489,"author":"malermeister","month":"2023-06","story":"Airbnb rental revenues have plunged in some parts of the US, this chart suggests","stance":"reference","why":"Counterexample to the claim that public housing is uniform","quote":"That part is just not true. See Vienna.","text":"> Also with all rental properties being government-owned there would be zero uniqueness and everyone would be forced to make due with whatever the government thought they needed. A good example of this is old Soviet-style tower blocks, where no matter how big your family you get the same size apartment. That part is just not true. See Vienna. This is public housing: [LINK] So is this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26663357,"author":"rossng","month":"2021-04","story":"A billion for every chip-maker who 'makes in India,' sources say","stance":"reference","why":"Vienna offered as a positive example of social housing","quote":"there is a more positive example in Vienna's social housing","text":"Sure, I can at least give you my opinion. * They eliminate a lot of street life. Towers almost always have completely dead frontages at ground level - and they are unlikely to be directly adjacent to a real street. You will be driving to a mall - private space that has none of the democratic nature of a public street. * They often reduce the connection between neighbours. Meeting someone in a lift or a corridor isn't the same as stopping to talk to someone in the street. * The 'parks' are normally poor quality. Humans tend to feel more comfortable in enclosed spaces[1], whereas the parks are open fields that feel overshadowed by the large towers. * The parks often feel unsafe too. A significant reason for this is that towers eliminate most of the 'eyes on the street'. You are likely to feel much safer walking past a row of terraced houses than between tower blocks at night. * The buildings are often poorly built/maintained and become eyesores. * They are often built in conjunction with wide roads and excessive parking. Most countries have a good example of how housing towers go wrong. France has its Grands Ensembles - many of which are really cool buildings but which have not aged well[2]. Glasgow is now demolishing many of its towers[3]. Here's an in-depth report from Create Streets, a social enterprise that studies this kind of thing in the UK[4]. For balance, there is a more positive example in Vienna's social housing[5]. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [VI"}],"r":[["oHlpmxLTxpw",1],["41VJudBdYXY",1]]},"gDfNRWsMRsU":{"wy":"Commenters recommend it as an artist's informed explanation of streaming economics and why Spotify pays so little compared with alternatives like Bandcamp.","q":{"x":"I find this artist's explanation of the situation helpful","i":38516704,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38516704,"author":"thomastjeffery","month":"2023-12","story":"Spotify cuts almost 1,600 jobs amid rising costs","stance":"rec","why":"Helpful artist explanation of Spotify's situation","quote":"I find this artist's explanation of the situation helpful","text":"The magic word is \"growth\". For context, I find this artist's explanation of the situation helpful: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37078176,"author":"jasode","month":"2023-08","story":"Ask HN: What are the economics of a streaming based record label?","stance":"rec","why":"Has streaming numbers useful for business planning","quote":"you can watch it for the data and see if it applies to your business plans.","text":"This 17 minute video has a few streaming numbers: [VIDEO] I don't agree with all his predictions and conclusions but you can watch it for the data and see if it applies to your business plans."},{"id":34596804,"author":"consumer451","month":"2023-01","story":"Spotify is first music streaming service to surpass 200M paid subscribers","stance":"rec","why":"Artist explains how Spotify underpays artists versus Bandcamp","quote":"As an example, he made 3x on Bandcamp vs Spotify for the same album.","text":"Benn Jordan, aka The Flashbulb, made a recent video titled: \"Why Spotify Will Ultimately Fail\" (Because they are screwing artists, on whom they depend to generate streamable content.) As an example, he made 3x on Bandcamp vs Spotify for the same album. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40563837,"author":"brink","month":"2024-06","story":"Spotify is increasing US prices again","stance":"reference","why":"Predicted Spotify's demise","quote":"Benn Jordan predicted the demise of Spotify last year, and I'm starting to believe he might be right.","text":"> “So that we can continue to invest in and innovate on our product features and bring users the best experience, we occasionally update our prices,” I don't care about your innovation. I haven't cared about a single new feature that's come out from Spotify in the last 5-7 years. I find myself listening to Spotify less than ever these days anyway. These prices are too much for me, so after ~10 years of premium Spotify, I'll be cancelling this month because I'm not getting enough value. I'll find other avenues of discovering new music. Benn Jordan predicted the demise of Spotify last year, and I'm starting to believe he might be right. [VIDEO]"},{"id":38516814,"author":"konart","month":"2023-12","story":"Spotify cuts almost 1,600 jobs amid rising costs","stance":"reference","why":"Provides an auto-generated summary of the video","quote":"For those against clicking","text":"[VIDEO] For those against clicking: 00:00:00 - 00:15:00 The author of the video argues that Spotify will ultimately fail due to the high cost of membership, the fact that streaming services pay independent musicians less than traditional music platforms, and the company's neglect of its only asset - its artists. 00:00:00 The author of the video argues that Spotify will ultimately fail because of the high cost of membership and the fact that streaming services pay independent musicians less than traditional music platforms. 00:05:00 The author of the video makes the case that Spotify will eventually fail because of its business model, which relies on rapid growth and unsustainable levels of value in the music industry. He argues that if Spotify's independent musicians were paid more fairly, the platform would be unable to survive. 00:10:00 The author of the video argues that Spotify will eventually fail due to the company's neglect of its only asset - its artists. The author believes that this neglect will lead to the eventual collapse of the streaming music industry as a whole. However, he also believes that this collapse will be hastened by the fact that for-profit companies are required to eventually pay their investors as little as possible. 00:15:00 The author of this video argues that streaming services like Spotify will ultimately fail because they rely on artificial scarcity (i.e. the notion that there are not enough songs available to listen to on the platform). They "},{"id":34596543,"author":"S_A_P","month":"2023-01","story":"Spotify is first music streaming service to surpass 200M paid subscribers","stance":"reference","why":"Cited on streaming economics favoring top artists","quote":"","text":"I don’t use Apple Music on windows so can’t argue with the bad client experience, but Spotify isn’t exactly perfect either. To me the issue with iTunes and Spotify is the fact that to be a viable business the streaming model is going to squeeze all artists into making a between 1-2 grand for a million streams if they are that lucky. Tidal promises more money but once the realize those economics don’t work and get large enough they will follow suit. What has happened is also an unprecedented breach of contract for older artists out there without streaming provisions in their contracts. I only know of a handful of instances where the artists have addressed this but I know of at least 1 example of a major label contract that specifically says “in the case of unforeseen circumstances (streaming, mind beaming, any new unavailable technology) the artist will split the proceeds 50/50. This same artist is not getting 50% of the revenue and is in fact getting the same (awful) deal as everyone else. [0] What Spotify/Apple Music/Tidal have done is create the same hierarchy that existed in the days of physical media where they pour money into the top1% of artists and have effectively made everyone else much worse off. [1] Anyone can make and release music today but it was hardly democratized. They same forces in the record industry have figured out how to screw streaming up in their favor now too. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] The 200 million subscribers of Spotify are subsidizing things like J"}],"r":[["B2482h_TNwg",1],["Bd8_vO5zrjo",1],["wh4aWZRtTwU",1],["HSxSgbNQi-g",1],["qs2QcycggWU",1],["NlIdzF1_b5M",1],["KRcecxdGxvQ",1],["0MeRN7LE1LQ",1],["069y1MpOkQY",1],["hBpetDxIEMU",1],["GZ5XN_mJE8Y",1],["NngCHTImH1g",1],["lqzJewjZUkk",1]]},"usSfgHGEGxQ":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an informed look at architecture in games and at how school design relates to shooter maps and Columbine.","q":{"x":"Jacob Geller did a really interesting video essay","i":41872330,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41872330,"author":"JRandomHacker42","month":"2024-10","story":"Rethinking School Design","stance":"rec","why":"Parallels between school architecture and shooter maps","quote":"Jacob Geller did a really interesting video essay","text":"Jacob Geller did a really interesting video essay [1] about the parallels that can be drawn between modern school architecture and maps from shooter games - on top of whatever other context or functionality they provide, they are fundamentally spaces that are designed around the question \"what happens if violence occurs here?\" [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":40403316,"author":"underlipton","month":"2024-05","story":"Disabling Modernism","stance":"rec","why":"How Columbine may have influenced school design","quote":"Jacob Geller has an excellent video essay","text":"Jacob Geller has an excellent video essay about how incidents like Columbine might have contributed to this trend (if only post-90s, obviously). [VIDEO]"},{"id":27227129,"author":"dharmab","month":"2021-05","story":"Building virtual worlds: video games and autobiographical architecture","stance":"rec","why":"More informed analysis of architecture in games","quote":"For a more informed analysis of architecture in games, Jacob Geller has done several video essays on the topic","text":"For a more informed analysis of architecture in games, Jacob Geller has done several video essays on the topic: The Shape of Infinity [LINK] Games, Schools, and Worlds designed for Violence [VIDEO] Control, Anatomy, and the Legacy of the Haunted House [LINK] Gaming's Harshest Architecture [LINK] The Architecture of Fumito Ueda [LINK]"}],"r":[["c2J91UG6Fn8",1],["meHYBhcpdvQ",1],["k1kndKWJKB8",1],["DlTq8DbRs4k",1],["N2jZryt607U",1],["OpEB6hCpIGM",1],["rJjKP8vYjpQ",1],["lIW5jBrrsS0",1]]},"xnWKz7Cthkk":{"wy":"HN uses it as the go-to evidence for Toyota pickup durability, and as a benchmark in discussions of truck toughness.","q":{"x":"It's a worthwhile watch if you want to see truck survive after being drowned in water, fire, and the rubble of a demolished skyscraper.","i":20286142,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":31011921,"author":"helsinkiandrew","month":"2022-04","story":"Never hose out a Honda Element","stance":"rec","why":"Playlist of attempts; part 3 has tower block drop","quote":"Part 3 with the 23-storey drop from a collapsing tower block is worth a watch","text":"Playlist of attempts: [VIDEO] Part 3 with the 23-storey drop from a collapsing tower block is worth a watch"},{"id":20286142,"author":"jakejarvis","month":"2019-06","story":"The Toyota Way","stance":"rec","why":"Hilux survives water, fire, and a demolished skyscraper","quote":"It's a worthwhile watch if you want to see truck survive after being drowned in water, fire, and the rubble of a demolished skyscraper.","text":"The entire decades-long overseas diversion saga surrounding American Toyota pickups has always fascinated me. Top Gear did an episode [0] trying to destroy a Toyota Hilux (similar to the Land Cruiser) and literally couldn't, which explains why it's so popular with terrorists. It's a worthwhile watch if you want to see truck survive after being drowned in water, fire, and the rubble of a demolished skyscraper. Say what you want about Toyotas, but that's quite the engineering feat in my opinion! [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":2656242,"author":"Griever","month":"2011-06","story":"DIY Weapons of the Libyan Rebels","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how well built Toyota trucks are","quote":"Top Gear had an excellent several-part episode","text":"Top Gear had an excellent several-part episode which illustrated just how ridiculously well built these things are. [VIDEO]"},{"id":46966324,"author":"wfleming","month":"2026-02","story":"Toyotas and Terrorists: \"Why are ISIS's trucks better than ours?\"","stance":"reference","why":"Referenced Top Gear segments","quote":"these are the referenced Top Gear segments","text":"For folks who have never seen it, these are the referenced Top Gear segments: - part 1: [VIDEO] - part 2: [VIDEO] - part 3: [LINK]"},{"id":40989862,"author":"toomuchtodo","month":"2024-07","story":"The Toyota Pickup Truck Is the War Chariot of the Third World (2013)","stance":"reference","why":"Links parts of the Killing a Toyota series","quote":"","text":"[VIDEO] (\"Youtube: Killing a Toyota Part 1 | Top Gear\") [LINK] (\"Youtube: Killing a Toyota Part 2 | Top Gear\") [LINK] (\"Youtube: Killing a Toyota Part 3 | Top Gear\")"},{"id":38469656,"author":"Galacta7","month":"2023-11","story":"Who makes the most reliable new cars?","stance":"reference","why":"Hilux survives ramming, sea, and fire","quote":"Top Gear once tried to intentionally kill a Toyota Hilux","text":"Top Gear once tried to intentionally kill a Toyota Hilux, ramming it, parking it in the sea, dropping a trailer on it, and literally setting it on fire. Nothing worked. It still drove. I'm convinced that whatever remains of the charred Earth after WW3, somebody will still be driving a Hilux. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29694757,"author":"Multicomp","month":"2021-12","story":"Finnish Man Blows Up Tesla Car Instead of Replacing Battery","stance":"reference","why":"Show tried to kill a Toyota over three videos","quote":"see this video where a popular automotive show set out to 'kill' (render inoperable without inputting new parts) a Toyota","text":"TL;DR: Toyotas tend to be more reliable, easier to maintain (less yak shaving), and thus tend to last longer and be more valuable. Toyotas have earned their cult-like status because of an extreme focus on quality at manufacture time (see Toyota Production System) which results in durability and reliability of the finished products (the vehicles themselves). For a real world (rather ridiculous and unscientific as it may seem), see this video where a popular automotive show set out to 'kill' (render inoperable without inputting new parts) a Toyota over the course of around 3 videos[1]. Here in the US, I drive a 1998 Toyota 4Runner, a cousin to the vehicle in the video, and I know that every single repair dollar it is fed translates to more miles on the road. Yes, if you feed new parts to old cars, they get slightly less old. But some brands (ancedotes are not data, yada yada, but:) primarily US domestic brands (though foreign BMW etc. can misbehave too) tend to make strange design decisions that result in an unreliable, poorly aging vehicle. A 199x Buick Rivera required my father to drop the front axle IIRC just to swap the oil pan, or something similar. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27387592,"author":"lightsighter","month":"2021-06","story":"Toyota launches groundbreaking 10-year warranty (in UK)","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence Toyotas are near indestructible","quote":"vehicles that are nigh close to indestructible [1,2,3].","text":"In my experience, the whole 10 year warranty seems a bit excessive for vehicles that are nigh close to indestructible [1,2,3]. My parents have had multiple Toyotas that made it to 300K-400K miles. My brother's Corolla was rear-ended by a semi doing 30mph and wasn't even close to being totaled. Eight years later and he's still driving it through swamps in south Florida. They are impeccable feats of engineering. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":21644498,"author":"boznz","month":"2019-11","story":"Tesla truck: $10.5B in pre-orders with $0 in ad spend","stance":"reference","why":"Destruction test as benchmark for trucks","quote":"Maybe TopGear can re-do the destruction test they went through with the Toyota HiLux","text":"Put any other name on this other than Tesla and it would have been laughed off stage (try the thought experiment). The Tesla name on it makes all the difference as you do look at it through different eyes, the eyes that says fuck the current norm and the horse it rode in on. I will never buy one, but its no uglier than any other big old truck, anyone who can see beauty in a truck, any truck, has too much testosterone for their own good IMHO. Maybe TopGear can re-do the destruction test they went through with the Toyota HiLux to see how it really holds up to the competition. ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":18481438,"author":"e12e","month":"2018-11","story":"Quietly, Japan has established itself as a power in the aerospace industry","stance":"reference","why":"Toyota pickup durability test","quote":"Always thought Toyota made pretty durable pickups","text":"Hm. Always thought Toyota made pretty durable pickups : \"Killing a Toyota Part 1 | Top Gear | BBC\" : [VIDEO]"},{"id":13530114,"author":"patrickk","month":"2017-01","story":"Volkswagen overtakes Toyota as the world's biggest carmaker","stance":"reference","why":"Hilux survives demolished building","quote":"Top Gear famously demolished a building with a Hilux perched on top[3], and the engine still worked afterwards.","text":"> 20 years ago you could pick up a world-class Supra or a proper Land Cruiser or a Tacoma or a Camry or Corolla that could practically be the last commuter car you ever need. All of them stubbornly reliable. many of the cars Toyota had on the lot in 1997 still sell for close to their retail price! The mid-90s to early 2000s were a golden era for Japanese cars. I dream of importing a Nissan Silvia S15[1] with the legendary SR20 engine. It's quite feasible, especially if you live in a left hand drive country (warning: it's complicated getting some of these cars into the US[2]). The Skylines of that era (e.g. the blue and silver one from the Fast and the Furious 2) still sell for insane money (around €35k-45k last time I checked, depending on the model and trim), they are really hot items to import from Japan. Also, the Hilux of the era is virtually indestructible. Top Gear famously demolished a building with a Hilux perched on top[3], and the engine still worked afterwards. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12531503,"author":"michaelt","month":"2016-09","story":"I quit my job, bought an army truck, and spent 19 months circumnavigating Africa","stance":"reference","why":"Hilux looks very durable","quote":"they do have a series of videos [1] which make a 1988 Toyota Hilux look very durable.","text":"Top Gear is not by any means a reliable source of motoring journalism, but they do have a series of videos [1] which make a 1988 Toyota Hilux look very durable. At least if you have a mechanic with a \"basic set of tools\" on hand. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":4739148,"author":"nekojima","month":"2012-11","story":"Mystery at Port Newark: Why Did 17 Plug-In Cars Burn?","stance":"reference","why":"Hilux driven through salt water","quote":"The rather famous 'Top Gear tries to kill a Toyota Hilux'.","text":"The rather famous 'Top Gear tries to kill a Toyota Hilux'. [VIDEO] The part when the Hilux goes in the English Channel (which is salt water)."},{"id":21603512,"author":"mattrp","month":"2019-11","story":"Tesla Cybertruck","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I’m hoping for Jeremy clarkson to take a whack at “killing a Tesla” a la the infamous hilux that wouldn’t die: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["RrHnLXMTOWM",1],["st571DYYTR8",1],["g3Ud6mHdhlQ",1],["uYNzqgU7na4",1],["vaCVh2SAZY4",1],["GJCF-Ufapu8",1]]},"BBwepkVurCI":{"wy":"HN people cite it as a vivid demonstration of how editing shapes perception in documentaries and reality television.","q":{"x":"here is an awesome video that shows how editing can do wonders for how we perceive things(1).","i":28414162,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28414162,"author":"superasn","month":"2021-09","story":"WaterBear – A free library of documentaries and shorts","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows how editing changes how we perceive footage.","quote":"here is an awesome video that shows how editing can do wonders for how we perceive things(1).","text":"I used to love watching documentaries but the more you watch them the more you see how most often they are biased and edited to show the authors / producers point of view. How a documentary is edited can mean the same facts and interview being used to show something being positive or totally negative. I guess it's true for all media but I've found this especially true for a lot of recent ones I've seen (last was Bikram yoga predator). Btw here is an awesome video that shows how editing can do wonders for how we perceive things(1). Though this one is about reality tv, I've seen the same done in a lot of recent documentaries too. (1) [VIDEO]"},{"id":42921743,"author":"hgomersall","month":"2025-02","story":"He went to jail for stealing someone's identity, but it was his all along","stance":"rec","why":"Excellent segments related to documentary editing and fluff.","quote":"the not-quite-on-point, but nevertheless excellent two related segments below","text":"Most mainstream documentaries are full of fluff. You can generally read the transcript of a half hour programme in a couple of minutes. I thought Charlie Brooker might have a useful segment on it, but all I could find were the not-quite-on-point, but nevertheless excellent two related segments below: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":28414841,"author":"specialist","month":"2021-09","story":"WaterBear – A free library of documentaries and shorts","stance":"rec","why":"Good points about editing and gear enabling reality TV.","quote":"That's quite good. Nice point about how better gear enabled the reality TV format.","text":"> the more you watch them aka Media literacy. > the more you see how most often they are biased What's the alternative? > ...here is an awesome video that shows how editing can do wonders for how we perceive things ... [VIDEO] That's quite good. Nice point about how better gear enabled the reality TV format. I did some radio and TV stuff as a kid. (A vocational tech program. Student radio, cable public access, job shadowing, \"journalism\", etc.) Transmuted me into a \"Kill your television\" type crank. I can't watch \"the news\" without yelling at the TV. As a counterpoint: Robert X Cringley's NerdTV had the radical notion of sharing all of the source footage. Viewers could watch his edit, highlight reels, and teasers. And then they could also make their own. Sadly, this format hasn't caught on. Anyway. It seems to me that Zoomers will prove to be the most media literate generation. And so therefore the least susceptible to ham-fisted narrative techniques. Certainly compared to Boomers."},{"id":31372652,"author":"mhh__","month":"2022-05","story":"EU Commission is planning automatic CSAM scanning of private communications","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"David Attenborough does not make the same style of programming that Curtis does. Curtis has a very specific world view, or world vibe, let's say whereas Attenborough does have views about the world but not much else beyond visual grandeur. His earlier stuff is definitely better than his more recent work, but I refer specifically to the hypernormalization documentary because it's absolutely full of \"Syria this\", \"Assad this\", \"[large country or company] decided to do [remarkably simple thing]\". Maybe it's right, but the point is that it's structured and edited in a way where he wants to make you feel his argument that the world is being forced into a state of inaction, not convince you that his argument is correct. In this specific documentary, he is undeniably flying extremely close to the sun in how he presents his argument versus what he is arguing against. He argument is all about the \"theys\" and the men in palaces, and obviously some decisions are, but some inference one makes in this mindset are simply the result of being fooled by randomness. Don't get me wrong, I like listening to Adam Curtis docs while I'm working, I just think that he goes too far. When he is covering identifiable flaws he is absolutely brilliant, e.g. his piece on Nick Leeson is really nicely done, when he is kind of mumbling about some kind of incompetent conspiracy to do [something] he is often charismatic but shallow. Also (a point about documentary filmmaking in general) the use of interviews is"},{"id":9142763,"author":"brownbat","month":"2015-03","story":"The ‘Loser Edit’ That Awaits Us All","stance":"reference","why":"Same footage edited to flip the tone.","quote":"using the same footage and completely flipping the tone","text":"Charlie Brooker's Screenwipe demonstrates this perfectly, using the same footage and completely flipping the tone: [VIDEO] The prologue of This American Life episode 458, \"Play the Part\" provides good testimony about this process from someone who worked in editing. [LINK] Chuck Klosterman's \"Sex, Drugs, and Cocoa Puffs\" has a chapter on the Real World, \"What Happens When People Stop Being Polite.\" It'd probably be the most work to seek out, but it's my highest recommendation, well worth it. He's a pretty entertaining writer, and has some great insights here. He tracks down one of the early stars and points out a few frightening ways that real life interactions mimic reality television shows."},{"id":7043127,"author":"brownbat","month":"2014-01","story":"Down The Rabbit Hole We Go: Mind-Expanding Documentaries","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates emotional editing techniques in reality TV.","quote":"Charlie Brooker once provided a nice demonstration of emotional editing techniques, albeit in the context of reality tv","text":"Charlie Brooker once provided a nice demonstration of emotional editing techniques, albeit in the context of reality tv: [VIDEO] Reality TV is just documentary filmmaking with a rapid production schedule and without the pretense of dignity. To crib from Bobby Shaftoe, it's really documentary filmmaking, but moreso."}],"r":[["OjMPJVmXxV8",1],["KLjgBLwH3Wc",1],["ajGX7odA87k",1],["7J7X7aZvMXQ",1],["k1luKAS_Xcg",1],["aHun58mz3vI",1]]},"MzzD2F73iGU":{"wy":"HN recommends it for DeRisi's fascinating stories about rare pathogens such as brain-eating amoebas.","q":{"x":"The stories he tells are absolutely fascinating and I thoroughly encourage everyone","i":24606454,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24606454,"author":"NamTaf","month":"2020-09","story":"Fatal brain-eating microbe found in Lake Jackson, Texas water supply","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Molecular biologist's fascinating stories, including brain-eating amoebas.","quote":"The stories he tells are absolutely fascinating and I thoroughly encourage everyone","text":"Adam Savage's podcast Still Untitled did a really fantastic episode [1] back around Halloween 2018 where they had a molecular biologist come on and talk about his work. The stories he tells are absolutely fascinating and I thoroughly encourage everyone wiht any interest in molecular biology and crazy stories to check it out. I won't go into it because I'll spoil it, but suffice it to say that normal episodes are 20-30 mins, this one went for 70 mins and the first time I listened to it I immediately restarted it to listen to it again. The first half of the episode is a story that gets more and more insane as it unfolds. Afterwards, he goes into a few other shorter stories of his work. Anyway, the link to this is because there's one story from about 38:15 to about 45 minutes in that discusses brain-eating amoebas he was involved in. It's not this exact one, but it's related to it. I recommend anyone who finds this interesting to check it out. Although there's video on youtube, you really don't need video for it and your podcast app of choice should have an audio version of it. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42302151,"author":"codethief","month":"2024-12","story":"Fish have a brain microbiome – could humans have one too?","stance":"rec","why":"Podcast with Joe DeRisi on his sequencing approach.","quote":"Adam Savage's excellent podcast with him","text":"From what I understand Joe DeRisi's approach[0] comes relatively close to your \"blender\" idea! [0]: I already mentioned Adam Savage's excellent podcast with him elsewhere in this dicussion but here's a direct link: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27852639,"author":"dzhiurgis","month":"2021-07","story":"Alphafold","stance":"reference","why":"Podcast on detecting rare infections by sequencing blood.","quote":"here's interesting podcast on how they detect rare infections","text":"Re step 1 and 2 - here's interesting podcast on how they detect rare infections: [VIDEO] Basically sequence everything what's in your blood and look for what doesn't match your genome === infection. The problem is this is orders of magnitude more compute intense than whole genome sequencing. Basically increased demand for sequencing far outmatches available compute!"}],"r":[["_rBO8neWw04",1],["s1i-dnAH9Y4",1]]},"TrfD3pC0VSs":{"wy":"HN points to it as an entertaining deep dive on Linux emulation on SmartOS and on how the Solaris kernel and libc are tightly coupled.","q":{"x":"I really enjoyed this talk[1] by Bryan Cantrill","i":11078820,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":11078820,"author":"ketralnis","month":"2016-02","story":"Show HN: Htop 2.0 released, now cross-platform","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Lengthy, enjoyable talk on emulating Linux syscalls on SmartOS, with htop rant.","quote":"I really enjoyed this talk[1] by Bryan Cantrill","text":"I really enjoyed this talk[1] by Bryan Cantrill, part of which is about what it took to emulate Linux's syscalls from within SmartOS. It's lengthy, but enjoyable. One of the applications that they tried to port directly to before the emulation layer was htop, and he has a good rant about what that took because of its use of /proc and how that made porting it very difficult. Given that, I'm sure this wasn't easy. Kudos! [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":10393284,"author":"AnbeSivam","month":"2015-10","story":"Running Linux containers on an illumos kernel","stance":"rec","why":"Compares Linux and SmartOS while running Linux containers on illumos.","quote":"Though it is from 2014, it is interesting on comparison between linux and smartos(solaris).","text":"Video of this talk. [VIDEO] Though it is from 2014, it is interesting on comparison between linux and smartos(solaris). There is also a recent, shorter video [LINK]"},{"id":21891625,"author":"the_why_of_y","month":"2019-12","story":"The Unix C library API can only be reliably used from C","stance":"reference","why":"Cantrill describes Solaris kernel/libc as a welded unit and mocks Linux libc ecosystem.","quote":"Here's a talk where Bryan Cantrill describes the tightly coupled Solaris kernel/libc combination as a \"welded unit","text":"A stable syscall ABI is a Linux peculiarity and was never a part of the UNIX tradition. Here's a talk where Bryan Cantrill describes the tightly coupled Solaris kernel/libc combination as a \"welded unit\", and mocks the Linux \"libc ecosystem\" and the complexity of ENOSYS handling in glibc. [VIDEO]"},{"id":15333856,"author":"Manozco","month":"2017-09","story":"Linux System Call Table","stance":"reference","why":"Cantrill on reimplementing Linux syscalls on a Solaris-derived kernel.","quote":"Bryan Cantrill did a bunch of conferences on that","text":"You might need that when you want to reimplement Linux, the Joyent team did that on their OS (derived from solaris) so that user can run linux binaries on a solaris kernel (so thay have dtrace, zfs, mdb, ...) Bryan Cantrill did a bunch of conferences on that (one here: [VIDEO] ) The idea behind is that Linux is only a list of syscalls, if you are able to reimplement them, you reimplement linux, you don't need anything else. On the contrary if you want to reimplement a BSD you need to reimplement their libc (and perhaps some other libraries)"},{"id":13081622,"author":"bcantrill","month":"2016-12","story":"\"Solaris being canned, at least 50% of teams to be RIF'd in short term\"","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"completed and productized Linux-branded zones","text":"From my perspective, if this rumor is true, it's a relief. Solaris died the moment that they made the source proprietary -- a decision so incredibly stupid that it still makes my head hurt six years later. Fortunately, Solaris was open long enough that we in the open source world were able to fork it with illumos[1]. And because illumos became the home for many of us that brought Solaris its most famous innovations (e.g., ZFS, DTrace and zones), it should come as no surprise that we've continued to innovate over the last six years. (Speaking only for Joyent, we added revolutionary debugging support for node.js[2], ported KVM to it[3], completed and productized Linux-branded zones[4], added software-defined networking[5] and developed first-class Docker integration[6] -- among many, many other innovations.) So illumos (and derivatives like SmartOS, OmniOS and DelphixOS) is vibrant and alive -- but one of our biggest challenges has been its association with the name \"Solaris\": I don't think of our system as Solaris any more than I think of it as \"SVR4\" or \"SunOS\" or \"7th Edition\" or any of its other names -- and the very presence of Solaris has served to confuse. And indeed, it is my good fortune to be working with a new generation of engineers on the operating system -- engineers for whom the term \"Solaris\" is entirely distant and its presence as an actual (if proprietary) system befuddling. So if the rumor is true (and I suspect that it is), it will allow everyone to know wha"},{"id":12410170,"author":"qwertyuiop924","month":"2016-09","story":"What’s up with the Beep driver in Windows 7? (2010)","stance":"reference","why":"Cantrill anecdote about an rpm race bug and a dirty compatibility workaround.","quote":"You can find the actual talk at","text":"The Simcity case reminds me of Bryan Cantrill's talk on lx-branded zones: apparently, there was a bug in rpm which didn't touch Linux, because Linux wasn't doing things fast enough to trigger the condition. So there was some code added to check if the running program was rpm, and to pause it briefly if it was. Apparently the comment the people who wrote it left amounted to, \"we wrote this, and even we feel dirty about it.\" You can find the actual talk at [VIDEO]"},{"id":12286941,"author":"ketralnis","month":"2016-08","story":"Steam on FreeBSD","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"it's the same trick that SmartOS's Linux zones use as well","text":"FreeBSD emulating Linux has way less performance overhead than either emulating Windows. FreeBSD's Linux \"emulator\" consists in a substituted syscall table and a kernel module for implementing syscalls that aren't native to FreeBSD (like epoll).[0][1] Here's an example of what that means. When a FreeBSD binary wants to delete a file it calls the syscall unlink(2). So it's executing as a regular old binary, does a regular syscall, specifies the unlink syscall number, the kernel looks up that syscall number in that binary's (FreeBSD's) syscall table, and jumps to that code. When you execute a Linux binary on FreeBSD you also execute it as a regular old binary except that you specify to use the Linux syscall table instead of FreeBSD's (IIRC this is done by the elf loader, so you're not even saying \"linuxrun ./mybinary\"; you're just saying \"./mybinary\"). It's executing more or less identically to a FreeBSD binary except that when it's time to delete a file it looks up the unlink system call in the Linux syscall table and jumps to that instead. Some of the arguments or calling conventions are different so most of the calls have a shim to rearrange stuff for the FreeBSD equivalents before calling into the same code the FreeBSD binaries are using. This is the same thing that FreeBSD does to support ABI versions for old versions of itself, to support SysVR4 binaries[2], and it's the same trick that SmartOS's Linux zones use as well[3] What this means is that if your binary isn't usin"},{"id":9951101,"author":"mwcampbell","month":"2015-07","story":"Announcing ASLR Completion","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"If bcantrill's description of VDSOs (buried in this presentation","text":"I don't understand. If bcantrill's description of VDSOs (buried in this presentation: [VIDEO] ) is accurate, then they're just a way to make certain syscalls faster, when syscalls are already fast enough."},{"id":9260344,"author":"bcantrill","month":"2015-03","story":"Triton: Docker and the “best of all worlds”","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on LX-branded zones given at illumos Day at Surge 2014.","quote":"the talk on LX-branded zones that I gave at illumos Day at Surge 2014.","text":"Funny you should mention that one in particular -- from our (SmartOS's) epoll(5) man page: While a best effort has been made to mimic the Linux semantics, there are some semantics that are too peculiar or ill-conceived to merit accommodation. In particular, the Linux epoll facility will -- by design -- continue to generate events for closed file descriptors where/when the underlying file description remains open. For example, if one were to fork(2) and subsequently close an actively epoll'd file descriptor in the parent, any events generated in the child on the implicitly duplicated file descriptor will continue to be delivered to the parent -- despite the fact that the parent itself no longer has any notion of the file description! This epoll facility refuses to honor these semantics; closing the EPOLL_CTL_ADD'd file descriptor will always result in no further events being generated for that event description. So while we do aspire to be bug-compatible, we're not about to compromise our principles over it. More details (or some of them, anyway) can be found in the talk on LX-branded zones that I gave at illumos Day at Surge 2014.[1][2] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":8895877,"author":"bcantrill","month":"2015-01","story":"Show HN: Jetpack, a FreeBSD Jail/ZFS-based container runtime","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"We have put (and are putting) a bunch of work into LX branded zones","text":"We have put (and are putting) a bunch of work into LX branded zones[1], and are at the point that they are working on an incredibly broad class of apps (64-bit and with on-the-metal performance). A concrete case in point: we recently ran the (amazing!) hundred language quine relay[2] in an LX branded 64-bit Ubuntu 14.04 zone on SmartOS.[3] As for ZFS support and Docker, it will be via sdc-docker[4], our (emerging) end-point for the Docker Remote API. The progress there has been swift and everything is being done in the open; expect to see something in production from us in this first calendar quarter. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK]"},{"id":8901016,"author":"the_why_of_y","month":"2015-01","story":"Interview: Lennart Poettering","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"the Solaris kernel/libc combination is so tightly coupled that it is described as a \"welded unit","text":"Of course you can get more specific - for example, the Solaris kernel/libc combination is so tightly coupled that it is described as a \"welded unit\" [VIDEO] OpenBSD solved the Year 2038 problem by incompatibly changing the kernel and libc ABI in lockstep, which broke every single existing binary application using time_t - how's that for tight coupling... [LINK]"},{"id":8817088,"author":"bcantrill","month":"2014-12","story":"Fin – Keith Wesolowski retires","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Linux suffers enormously from NIH","text":"Assuming the last question is earnest: while I think \"amateur\" is a tad strong, I do think that Linux suffers enormously from NIH; it remains the first system (or first Unix system, anyway) for many of its contributors, who seem to know no other way -- and this shows up all over the system.[1] In terms of how this has changed over time: if anything, it used to be much, much worse. For example, Linus' attitude on kernel debugging circa 2000[2] is not merely amateurish, and not merely irresponsible, but actively dangerous. While his attitudes have soften over the years, there still remain striking differences of engineering principle between Linux and other systems -- of which systemd is merely (in my opinion) one particularly egregious manifestation. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":8568343,"author":"helper","month":"2014-11","story":"SmartDataCenter and Manta are now open source","stance":"reference","why":"Cantrill talk on running Linux binaries on SmartOS via LX-branded zones.","quote":"Bryan Cantrill gave a talk on it at SurgeConf this year","text":"Not sure what in the announcement you are referring to. The only thing it says about Docker is that it has brought move visibility to OS-virtualization. But, Joyent is infact working on docker support for SmartOS. They have revived an old Solaris feature called LX-branded zones that allows running linux binaries directly in SmartOS. Bryan Cantrill gave a talk on it at SurgeConf this year: video: [VIDEO] slides: [LINK]"}],"r":[["hgN8pCMLI2U",1]]},"es6U00LMmC4":{"wy":"Some commenters credit it with persuading them to go vegan, while others dismiss parts of it as pseudoscience.","q":{"x":"the logical inconsistencies become very apparent","i":11145238,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":10948120,"author":"noondip","month":"2016-01","story":"Confessions of a Paleo Diet Pioneer","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Persuaded the commenter to go vegan.","quote":"Best Speech You Will Ever Hear by Gary Yourofsky","text":"> Do you spend more on food than you did as a non-vegan? I did when I'd first started out with plant-based eating, because I didn't know which foods to purchase and a lot went to waste. I had also sought out meat, cheese and dairy alternatives like Beyond Meat and Tofurky which aren't competitively priced (yet). Now that I've been vegan for over a year - and eating almost exclusively whole foods - the grocery bill for my household is drastically lower. I buy a lot of food in bulk. A 25 lb. bag of brown rice provides tens of thousands of calories to last months and costs a mere ~$60, for example. Canned legumes, frozen fruits and veggies and seasonal produce from my garden, CSA or farmers' markets are all very affordable. > What made you take up veganism? Two YouTube videos - 101 Reasons to Go Vegan - [LINK] Best Speech You Will Ever Hear by Gary Yourofsky - [VIDEO] Later, I learned animal agriculture is responsible for the most greenhouse gas emissions and avoiding animal products can protect against most \"diseases of affluence\", which reaffirmed my decision."},{"id":11145238,"author":"noondip","month":"2016-02","story":"Evidence that fish have feelings","stance":"rec","why":"Argues about inconsistency in which animals we eat.","quote":"the logical inconsistencies become very apparent","text":"> Why do I have to give eating salmon any thought? The same reason you would give thought to killing a Golden Retriever for food. Due to indoctrinated beliefs, people think it's alright to kill certain animals for food, but not others, and when asked why, the logical inconsistencies become very apparent. See [LINK] and [VIDEO] > Am I supposed to justify stomping on a cockroach too? Where does this moralizing end? Any killing is self-defense in always justified. However, if there's an opportunity to practice non-violence, why not take it?"},{"id":11702647,"author":"PerfectElement","month":"2016-05","story":"Luxembourg's leaders have proposed a far-reaching animal rights bill","stance":"reference","why":"Popular speech credited with influencing many Israelis.","quote":"how he alone made 8% of Israel go vegan[0] with his popular Youtube speech[1].","text":"I think that's inevitable. You just have to look at Gary Yourofsky and how he alone made 8% of Israel go vegan[0] with his popular Youtube speech[1]. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":4840546,"author":"jiggy2011","month":"2012-11","story":"Eating meat and cooking food is how humans got their big brains","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Lots of psuedoscience in there.","text":"I think Gary Yourofsky claims something similar to that. But he's pretty extreme even by vegan standards. [VIDEO] Lots of psuedoscience in there."}]},"m1lhGqNCZlA":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an amazing, entertaining talk and cites it in threads on power-line sabotage and coup tactics.","q":{"x":"If you've not seen this amazing talk I can't recommend it enough.","i":21034270,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21034270,"author":"taylorfinley","month":"2019-09","story":"Aerial Saw Is Boon to Line Trimming","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Amazing talk featuring a drone-mounted saw for cutting power lines","quote":"If you've not seen this amazing talk I can't recommend it enough.","text":"I thought this link was going to be about Mr. Choppy, the drone-mounted power saw designed to cut power lines (for instigating coups, naturally) that Chris Rock showed off[0] in his DEF CON 24 talk, 'How To Overthrow a Government.' If you've not seen this amazing talk I can't recommend it enough. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13609570,"author":"eatbitseveryday","month":"2017-02","story":"U.S. Visitors May Have to Hand Over Social Media Passwords","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on forging identities and overthrowing a government","quote":"You might be interested to listen to this talk at DEF CON.","text":"You might be interested to listen to this talk at DEF CON. Chris Rock (the speaker) tells you how to one could forge new identities, SSN, etc. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":14679890,"author":"hulahoof","month":"2017-07","story":"SEC Files Fraud Charges in Bitcoin and Office Space Investment Schemes","stance":"reference","why":"Talk mentions this tactic as part of creating conditions for a coup","quote":"he mentions this tactic as part of creating the environment for a coup","text":"IIRC in this talk by Chris Rock at DC24 he mentions this tactic as part of creating the environment for a coup: Edit - direct link [VIDEO]"},{"id":14204467,"author":"tonyarkles","month":"2017-04","story":"Fusion reactors: Not what they’re cracked up to be","stance":"reference","why":"Talk covers taking out power lines with a drone-mounted saw","quote":"A pair of Skilsaws hanging under a quadcopter.","text":"I'm not 100% positive that this is the right video, but I think it is. As part of the \"overthrow a nation\" plan, he talks specifically about destroying critical infrastructure, like electricity. The solution for taking out high voltage powerlines? A pair of Skilsaws hanging under a quadcopter. Drop down onto it and... something bad's going to happen! edit: I also missed the link: [VIDEO] edit 2: I scrolled around a bit and it's near the 30 minute mark."}]},"-7qT_38iRSc":{"wy":"Cited in threads about search bias, election influence and big-tech data practices as the most comprehensive video of Epstein's research.","q":{"x":"It's worth watching even if it's 1 hour and 23 minutes.","i":15016894,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":30273818,"author":"robbedpeter","month":"2022-02","story":"TikTok shares your data more than any other app and it’s unclear where it goes","stance":"rec","why":"Most comprehensive video on Epstein's election monitoring research","quote":"is the most comprehensive video, but I'll need to spend more time later to gather better links.","text":">>The 2020 presidential election monitoring project successfully recruited a diverse group of 732 registered voters in three battleground states: Arizona, Florida, and North Carolina. It preserved more than 500,000 ephemeral experiences on Google, Bing, Yahoo, YouTube, and Facebook. These are the fleeting experiences that Big Tech companies use to influence votes and opinions worldwide and that are normally lost forever. >>A preliminary analysis of the data shows that Google search results (but NOT search results on Bing or Yahoo) had a significant liberal bias – enough to have shifted at least 6 million votes in the months leading up to the election. You need to know the methodology being used to understand the results, as they tracked ephemeral experiences using surveillance software with volunteers. This guy needs a website team in the biggest way. It's hard to find the information and it's scattershot. It's very frustrating because the work he's doing is solid. Don't let the shitty web presence deter you. The Rogan podcast was awkward af but he does provide a lot of substantive content if you're open to listening. [LINK] is probably the most coherent site, but there's no salient tldr breakdown, to learn more you'll need to watch the videos. [VIDEO] is the most comprehensive video, but I'll need to spend more time later to gather better links."},{"id":15016894,"author":"reirob","month":"2017-08","story":"Facebook’s Onavo Gives Social-Media Firm Inside Peek at Rivals’ Users","stance":"rec","why":"Seminar on unethical algorithms of massive scale","quote":"It's worth watching even if it's 1 hour and 23 minutes.","text":"Stanford Seminar - Unethical Algorithms of Massive Scale [1]. It's worth watching even if it's 1 hour and 23 minutes. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16035480,"author":"drallison","month":"2017-12","story":"The search engine manipulation effect and its possible impact on elections [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Epstein's Stanford talk on search engine effects","quote":"Robert Epstein has given a couple of talks on the search engine effect at Stanford","text":"Robert Epstein has given a couple of talks on the search engine effect at Stanford which have been published to YouTube. Stanford Seminar - Unethical Algorithms of Massive Scale - YouTube Video for epstein youtube stanford ▶ 1:23:56 [VIDEO] Jun 8, 2017 - Uploaded by stanfordonline EE380: Computer Systems Colloquium Seminar Unethical Algorithms of Massive Scale: New Data, a New ... Stanford Seminar - The Search Engine Manipulation Effect ... - YouTube Video for epstein youtube stanford search engine ▶ 1:14:04 [LINK] Oct 29, 2015 - Uploaded by stanfordonline \"The Search Engine Manipulation Effect (SEME) and Its Unparalleled Power To Influence How We Think ..."}]},"0z03xkwFbw4":{"wy":"HN points to it as an accessible case that diets work differently for different people, partly due to gut bacteria.","q":{"x":"It was not just about the food, it was also about the person eating it","i":21951771,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":38375414,"author":"gardnr","month":"2023-11","story":"In the Gut's 'Second Brain,' Key Agents of Health Emerge","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting talk on best diet and the gut.","quote":"This is an interesting TEDx talk from Eran Segal","text":"Then there's something about Heart Rate Variability (HRV), the vagus nerve, parasympathetic nervous system, and gut biome all being connected. I can measure one of those relatively easily with a chest strap. It seems like the only thing we do know at this point is that we don't know anything. This is an interesting TEDx talk from Eran Segal \"What is the best diet for humans?\": [VIDEO] My current conjecture / conspiracy theory / understanding includes: * Bacteria near the roots of plants generate electricity. * Plants grow faster in electrified soil. * Electrical brain stimulation can enhance brain function. * There are processes and interactions happening in our gut that we really don't understand yet. * Many ubiquitous things in our environment wreak havoc on our gut biomes (e.g. chlorine, emulsifiers). * There may be more to establishing and maintaining a healthy gut biome than simply consuming foods rich in pre/probiotics. The right kind of exercise and stress may play essential role in keeping it all humming along."},{"id":21951771,"author":"lhuser123","month":"2020-01","story":"Caffeine attenuates high-fat-high-sucrose lipogenesis and body fat accumulation","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on how food effects differ per individual.","quote":"It was not just about the food, it was also about the person eating it","text":"> Nutrition is confusing because we’re not used to looking at lots of tiny effects adding up to something large. This. I think is always more complicated than we think. And one thing that many times is left out of the discussion is that some foods may have different effects on one individual than others. I was once looking for videos that talk about this and after watching too many, the only one found was : “What is the best diet for humans? | Eran Segal | TEDxRuppin” [VIDEO] When explaining their studies, at one point he said “It was not just about the food, it was also about the person eating it”"},{"id":21343793,"author":"fluffything","month":"2019-10","story":"Edward Snowden on The Joe Rogan Experience [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Research that diets work differently for different people.","quote":"There is some research nowadays showing that different diets work differently well for different people","text":"There is some research nowadays showing that different diets work differently well for different people: [VIDEO] Most of our current long-term (>1 year long) studies about diets deal with the problem of \"subjects not sticking to the diet\" by just saying that X% of participants stopped the diet and then proceeding to analyze the health benefits for the Y% that stuck to it. That's a fair thing to do, but what's not supported from those results is the conclusion that such a diet would have worked very well for the X% had they stuck to it."},{"id":17552577,"author":"jmulho","month":"2018-07","story":"Show HN: Glycemic load calculator","stance":"reference","why":"Individual glycemic response varies with gut bacteria.","quote":"There is a Ted Talk about how glycemic response varies by individual due to different makeup of gut bacteria.","text":"I don't know how to calculate it. But I do know that the combination matters. Try this experiment. Eat a bagel plain for breakfast. Wait as long as you can for lunch. Note when you start feeling like you are starving and your hands get shaky (blood sugar crash). Next day eat the bagel with peanut butter. Note how you feel after the same amount of time. I predict the blood sugar crash will later, or won't happen at all. I have found that the secret to sustaining a vegan diet is beans (resistant starch). They release sugar into your blood over many hours, blunting the blood sugar roller coaster that would otherwise be caused by eating a lot of fruits and vegetables without a lot of meats and fat. I have done a version of the above proposed experiment with a foot long Subway sandwich, veggies only (no meat, no cheese, no mayo). I feel like I am starving in 3 hours. Add hummus, I feel fine for 6 hours. I know it adds some calories, but I'm convinced it is more about the resistant starch. According to this video [LINK] eating beans in one meal even reduces the blood sugar spike in later meals! Another interesting thing. There is a Ted Talk about how glycemic response varies by individual due to different makeup of gut bacteria. I think it is this one: [VIDEO] . The guy claims he can analyze your gut bacteria and tell you which foods will spike your blood sugar. Subway might be bad for me but fine for you!"}]},"4j_UjIshzMc":{"wy":"HN cites it in mass timber and construction threads as an example of insulation-free, glue-free all-wood building in Europe.","q":{"x":"where Matt Risinger is visiting a Swiss factory where they use wooden (berch?) nails and dowel pins in stead of glue.","i":24606712,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24606712,"author":"ehou","month":"2020-09","story":"Cement is the source of 8% of the world's CO2 emissions (2018)","stance":"rec","why":"Glue-free CLT with wooden nails and dowels, no insulation needed.","quote":"where Matt Risinger is visiting a Swiss factory where they use wooden (berch?) nails and dowel pins in stead of glue.","text":"Good question. There is also CLT without glue. Check [VIDEO] where Matt Risinger is visiting a Swiss factory where they use wooden (berch?) nails and dowel pins in stead of glue. And by using more layers they also need no insulation. Gives R=24..."},{"id":22056935,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2020-01","story":"The hottest new thing in sustainable building is, uh, wood","stance":"rec","why":"American builder learns German and Swiss wood building techniques.","quote":"an American builder visiting DE and CH and learning about them","text":"For those not familiar with these techniques, an American builder visiting DE and CH and learning about them: * [VIDEO] * [LINK] * [LINK]"},{"id":24606775,"author":"ehou","month":"2020-09","story":"Cement is the source of 8% of the world's CO2 emissions (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Glue-free CLT example.","quote":"There is also CLT without glue, see my other comment.","text":"There is also CLT without glue, see my other comment. Check [VIDEO] . \"passive designs without active mechanical assistance\" - do you mean passive (highly insulated, air tight) designs without mechanical ventilation?"},{"id":24607461,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2020-09","story":"Cement is the source of 8% of the world's CO2 emissions (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Swiss example of growing wood building in Europe.","quote":"some Swiss examples","text":"> I live in Europe and I can't understand why houses in the US are built with wood and drywall. It surely must be cheaper to use bricks/concrete and then renovate it fully every 20 years, or am I wrong here? I would think that renovations are much easier to do, regardless of the time interval between them, with wood and drywall. Drywall can be torn apart with by someone with just their hands; wood may need some cutting, but once in shorter pieces, can often just be yanked. Even a large house can probably be gutted/internally-exposed in a day or two. With any kind of masonry you have to swing a sledge hammer with a lot of force or bring in jack hammers. Once set, concrete will resist a lot of force against it, which is why it's generally used for slabs and foundations. It also depends on what you mean by renovate: often changes are in internal layout, so if one uses good design techniques for the shell: * [LINK] And floor trusses to reduce/eliminate internal load-bearing walls: * [LINK] It give completely flexibility on what can be done inside the box. Wood is also fairly cheap and plentiful in North America. As for drywall: what are the alternatives for internal walls? Lath and plaster? * [LINK] Some growth in popularity for wood in Europe; some Swiss examples: * [LINK] * [VIDEO]"},{"id":20058939,"author":"jaggederest","month":"2019-05","story":"As Mass Timber Takes Off, How Green Is This New Building Material?","stance":"reference","why":"Solid wood replaces insulation, wallboard and sheathing.","quote":"instead you just... put more wood on it.","text":"[VIDEO] That's literally what it is. You can do it in all sorts of interesting different ways, but the idea is, where with other techniques you'd use e.g. insulation, gypsum wallboard, oriented strand board sheathing or plywood, instead you just... put more wood on it."},{"id":19217570,"author":"jhallenworld","month":"2019-02","story":"At 18 Stories, Mjøsa Tower Is World’s Tallest Wooden Building","stance":"reference","why":"Swiss all-wood houses with thick walls and no insulation.","quote":"This Swiss company makes all-wood houses: meaning no insulation","text":"This Swiss company makes all-wood houses: meaning no insulation, they just make the solid wood walls thick enough to pass insulation codes without requiring foam or fiberglass. Also no metal fasteners. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["K3lP3BhvnSo",1],["ORzNZUeUHAM",1],["ipVxxxqwBQw",1],["WVDQEoe6ZWY",1],["BCmz-fgp24E",1],["rkfAcWpOYAA",1],["jN7mSXMruEo",1],["Uwl3-jBNEd4",1],["ASXJgvy3mEg",1],["1FXuMs6YRCY",1],["wdgULBpRoXk",1],["_rBO8neWw04",1],["-ZDZtBRTyeI",1],["8wlld3Z9wRc",1],["lBVvnDfW2Xo",1],["7zrx-b2sLUs",1],["Ll6-eGDpimU",1],["NSaRb-iFwPA",1],["YtfzjehDg74",1],["NRoUC9P1PmA",1]]},"5V1ynVyud4M":{"wy":"HN mentions it as one of the talks on Eve, usually as related prior work in discussions of end-user and visual programming.","m":[{"id":34486457,"author":"EdiX","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: Has anyone fully attempted Bret Victor's vision?","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on how such approaches fail in practice","quote":"Which is an interesting talk about how these approaches fail in practice.","text":"A lot of people did. To what others have said, I'm going to add this: [VIDEO] Which is an interesting talk about how these approaches fail in practice."},{"id":26399297,"author":"anotheryou","month":"2021-03","story":"Nodes.io – A new way to create with code","stance":"rec","why":"Early Eve prototype that is table based","quote":"Check out this early prototype of eve mentioned here","text":"Check out this early prototype of eve mentioned here: [VIDEO] Not language agnostic, but table based. (eve went in to a different direction afterwards though)"},{"id":21558616,"author":"hencq","month":"2019-11","story":"Tinyland","stance":"reference","why":"Eve as related prior work","quote":"","text":"Very interesting. I'm curious what you'll come up with. The description also reminds me of Eve [1][2], which is itself based on Bloom [3][4]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK]"},{"id":18423181,"author":"cmontella","month":"2018-11","story":"Against the Current: What We Learned from Eve","stance":"reference","why":"One of the author's Eve talks","quote":"","text":"That's a good suggestion. I'll do a little makeshift one here :) Blog posts are mostly here: [LINK] and a couple here: [LINK] Talks: [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] Prototypes are collected here: [LINK] I'll try to make this nicer and put it on our homepage."},{"id":12822141,"author":"roller","month":"2016-10","story":"Eve: Programming designed for humans","stance":"reference","why":"Chris Granger's Eve talk at Strange Loop","quote":"Chris Granger gave an Eve talk at the same conference.","text":"For an introduction to Dedalus with less reading, Peter Alvaro's 2015 Strange Loop talk was fantastic. [LINK] --- Edit: Actually Chris Granger gave an Eve talk at the same conference. It's interesting to see how Eve has evolved since. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["VZQoAKJPbh8",1]]},"6Nfqpr3ygSg":{"wy":"HN uses it as the go-to demonstration of the loudness war and what over-compressed mastering does to music.","q":{"x":"A comparison between these two versions was the eye opener for me.","i":19112324,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":30586585,"author":"theshrike79","month":"2022-03","story":"Loudness War","stance":"rec","why":"Visualizes the dynamic range difference between the two masters","quote":"Here's a Youtube video visualising the difference","text":"If you don't believe in the Loudness War, or think that you can't hear the difference, here is a challenge for you: Grab Metallica's Death Magnetic. Listen through it all the way. Now find the Guitar Hero III remastered version of the same album, listen through it. I guarantee you feel exhausted after the official master version, because there is no dynamic range, it's just a full spectrum of noise. Here's a Youtube video visualising the difference: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19112324,"author":"theshrike79","month":"2019-02","story":"They Don’t Make Music Like They Used To","stance":"rec","why":"Eye-opening comparison of over-compressed versus properly mastered versions","quote":"A comparison between these two versions was the eye opener for me.","text":"For the unbelievers, listen to Metallica's Death Magnetic first from the official version. After that, try the Guitar Hero version and it'll quite literally blow your mind. A video comparing both: [VIDEO] I tend to get physically and mentally exhausted when I have to listen to music that's maximised for loudness, took me a while to figure that out. A comparison between these two versions was the eye opener for me."},{"id":19113493,"author":"jcelerier","month":"2019-02","story":"They Don’t Make Music Like They Used To","stance":"reference","why":"Difference is clearly audible and even physically felt","quote":"enough that I can feel them just by the vibrations in my desk","text":".. whaaat ? the difference between the retail and GH3 parts here : [VIDEO] are enough that I can feel them just by the vibrations in my desk"},{"id":17816445,"author":"DiabloD3","month":"2018-08","story":"Is Pop Music Evolving, or Is It Just Getting Louder? (2012)","stance":"reference","why":"Compares retail and Guitar Hero mixes of a song","quote":"for a comparison on the least ruined song on the album","text":"Death Magnetic, released a decade ago. Famous for being so bad, Guitar Hero's development team asked for the original unmastered files, was granted their request, and then properly mastered them just so they could be included in the game without destroying the experience. Guitar Hero's version of Death Magnetic was apparently pirated more times than rips of the authentic release. It was heavily praised for being a more clear, detailed mix, that didn't shove highs under the bus for muddy lows and generally ruined vocals. [VIDEO] for a comparison on the least ruined song on the album, that the author volume matched for the video. The original release had to be decreased 14dB to match the level of the GH3 release for this comparison."}]},"A3oIiH7BLmg":{"wy":"Recommended in threads about culture and time orientation as an accessible presentation of Zimbardo's framework.","q":{"x":"another approach I found interesting is the one proposed by Philip Zimbardo neatly presented in this video","i":7840481,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":7840481,"author":"blaze33","month":"2014-06","story":"How Different Cultures Understand Time ","stance":"rec","why":"Zimbardo's past, present, future time orientations","quote":"another approach I found interesting is the one proposed by Philip Zimbardo neatly presented in this video","text":"Instead of the linear, multi-active and cyclic times paradigm, another approach I found interesting is the one proposed by Philip Zimbardo neatly presented in this video: [VIDEO] It presents individuals as being either past, present or future oriented. Apparently, the closer we are to the equator the more present-oriented we tend to be (because there are less seasonal changes)."},{"id":4912194,"author":"amirmc","month":"2012-12","story":"Facebook: Your Year in Review","stance":"rec","why":"Zimbardo on how people orient toward time","quote":"You might find this video interesting.","text":"> There, the culture is to live by the day, because winter never comes and they don't need to prepare for hard seasons You might find this video interesting. Philip Zimbardo on Time. [VIDEO]"},{"id":1407260,"author":"watmough","month":"2010-06","story":"What a toddler can tell you about the future of media","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"See also this video entitled 'The Secret Powers of Time', posted on twitter by Jeff Atwood.","text":"See also this video entitled 'The Secret Powers of Time', posted on twitter by Jeff Atwood. [VIDEO] I have to profess some interest in this, since my own 1-month old will likely grow up with the same iPhones, iPads etc., as the toddler in the article. The most worrying thing, is how the heck can kids raised on new media, Apps and books that talk, ever hope to even tolerate sitting in a classroom enjoying a traditional education? I dread to think of the overhaul that is needed and coming to education."},{"id":1423230,"author":"samratjp","month":"2010-06","story":"Under Pressure, Teachers Tamper With Test Scores","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Obligatory-long-yet-relevant-animation(?) on our educational system","text":"Obligatory-long-yet-relevant-animation(?) on our educational system: [VIDEO] When are we (loosely speaking) going to realize that we're not teaching in the right medium."}],"r":[["gKWNjFagR9k",1]]},"CS01Hmjv1pQ":{"wy":"HN points people to it as the short, accessible account of how Stuxnet was dissected, usually when Stuxnet or state-sponsored malware comes up.","q":{"x":"The Stuxnet TED talk [0] was really interesting","i":4033665,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":4033665,"author":"vecinu","month":"2012-05","story":"Flame: Massive cyber-attack discovered, researchers say","stance":"rec","why":"Really interesting Stuxnet dissection.","quote":"The Stuxnet TED talk [0] was really interesting","text":"I assume we are going to see a complicated and interesting dissection a la Stuxnet? The Stuxnet TED talk [0] was really interesting, I ended up giving a talk to my department at work afterwards. [0] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":2677618,"author":"phlux","month":"2011-06","story":"Stuxnet Virus Weapon Now Being Sold On The Black Market","stance":"rec","why":"TED talk dissecting Stuxnet.","quote":"Watch these in this order","text":"Watch these in this order: [LINK] - brief overview great animation [VIDEO] - ted talk on stux [LINK] detailed analysis of stuxnet the last one is long and Bruce appears to be an arrogant prick, but it is clear - cyber war has been here for a while."},{"id":2691438,"author":"phlux","month":"2011-06","story":"LulzSec releases trove of AZ law enforcement documents","stance":"rec","why":"TED talk dissecting Stuxnet.","quote":"Watch these in this order","text":"PSY-OP MOTHERFUCKER, CAN YOU HACK IT?! This is NOT script kiddies going at it, this is bio-stux.... See the following: Watch these in this order: [LINK] - brief overview great animation [VIDEO] - ted talk on stux [LINK] . detailed analysis of stuxnet the last one is long and Bruce appears to be an arrogant prick, but it is clear - cyber war has been here for a while. --- As I have mentioned, cyber war is here - and the legislation is lacking in its awareness - but becuase we have incompetant douches in charge of tech legislation, we will have physical war used as a metaphor to cyber warfare and thus have draconian influences from physical imprisonment being applied to digital situations. Utterly broken."},{"id":29425274,"author":"Terry_Roll","month":"2021-12","story":"Microsoft Windows RCE Vulnerability Gives an Attacker Complete Control","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"One other point not mentioned, is copying 4GiB of data across a network does take a bit of time, over the internet even longer. This link [LINK] is to a 2.9GB file so you can see how long it will take to download 4GiB over your internet connection. There is also the point on ASLR making life difficult, well if ASLR is switched on, there are a number of other options which may be also switched on in that part of the system, eg Control Flow Guard, DEP (been around for a couple of decades now), forced ASLR, bottoms up ASLR, High Entropy (more useful if you have shed loads of ram installed than minimum amounts), SEHOP ( [LINK] ) and Validate Heap integrity which are all on by default now a days in the systems I have seen. Then there is group policy where in Pro or above, you can control what programs run, in home you can add some of the group policy reg keys and get the same functionality when the functionality exists in the dll's. It reminds me of the bug where you could use notepad to pop a shell, but I'd be interested to see if this would still work with core isolation switched on, Defender Application Guard switched on to sandbox different apps, not just Edge. The number of installers for various apps which dont handle Ransomware Protection aka Controlled Folder Access being switched on, even some big names like Dell's Data Vault havent got that sorted yet, but I still think the best way to hack a system is the old way and get them to download and install an app which can run"},{"id":29428883,"author":"_kbh_","month":"2021-12","story":"Microsoft Windows RCE Vulnerability Gives an Attacker Complete Control","stance":"reference","why":"Background on Stuxnet attribution.","quote":"","text":"> Its worth reminding ourselves about Stuxnet, they still havent caught those people, but lots of assumptions exist. [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] I thought it was widely known that stuxnet is very likely if not certainly a join NSA and Israel operation."}],"r":[["rOwMW6agpTI",1]]},"RoGuvvzHY1A":{"wy":"HN points to it as an interesting real-world example of earth-sheltered living for staying cool or warm.","q":{"x":"That entire place is mind-bending.","i":47474666,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":47474666,"author":"pierrec","month":"2026-03","story":"Chest Fridge","stance":"rec","why":"Video of the home in action","quote":"That entire place is mind-bending.","text":"Now that is the coolest fridge I've ever seen. Found a video of it in action (yes, featuring the same dad joke all over the comments but that is not stopping me): [VIDEO] That entire place is mind-bending."},{"id":37208940,"author":"1letterunixname","month":"2023-08","story":"Ask HN: Possible to go/live underground to cool off?","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting underground home","quote":"The one by the people who flew with the geese is interesting.","text":"The one by the people who flew with the geese is interesting. [VIDEO] Edit: I'm thinking mostly underground is much cheaper and reduces the risks of wildfire, wind, hail, tornado, and lightning. / There's also the opal miners in Australia who built their whole town underground. [LINK]"},{"id":36504737,"author":"sacnoradhq","month":"2023-06","story":"The Race to Build a Better Air Conditioner","stance":"reference","why":"Example of semi-subterranean housing","quote":"Go semi-subterranean with heat pumps for unusual weather.","text":"The problem is poorly-designed housing. Go semi-subterranean with heat pumps for unusual weather. Example: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["-ZxPhDC-r3w",1],["HipTO_7mUOw",1]]},"V2P8mjWRqpk":{"wy":"Pointed to as a look at Hetzner's unusual semi-custom server hardware and cooling.","q":{"x":"The second is especially interesting as it demonstrates Hetzner's unique semi-custom hardware.","i":45970269,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45970269,"author":"jsheard","month":"2025-11","story":"A day at Hetzner Online in the Falkenstein data center","stance":"rec","why":"Demonstrates Hetzner's unique semi-custom hardware","quote":"The second is especially interesting as it demonstrates Hetzner's unique semi-custom hardware.","text":"Der8auer did some similar videos a few years ago, but in English: [LINK] [VIDEO] The second is especially interesting as it demonstrates Hetzner's unique semi-custom hardware."},{"id":39441951,"author":"jsheard","month":"2024-02","story":"Hetzner GPU Server","stance":"rec","why":"Custom stripped-down motherboards arranged for rack airflow","quote":"There's some videos out there showing off their custom motherboards","text":"There's some videos out there showing off their custom motherboards, it's an odd mixture of standard-ish consumer ATX but stripped down to the absolute bare essentials and with some components rearranged for better airflow in racks chassis they also build themselves: [VIDEO] Another video showing the custom racks and other infrastructure: [LINK]"},{"id":39443698,"author":"dist-epoch","month":"2024-02","story":"Hetzner GPU Server","stance":"reference","why":"ASUS AM5 boards with rotated CPU and memory block for airflow","quote":"Hetzner is buying ASUS consumer AM5 motherboards, but with the whole CPU + memory block rotated","text":"Hetzner is buying ASUS consumer AM5 motherboards, but with the whole CPU + memory block rotated so that the memory sticks are horizontal instead of vertical for better airflow: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36813024,"author":"thejosh","month":"2023-07","story":"Fly.io Postgres cluster down for 3 days, no word from them about it","stance":"reference","why":"Hetzner's custom AM5 hardware and cooling","quote":"Hetzner also do some crazy-cool stuff, especially around the 7950X3D, cooling, AM5 etc.","text":"Hetzner also do some crazy-cool stuff, especially around the 7950X3D, cooling, AM5 etc. ( [VIDEO] ). They also do some amazing stuff with ARM (their cloud offering is really solid for this)."}],"r":[["5eo8nz_niiM",1],["gWVmPtr9O0g",1],["7ttG90raCNo",1],["jB0vBmiTr6o",1]]},"XehNK7UpZsc":{"wy":"HN recommends it for its argument that single-player campaigns and modding are overlooked in RTS design.","q":{"x":"makes a compelling case for why single-player campaign and mod-development experiences are often overlooked","i":37880311,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":39131313,"author":"ixwt","month":"2024-01","story":"Microsoft lays off 1,900 Activision Blizzard and Xbox employees","stance":"rec","why":"Argues next RTS should target casual players who love story","quote":"GiantGrantGames on Youtube made an interesting video about","text":"GiantGrantGames on Youtube made an interesting video about \"The Next Major RTS Will Fail. This is Why\"[0]. Turns out he got a lot of this information because he was one of the players that had a huge influence in the development, and this is what ZeroSpace focused their development efforts on. One of which, is targeting the casual audience, and their love for a good story. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37880311,"author":"btown","month":"2023-10","story":"Microsoft $69B deal to buy Call of Duty maker Activision Blizzard cleared by UK","stance":"rec","why":"Why campaigns and modding matter for RTS crossover success","quote":"makes a compelling case for why single-player campaign and mod-development experiences are often overlooked","text":"Giant Grant Games makes a compelling case for why single-player campaign and mod-development experiences are often overlooked but are key for RTS crossover success. And this came out long before the success of BG3. I hold out hope that someone will crack the formula! [VIDEO]"},{"id":39520282,"author":"shagie","month":"2024-02","story":"Cactus – A Modern Diablo II Version Switcher, Character Isolator, & Mod Manager","stance":"reference","why":"Found while browsing, relates to solo content decline","quote":"In my stumbling around YouTube in the past week, I found","text":"In my stumbling around YouTube in the past week, I found \"The Next Major RTS Will Fail. This Is Why\" - [VIDEO] The publishers have listened to their hard core players - and created games for them. Is Diablo III for the people doing seasons? or the people who play through the campaign in solo mode? With modern games, this in turn means that the casual to competitive conversion rate is low (because of the lack of solo content to hook the casual players) and publishers look to milk more from the competitive players by making the game even less accessible. The games are being developed as multiplayer first today... and that in turn means that the solo modes that the casual games aren't there. Honestly, if MMO is one of the first things listed on a game, and solo content is lacking - that gets a \"nope\" or \"meh\" from me on Steam. Meanwhile, some fun old games I still pull up from time to time to play."}],"r":[["Qct6LKbneKQ",1],["7JUs4c99-mo",1],["5TFDG-y-EHs",1],["MiUHjLxm3V0",1],["fU9hR3kiOK0",1],["UQuIVsNzqDk",1],["ML4tw_UzqZE",1],["0bcZb-SsnrA",1],["dKx1wnXClcI",1],["jvPPXbo87ds",1]]},"cQoQE_HDG8g":{"wy":"Whenever Wayland versus X comes up, HN points to this talk as the authoritative explanation from an X maintainer of why X was replaced.","q":{"x":"a great presentation by one of the guys behind wayland, who worked on X a lot before","i":26421574,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":30244099,"author":"kaba0","month":"2022-02","story":"Less Than 10% of Firefox Users on Linux Are Running Wayland","stance":"rec","why":"X maintainer explains why Wayland drops the middle man","quote":"Please have a look at an X maintainers very own presentation","text":"Please have a look at an X maintainers very own presentation: [VIDEO] Wayland is not a middle man as it is the compositor itself as well - it does the minimal amount of work necessary to compose the desktop. X on the other hand just transfers messages between clients and the compositor without doing any actual work (x render functions are almost never used by modern applications)"},{"id":26418630,"author":"tux1968","month":"2021-03","story":"Guide: Full Wayland Setup for Linux","stance":"rec","why":"Why Wayland replaced the old X codebase and architecture","quote":"there's a decent video from back in 2013 about it.","text":"I tried to give a hint about the basic reason of trying to get out from under the weight of a huge code base that had become old and crufty while the very architecture it was designed around was becoming moot since things were shifting to client side rendering already. Couldn't find a good article on short notice, but there's a decent video from back in 2013 about it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26421574,"author":"kaba0","month":"2021-03","story":"Guide: Full Wayland Setup for Linux","stance":"rec","why":"Wayland developer explains X's misaligned hardware abstraction","quote":"a great presentation by one of the guys behind wayland, who worked on X a lot before","text":"Here you are: [VIDEO] It’s a great presentation by one of the guys behind wayland, who worked on X a lot before. Basically, X has a fundamentally misaligned abstraction of the underlying hardware - which is expected based on its age. When used with a compositor, it is basically a middle-man with no function whatsoever. So Wayland decided to cut out the middle man and pass events directly between client and compositor. But please watch the video, I think it will answer all your questions. Also, it’s not like X will be totally deprecated, XWayland is an API implementation of it that will be supported forever."},{"id":38452715,"author":"dralley","month":"2023-11","story":"Red Hat Enterprise Linux 10 Plans for Wayland and Xorg Server","stance":"reference","why":"Decade-old talk on Wayland's motivation","quote":"Here's a decade-old talk on Wayland","text":"Here's a decade-old talk on Wayland [VIDEO] Wayland has been the default on Fedora and Arch w/ GNOME since around 2018, 5 years ago. It became the default on Debian w/ GNOME in 2019, 4 years ago. So yes, long time coming. KDE took longer but it's now finally ready."},{"id":21624885,"author":"cyphar","month":"2019-11","story":"Are We Wayland Yet?","stance":"reference","why":"Wayland developers explain problems caused by the Xorg design","quote":"the Xorg spec is also a major cause of problems with modern Linux graphics[1]","text":"One of the arguments made by the Wayland spec folks (many of whom were involved in Xorg development for many years) is that the Xorg spec is also a major cause of problems with modern Linux graphics[1]. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13199320,"author":"cyphar","month":"2016-12","story":"Fedora 25: With Wayland, Linux has never been easier (or more handsome)","stance":"reference","why":"X is not truly network transparent anymore","quote":"","text":"> However it's the lack of network transparency which stops me from using Wayland in the foreseeable future. X is not network transparent, and hasn't been for a long time. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["uxykI30fS54",1],["UiS27feX8o0",1],["p3lsYlod5OU",1],["iSVHp6CAyQ8",1],["GWQh_DmDLKQ",1],["_ahvzDzKdB0",1]]},"ispyUPqqL1c":{"wy":"HN users cite it as a data-rich look at falling fertility that points to declining coupling rather than people choosing fewer kids.","q":{"x":"He makes the argument that declining birthrates are due more to a fall in coupling than a fall in people in relationships choosing to have kids.","i":46962702,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":46962702,"author":"ndiddy","month":"2026-02","story":"The US is flirting with its first-ever population decline","stance":"rec","why":"Argues falling birthrates stem from fewer couples, correlated with mobile internet use.","quote":"He makes the argument that declining birthrates are due more to a fall in coupling than a fall in people in relationships choosing to have kids.","text":"I watched this video a while ago that said something similar. [VIDEO] The decline in birthrates isn't related to growing living standards, as poorer countries also have declining birthrates. Turkey has a lower birthrate than the UK, and Mexico has a lower birthrate than the US. Places like North Africa and South India have seen declines in birthrates comparable to the West. He makes the argument that declining birthrates are due more to a fall in coupling than a fall in people in relationships choosing to have kids. He brings up that birthrates would actually be increasing if marriage rates remained constant. This means that all the incentives countries push such as subsidized childcare or tax breaks to have kids are putting the cart before the horse, as a growing share of young people don't have a partner to have kids with to begin with. He then brings up that the fall in coupling a country experiences is roughly correlated to the rate of mobile internet usage in that country. 46% of American teens say they use social media \"almost constantly\" vs. 24% a decade ago. People would rather use social media than go out and meet others. He points to South Asia as an example, as it's experienced a relatively smaller decline in marriage rates, and mobile internet usage there is lower than in the rest of the world. I suppose it's yet another way that cell phones are impacting society."},{"id":46410189,"author":"derriz","month":"2025-12","story":"How we lost communication to entertainment","stance":"rec","why":"Reaches similar conclusion on population decline with many graphs and facts.","quote":"includes lots of graphs and other interesting factlets","text":"Patrick Boyle eventually comes to a similar conclusion in his video about global population decline - [VIDEO] - but includes lots of graphs and other interesting factlets. * warning for Americans: not suitable for those offended by sarcasm"},{"id":43673348,"author":"pfdietz","month":"2025-04","story":"Half the men in Seattle are never-married singles, census data shows","stance":"reference","why":"Concludes fewer couples forming is a major cause of falling fertility.","quote":"Patrick Boyle has looked at causes of decline in the total fertility rate around the world","text":"Patrick Boyle has looked at causes of decline in the total fertility rate around the world and concluded reduction in the formation of couples (married or not) is a major cause. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["wo84LFzx5nI",1],["YcUycQoz0zg",1],["3ZTGwcHQfLY",1]]},"qSfdtmcZ4d0":{"wy":"Cited as the source for Sapper's end and SvelteKit's origin, and as an early view of where JS frameworks were heading.","q":{"x":"I really do believe this is the next phase of how JS web frameworks will work in the future.","i":26560340,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":26558766,"author":"qbasic_forever","month":"2021-03","story":"SvelteKit Is in Public Beta","stance":"rec","why":"SveltKit alpha introduction and its evolution from Sapper.","quote":"first posts and videos of sveltekit when he introduced the alpha version","text":"Check out the creator's first posts and videos of sveltekit when he introduced the alpha version: [VIDEO] The way I understand it is it's an evolution of sapper to be more versatile, especially with respect to the backend. Out of the box it can work more easily with serverless providers that give you static file hosting + some kind of basic API gateway and serverless function platform. So you can write full stack client and server-side code all using the same framework (like you can with sapper). But the key difference from sapper is that you're not stuck using a node.js backend, with sveltekit you can swap in different backend implementations, like one targeting a serverless/lambda system."},{"id":26560340,"author":"alexandargyurov","month":"2021-03","story":"SvelteKit Is in Public Beta","stance":"rec","why":"Rich Harris's early talk on the future of JS web frameworks.","quote":"I really do believe this is the next phase of how JS web frameworks will work in the future.","text":"Rich Harris did a great talk[0] at an early stage of SvelteKit's development called \"Futuristic Web Development\". I really do believe this is the next phase of how JS web frameworks will work in the future. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25075686,"author":"dmitriid","month":"2020-11","story":"Angular 11","stance":"rec","why":"Conference talk on the future of Svelte.","quote":"this video from recent conference on the future of Svelte","text":"In addition to links in sibling comments, there's the official Svelte blog with regular updates: [LINK] and this video from recent conference on the future of Svelte: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29911161,"author":"Glench","month":"2022-01","story":"SvelteKit","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on Svelte and future web development.","quote":"","text":"I don't feel like this page really does SvelteKit justice. SvelteKit is kind of like Next.js but taken to the next level. I know people using less mainstream frameworks tend to be a bit overzealous in their advocacy, but I've been using SvelteKit a bunch and I love it more than any framework I've ever used: - All the component/JS/CSS/HTML/endpoint plumbing is taken care of and optimized for you with no configuration. Including code-splitting per route! You can get up and running in seconds. - Can use the same JS/TS on the front and backends. This was always my issue with Django and Rails. - Server-rendered for performance and SEO with client-side hydration of components. - Optional client-side routing for performance. - Filesystem-based routes are so easy to use and maintain. Want to make a new route? Just make a new file in the /routes folder! Want to modify an existing route but don't know where to look? Just look at the url and it's the same as the folder structure! It's always annoying to have to hunt around to find code. - Pre-rendering aka static page generation per route! This means you can run a static marketing site and the dynamic app off the same stack. This is something I've always run into and never felt like I had a good experience with. - I don't know how they did it but the dev experience is blazing fast with instant hot module reloading. - Deploy to any static, serverless, or server host. You can make your own \"adapter\" for any platforms that aren't already s"},{"id":26573857,"author":"alfonsodev","month":"2021-03","story":"Comparing Svelte and React","stance":"reference","why":"Rich Harris talk on where Svelte is heading next.","quote":"Here is the video I had in my bookmarks for a while","text":"I think many people adopted React because Facebook was behind it, and using it in production, the ecosystem is huge, there is a react- package for everything you can image, there is also react-native, I don't like it, but there it is, you can reuse the knowledge to build native mobile apps. Now Svelte, I haven't been following, but I think the creators are working in what's going to be next and even better framework[1], to your point of things in Svelte not changing as much as in React, maybe it just a matter of time. Here is the video I had in my bookmarks for a while, I still couldn't make the time to watch, so I hope my comment is not too far from reality. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24887056,"author":"tobr","month":"2020-10","story":"The Rome Toolchain: A linter, compiler, bundler, and more","stance":"reference","why":"Hints Sapper will be replaced by built-in file-based routing.","quote":"Sapper in particular is probably not the future","text":"As an aside, Sapper in particular is probably not the future, as there’s been an unofficial announcement[1] that it will be replaced with file-based routing built in to the Svelte app template. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24909118,"author":"pier25","month":"2020-10","story":"Next.js 10","stance":"reference","why":"Rich Harris announces Sapper's end and SvelteKit.","quote":"Sapper is dead btw. Rich said it recently in a video.","text":"And... Sapper is dead btw. Rich said it recently in a video. The Svelte team is working on the next big thing combining Svelte with Snowpack. The project is called SvelteKit. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24911110,"author":"atom_arranger","month":"2020-10","story":"Next.js 10","stance":"reference","why":"Svelte heading in a new direction, no Sapper 1.0.","quote":"Sapper is not getting a 1.0.0.","text":"Relatively recent news, Sapper is not getting a 1.0.0. Svelte is going in a different direction in the future. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["5IUj1EZwpJY",1],["2u6HTG8LuXQ",1],["agZ0xISi40E",1],["5cWck_xcH64",1],["KqFNbCcyFkk",1],["hxM8QmyZXtg",1],["r9rGX91rq5I",1],["MZvmYaFkNJI",1],["Z1PCtIaM_GQ",1],["J7GY1Xg6X20",1],["j6VlT_jUVPc",1]]},"rG_Eh0J_4_s":{"wy":"Recommended as the introductory explainer whenever rotating detonation engines show up in the news.","q":{"x":"Scott Manley has an introductory video about RDEs","i":28341361,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":40945575,"author":"class3shock","month":"2024-07","story":"GE Aerospace Successfully Develops and Tests New Hypersonic Dual-Mode Ramjet","stance":"rec","why":"Cool explainer on rotating detonation engines.","quote":"Cool video from Scott Manly talking about RDE's","text":"Another similar thing from the end of last year: [LINK] They really seem to love to talk about how fast they move except wait: [LINK] Per the above in 2019 they had been developing RDE technology for 5 years. Now developing the tech is different then building a prototype but it's good to take these timetable numbers with a large dose of salt. It is interesting to see RDE's getting alot of headlines recently. Really cool tech that works off of a principle known for a long time but only just recently hitting an inflection point in terms of development. Cool video from Scott Manly talking about RDE's: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28341361,"author":"chrispeel","month":"2021-08","story":"Japan tests a rotary detonation engine in space, big increase in fuel efficiency","stance":"rec","why":"Introductory video about rotating detonation engines.","quote":"Scott Manley has an introductory video about RDEs","text":"Would an toroidal aerospike work with rotary detonation? :-) What sort of nozzles can one use with an RDE? I see that one can have multiple rotating pulses in an RDE [1]; I guess with some work the engine designer can control the number of pulses, is that right? Are there performance tradeoffs when deciding how many pulses to employ? What are they? If employing multiple pulses, does one want them rotating the same direction, or opposite directions? BTW, Scott Manley has an introductory video about RDEs [2]. [1] 5:50 in [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":33047965,"author":"zeristor","month":"2022-10","story":"Japan tests explosion-powered rocket for the first time in space (2021)","stance":"reference","why":"RDEs are about 25% more efficient.","quote":"Scott Manley did a video 2 years ago on rotating detonation gun engines, them being 25% more efficient.","text":"Scott Manley did a video 2 years ago on rotating detonation gun engines, them being 25% more efficient. More bang for your buck? [VIDEO] I almost did mechanical engineering, in my placement before starting a degree I didn’t start I was working with detonation guns to fire tungsten carbide into machined parts to make them last far longer in nuclear power stations. That had finished, just work on jet engine blades."}],"r":[["C4VHfmiwuv4",1]]},"tQpqsmwlfY0":{"wy":"Cited again and again as the concrete case study behind the claim that NASA got a 15,000x speedup moving from Simulink to Julia.","q":{"x":"From Simulink to Julia, his code went from 1000 lines to 50 lines and ran 15,000x faster.","i":32773248,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":34477967,"author":"eggy","month":"2023-01","story":"GNU Octave","stance":"rec","why":"Interactive exploration with 500 Monte Carlo simulations in under 60ms","quote":"At 14:00, the interactive slider exploration due to the speed is amazing.","text":"<<was given at a SIAM conference and the recording can be found here: [VIDEO] >> At 14:00, the interactive slider exploration due to the speed is amazing. Over 500 Monte Carlo simulations in less than 60ms."},{"id":32773248,"author":"NicolasL-S","month":"2022-09","story":"From Common Lisp to Julia","stance":"rec","why":"Case study migrating Simulink to Julia at NASA, far shorter and faster","quote":"From Simulink to Julia, his code went from 1000 lines to 50 lines and ran 15,000x faster.","text":"You should check out this case study of migrating Simulink to Julia at NASA: [VIDEO] From Simulink to Julia, his code went from 1000 lines to 50 lines and ran 15,000x faster."},{"id":35782288,"author":"ChrisRackauckas","month":"2023-05","story":"Can Fortran survive another 15 years?","stance":"reference","why":"Primary source of the 15,000x claim by a NASA engineer","quote":"You did watch the whole video to understand the application and the caveats etc.","text":"> Definitely 15000 times faster than MATLAB as claimed by JuliaComputing can be achieved with such seemingly concrete and realistic benchmarks. I believe you're talking about NASA Launch Services engineers claiming Julia's ModelingToolkit simulations outperformed Simulink by 15,000x? That claim was of course not made by Julia Computing or anyone affiliated by Julia Computing, which is pretty clear because the person who makes the claim very clearly describes his affiliation at the beginning of the video. The source is here: [VIDEO] , at 12:55. You did watch the whole video to understand the application and the caveats etc. instead of just reading the headline and immediately coming to a conclusion, right?"},{"id":35894095,"author":"NicolasL-S","month":"2023-05","story":"Julia 1.9 Highlights","stance":"reference","why":"NASA engineer got 15,000x speedup and 1000 lines down to 50","quote":"This NASA engineer switched from Simulink to SciML and obtained a 15,000x speed up while his code went from 1000 lines to 50 lines long","text":"I would be cautious saying Simulink has no Julia equivalent. This NASA engineer switched from Simulink to SciML and obtained a 15,000x speed up while his code went from 1000 lines to 50 lines long: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34470675,"author":"ChrisRackauckas","month":"2023-01","story":"GNU Octave","stance":"reference","why":"SIAM conference recording of a user showing 15,000x acceleration","quote":"A showcase from one of the library's users, demonstrating a 15,000x acceleration, was given at a SIAM conference","text":"ModelingToolkit.jl, a comparison is given here: [LINK] . A showcase from one of the library's users, demonstrating a 15,000x acceleration, was given at a SIAM conference and the recording can be found here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28949711,"author":"ChrisRackauckas","month":"2021-10","story":"Composability in Julia: Implementing Deep Equilibrium Models via Neural ODEs","stance":"reference","why":"NASA's 15,000x acceleration as example of Julia's high-profile users","quote":"NASA showing a 15,000x acceleration","text":"Lots to say here. First of all, the community growth has been pretty tremendous and I couldn't really ask for more. We're seeing tens of thousands of visitors to the documentation of various packages, and have some high profile users. For example, NASA showing a 15,000x acceleration ( [VIDEO] ) and the Head of Clinical Pharmacology at Moderna saying SciML-based Pumas \"has emerged as our 'go-to' tool for most of our analyses in recent months\" in 2020 (see [LINK] ). We try to keep a showcase ( [LINK] ) but at this point it's hard to stay on top of the growth. I think anyone would be excited to see an open source side project reach that level of use. Since we tend to focus on core numerical issues (stiffness) and performance, we target the more \"hardcore\" people in scientific disciplines who really need these aspects and those communities are the ones seeing the most adoption (pharmacology, systems biology, combustion modeling, etc.). Indeed the undergrad classes using a non-stiff ODE solver on small ODEs or training a neural ODE on MNIST don't really have those issues so they aren't our major growth areas. That's okay and that's probably the last group that would move. In terms of the developer team, throughout the SciML organization repositories we have had around 30 people who have had over 100 commits, which is similar in number to NumPy and SciPy. Julia naturally has a much lower barrier to entry in terms of committing to such packages (since the packages are all in Julia r"},{"id":28525233,"author":"snicker7","month":"2021-09","story":"Fortran Web Framework","stance":"reference","why":"NASA modeling spacecraft dynamics with ModelingToolkit, 15,000x faster than Simulink","quote":"NASA switched from SIMULINK to ModelingToolkit (Julia) for modeling spacecraft dynamics [0].","text":"NASA switched from SIMULINK to ModelingToolkit (Julia) for modeling spacecraft dynamics [0]. They found that the latter was much easier and gave a 15,000x (!!!) performance improvement. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28066585,"author":"amkkma","month":"2021-08","story":"Growing open source from Torch to PyTorch","stance":"reference","why":"NASA example of Julia adoption","quote":"","text":"Sure! NASA: [VIDEO] Moderna: [LINK] [LINK] There are many many more. These unique and sought after capability are what got Julia Computing its 24 mil series A ( [LINK] )"},{"id":27888295,"author":"snicker7","month":"2021-07","story":"Julia Computing raises $24M Series A","stance":"reference","why":"NASA moved from Simulink to Julia for a 15,000x speedup","quote":"NASA switched from MATLAB (Simulink) to Julia and got a 15,000x performance boost.","text":"For satellite seperation dynamics modeling, NASA switched from MATLAB (Simulink) to Julia and got a 15,000x performance boost. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["lMcRBdSdO_U",1],["aNR4n0i2ZlM",1],["z8MVKianh54",1]]},"vZ_a21fPkYM":{"wy":"Linked in Reaction Engines threads as the story behind the engine, though one commenter is skeptical of the company's funding record.","q":{"x":"Here's a documentary that was broadcast on BBC4 a couple of months ago about Bond and his quixotic journey to get HOTOL/Skylon into the sky","i":4843078,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":9872041,"author":"curiousjorge","month":"2015-07","story":"Reaction Engines Reveals Secret of Sabre Frost Control Technology","stance":"rec","why":"Inspiring look at the Skylon engine project.","quote":"Very inspiring stuff.","text":"I think this will be the single biggest revolution since aviation history. It would change the way we travel, send things up to space. Of course, military consequences will be interesting as well. I wonder if US military already has something like this in the works. I'd hate it to see it fall into the wrong type of people. Very inspiring stuff. [VIDEO]"},{"id":4843078,"author":"peterstjohn","month":"2012-11","story":"British aerospace company claims biggest engine advance since the jet","stance":"rec","why":"BBC documentary on Bond's quest for HOTOL/Skylon with a heat-exchanger test.","quote":"Here's a documentary that was broadcast on BBC4 a couple of months ago about Bond and his quixotic journey to get HOTOL/Skylon into the sky","text":"Here's a documentary that was broadcast on BBC4 a couple of months ago about Bond and his quixotic journey to get HOTOL/Skylon into the sky: [VIDEO] It includes a test-firing of the new heat-exchanger at the end."},{"id":4917524,"author":"aspratley","month":"2012-12","story":"Oxygen-Powered Jet Travels The World In 4 Hours","stance":"reference","why":"Documentary on the engine's developers.","quote":"There's also a documentary on the developers","text":"This has a far better explanation of what the engine is about: [LINK] There's also a documentary on the developers: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4843916,"author":"rfugger","month":"2012-11","story":"British aerospace company claims biggest engine advance since the jet","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for the story of classified work blocking the engineers' patent.","quote":"According to this BBC Four documentary (found in another comment)","text":"According to this BBC Four documentary (found in another comment) [VIDEO] the engineers wanted to apply for a patent once the British government stopped funding their work, but the government classified their work as secret, preventing them from doing so. EDITED TO ADD: Apparently the original secret classification expired in 1993, at which point they filed a patent which ended up being owned by Rolls Royce. Their current work circumvents that patent."},{"id":9872106,"author":"Theodores","month":"2015-07","story":"Reaction Engines Reveals Secret of Sabre Frost Control Technology","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"These guys have been begging for funding and making the same 'design studies' for decades","text":"This project is a bit like the British 'Bloodhound' land speed record attempt in that there is a bit of 'Duke Nukem' about it, i.e. it is never going to happen! Very 'British' though, with the usual cause for failure, e.g. short term return on investment demands of the city, the uselessness of the government for backing 'British genius' and so on. Anyone not sceptical is advised to watch the BBC documentary 'The Three Rocketeers': [VIDEO] These guys have been begging for funding and making the same 'design studies' for decades, the fact that British Aerospace 'BAE SYSTEMS' have disowned the project is quite telling, it is not anywhere near being tangible and it never will be. The only lesson to be learned here is how not to run your start up!!!"}]},"Gr7T07WfIhM":{"wy":"HN points to it for its account of propaganda that sows distrust and confusion, and as a starting point for Adam Curtis's work.","q":{"x":"Adam Curtis is great, definitely check out his other work.","i":49221744,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":49221744,"author":"adwi","month":"2026-08","story":"The USSR in 100 Photographs","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Good starting point for Adam Curtis's work","quote":"Adam Curtis is great, definitely check out his other work.","text":"Adam Curtis is great, definitely check out his other work. Hypernormalisation is a good place to start: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42902264,"author":"zackmorris","month":"2025-02","story":"The Strike Is Coming","stance":"reference","why":"Commenter hasn't watched it yet but cites it","quote":"I only learned of this documentary this morning, so have yet to watch it","text":"We should be mindful that HN doesn't have an unflag button. The post shouldn't be flagged, so flagging it acts on behalf of the status quo. Let the flagging stand as a testament to collaboration. We already experienced a general strike during the COVID-19 pandemic. Rather than breaking billionaires and multinational corporations, it made them stronger. So withholding our labor is part of the equation, but not all of it. We're moving into hypernormalization: [LINK] I only learned of this documentary this morning, so have yet to watch it: [VIDEO] Hypernormalisation | Full Documentary | Adam Curtis Within a corporation, one person at the top dictates all behavior in exchange for monetary compensation, meaning that it's the job of each subordinate to shield superiors from work or anything that's against the interests of the corporation. Yes-men are rewarded handsomely while objectors are laid off. Over time, corporations become akin to feudal states, vying for control of the kingdom. The wealthiest/most powerful person performs the role of king and dictates each corporation's behavior in exchange for that corporation's right to exist. What this means is that corporate strategies such as running government like a business are eroding democracy globally. Where is this all going? Labor compensation diverged from productivity in the 1970s when global industrial capacity and energy production could no longer keep pace with the rising cost of the Cold War - aka the guns vs butter model"},{"id":39405648,"author":"walrus01","month":"2024-02","story":"Pro CCP 'spamouflage' network pivoting to focus on US election","stance":"reference","why":"Part on Surkov and propaganda sowing distrust","quote":"Vladislav Surkov and propaganda methods of making people distrust everything and sow strife and confusion","text":"See also, the part from 2:22:53 onwards: Vladislav Surkov and propaganda methods of making people distrust everything and sow strife and confusion. [VIDEO] [LINK]"}],"r":[["X1XoCQm5JSQ",1]]},"i7kh8pNRWOo":{"wy":"HN cites it as the best motivation advice some commenters have heard, and it is often shared in procrastination threads.","q":{"x":"Best advice I heard was from Jonathan Blow","i":39481424,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39481424,"author":"jmacklin308","month":"2024-02","story":"Ask HN: Does procrastination advice help you?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best advice on motivation the commenter has heard.","quote":"Best advice I heard was from Jonathan Blow","text":"Best advice I heard was from Jonathan Blow: [VIDEO] And Dr. Huberman: [LINK] Everything else didn't have enough data for me to latch onto. Life is difficult, and some tasks are not meant to be fun. You can't fix that. You can however reframe your minds' perspective on the manner. Instead of thinking \"Man there are so many dishes to do, I'd rather be playing Helldivers 2 now\" you can think \"I can do 5 dishes... That was easy, I can do 5 more dishes... Look how many dishes i did! I can do 5 more\". Even if it's clearly a lie to yourself that you enjoy it, you'll find that your brain will start to believe it. Over time, your brain will take on the new perspective. This is why \"faking it until you make it\" works."},{"id":30943929,"author":"loevborg","month":"2022-04","story":"Thoughts on the Witness (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Blow talks about depression.","quote":"He's also an interesting speaker. Here he talks about depression","text":"In my view, The Witness is the best video game of all times. On the surface, it's a puzzle game. I bought it (for something ridiculous like €5 on the iPad I believe) expecting to enjoy a few logic puzzles and remembering the games of my childhood like Myst and The 7th Guest (and I was also happy that, unlike many games it wasn't about shooting people). But it ended up a much bigger deal than that. I found The Witness (which doesn't have dialog or a plot) immersive like no other game and I think about it every week, years after completing the game. If it's not about the puzzles, what is it about? To avoid spoilers, here are just a few hints: • The Witness is a game about learning and perception. And about meditation. (That's only my interpretation, though; like any work of art, you may have another valid interpretation). • The author, Jonathan Blow, is a \"programmer's programmer\", who built his own game engine for The Witness and is in the process of writing his own programming language for his next project. He's also an interesting speaker. Here he talks about depression: [VIDEO] • The Witness is as close to a video game equivalent of \"film d'auteur\" as you can get (at least compared to most of the other games out there, which are commercial Hollywood productions) (Disclaimer: I'm not a gamer, and haven't played more than 2-3 games in the last decade.)"},{"id":19492909,"author":"vfinn","month":"2019-03","story":"A guide to difficult conversations","stance":"reference","why":"Blow on observing and understanding negative emotions and thoughts.","quote":"","text":"It seems his teachings are based on the teachings of Jiddu Krishnamurti. There are some good audio books by him on Youtube, like Freedom from the Known . On a slightly related note, Jonathan Blow also has a talk called Techniques for dealing with lack of motivation, malaise, depression ( [VIDEO] ), where he also talks about the importance of observational skills as a means of understanding / \"putting out\" your (negative) emotions and thoughts."},{"id":18099357,"author":"modernerd","month":"2018-09","story":"Valley Forged: How One Man Made the Indie Video Game Sensation Stardew Valley","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> in four decades of living on this planet I don't think I've ever come across anything that fits that bill. Keep looking! But also, it's possible at 40+ that you may have already found it. Perhaps you've not given that thing the chance it needed for you to fall in love with it — the days and weeks of exploration and the obsessive iteration mentioned in the GQ article that it takes to grok something and absorb it until it feels like it's yours . I'm almost your age and now rewinding my career path. I kept chasing different tech career paths hoping to find something that “fit the bill”, which only showed me that nothing was as interesting to me than what I started my career doing (game design/animation). It took a couple of changes to realise that: - Reflecting on how I spend my free time and time on social media, and the speakers and personalities I'm fascinated by and naturally drawn to. (They are mostly game designers and devs.) - Looking back at the work in the past that has made me happiest and most fulfilled. From there it was a case of having the discipline to commit to it — I spend most mornings and evenings skilling up and having fun with game design and development, although my day job is still in front-end right now. On the motivation side of things you mentioned, I don't think it's a case of “find the right thing and you'll find your motivation.” It's more a case of discipline, projecting your self now to your future self, and knowing a few psychological techniques"},{"id":15416185,"author":"jsheard","month":"2017-10","story":"Techniques for dealing with lack of motivation, malaise, depression [video]","stance":"reference","why":"YouTube mirror of the Twitch talk, avoiding buffering problems.","quote":"Here's the YouTube mirror, since Twitch VODs often have janky buffering problems in my experience","text":"Here's the YouTube mirror, since Twitch VODs often have janky buffering problems in my experience: [VIDEO] + [LINK]"}]},"iph500cPK28":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as the more rigorous, polite and complete treatment of the Veritasium puzzle, though one dissents that the original misleads people.","q":{"x":"Let me highly recommend Mehdi Sadaghdar’s (ElectroBOOM) excellent response video","i":29599831,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29599831,"author":"jacobolus","month":"2021-12","story":"I bought 1000 meters of wire to settle a physics debate [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Polite, empirical, entertaining response explaining the subtleties of the lamp puzzle.","quote":"Let me highly recommend Mehdi Sadaghdar’s (ElectroBOOM) excellent response video","text":"Controversial is fine to the extent the controversy shows people struggling toward clearer understanding and better explanations, and as long as all parties are operating in good faith (i.e. not being deliberately misleading). Let me highly recommend Mehdi Sadaghdar’s (ElectroBOOM) excellent response video [VIDEO] which does a great job of empirically investigating and theoretically explaining the subtleties involved, in a polite, respectful, and entertaining way. I have similarly enjoyed the exchange between Sadaghdar and Steve Mould about the physics of the “chain fountain”. These kinds of friendly scientific “debates” show viewers (e.g. kids) a bit of how the scientific process and scientific discourse works, in a form that is more accessible and digestible than technical journal papers or history books. Having a discussion back and forth helps to improve both viewers’ specific knowledge and viewers’ processes for comprehending and interrogating new information, so long as the median viewer actually sees some of the responses. (Someone who only ever saw the first Veritasium video probably ends up with a somewhat wrong mental model.)"},{"id":29600418,"author":"afiori","month":"2021-12","story":"I bought 1000 meters of wire to settle a physics debate [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Corrects mistakes in the Veritasium video.","quote":"which I think ElectroBoom's response* addresses nicely","text":"In my opinion the video made a couple of mistakes, which I think ElectroBoom's response* addresses nicely. Also its proof using the pointing vector to show the flow of energy is misleading; In the video Derek shows that the energy flows toward the lamp when the circuit is already in a stable configuration, but the point of the video is to explore what happens before the circuit reaches that configuration. This is relevant: assume that the right half of the cable is connected to the positive pole of the battery, in a stable configuration every point of the right half of the circuit has a positive charge (creating the poynting vector field as shown in the video) while 1/c seconds after the switch was closed the wire just to the right of the lightbulb will have a negative charge (the cable near it is positively charged from the battery and so the upper cable acts as a the negative half of a capacitor) which changes the direction of the poynting vector. In the end it felt like it was telling you that its message was that energy travels via the fields but what it actually said what that switches cause electric interference. * [VIDEO]"},{"id":29601345,"author":"naasking","month":"2021-12","story":"I bought 1000 meters of wire to settle a physics debate [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Full details from an electrical engineer.","quote":"You should watch Electroboom's analysis if you want the full details from an electrical engineer","text":"You should watch Electroboom's analysis if you want the full details from an electrical engineer: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29672707,"author":"haxiomic","month":"2021-12","story":"Electricity Is Magnetism","stance":"rec","why":"Clearer response after the confusing Veritasium video.","quote":"ElectroBOOM has a good response","text":"The veritasium video didn’t create a clear picture of what’s going on and you shouldn’t feel bad for feeling more confused after watching it ElectroBOOM has a good response [VIDEO]"},{"id":29923567,"author":"aemreunal","month":"2022-01","story":"5.5 mm in 1.25 nanoseconds","stance":"reference","why":"Popular response video that Veritasium also answered.","quote":"this was a popular response video by ElectroBOOM that Veritasium also responded to in the comments","text":"Disclaimer: I don't understand nearly enough about the physics of electricity, but this was a popular response video by ElectroBOOM that Veritasium also responded to in the comments: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30064975,"author":"tzs","month":"2022-01","story":"DC Circuit Water Analogy (1998)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Other well-known YouTube channels such as EEVblog [3] and ElectroBOOM [4] responded.","text":"> Anything involving the electrical energy not inside wires has no water analogue (ie the electromagnetic waves inside a transformer) You don't even need fancy things like transformers for energy not in the wires to be important. Even a simple DC circuit consisting of a battery in series with a light bulb mostly involves an electromagnetic field to transfer energy from the battery to the light bulb, which mostly takes place outside the wires. The function of the wires when it comes to energy transfer is to carry moving charge which creates the magnetic part of the electromagnetic field that actually carries the transferred energy. Here's a pretty good explanation [1]. That video was from January 2019, and not controversial. Veritasium did a video on the topic in late 2021 that didn't really present things as well and ended up being quite controversial [2]. Other well-known YouTube channels such as EEVblog [3] and ElectroBOOM [4] responded. There was also someone who bought a bunch of wire and did the experiment as described in the Veritasium video (although scaled down). That was discussed on HN and that discussion contains some very interesting links [5]. In particular the link to a talk by Rick Hartley in this subthread is very informative [6]. He talks about how most EMI problems in PCB designs are due to people not taking into account the the energy is not in the wires. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [VIDEO] [5] [LINK] [6] [LINK]"},{"id":29603272,"author":"tdrdt","month":"2021-12","story":"I bought 1000 meters of wire to settle a physics debate [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Explains the video is right but begins with a trick question.","quote":"Medhi says the video is right but starts with a trick question.","text":"Medhi Sadaghdar (Electroboom) explains this. Medhi says the video is right but starts with a trick question. That's what the controversy is about. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29604214,"author":"Abishek_Muthian","month":"2021-12","story":"I bought 1000 meters of wire to settle a physics debate [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Constructive criticism of why the real answer is not among options.","quote":"One of the reasonable constructive criticism I came across for this video is from Mehdi(ElectroBOOM)[1]","text":"> AFAIU, the entire point of the video was to show that electricity travels not through wires but via electric fields (\"through the air\" so to speak). Only that he doesn't context the video with that, But with a 'trick/googly' question. One of the reasonable constructive criticism I came across for this video is from Mehdi(ElectroBOOM)[1] where he explains why the real answer is not in the options offered by Derrick. Personally I like the quality of Veritasium videos, but I lately feel that the barometer has shifted towards sensationalism rather than scientific education. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29672717,"author":"contravariant","month":"2021-12","story":"Electricity Is Magnetism","stance":"reference","why":"Rebuttal explaining situation by drawing in stray capacitance.","quote":"See also ElectroBOOM's rebuttal [1] where he illustrates how the situation can be explained by explicitly drawing in the stray capacitance.","text":"This discussion seems to revolve around Veritasium's video explaining how elecitricty doesn't quite flow inside of power lines. I can't help but feel that \"electricity doesn't travel along the wires\" is a somewhat unhelpful \"well, actually\" explanation that kind of misses the point. And you can tell it misses the point because you can't actually carry electricity without free electrons. And at least in part the confusion seems to focus itself around the concept of stray capacitance, which makes it doubly confusing when you illustrate it with circuit diagrams where stray capacitance is deliberately left out. See also ElectroBOOM's rebuttal [1] where he illustrates how the situation can be explained by explicitly drawing in the stray capacitance. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36920346,"author":"Manuel_D","month":"2023-07","story":"U.S. Marine Corps Antenna Handbook (1999) [pdf]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I find that video usually leads people to an even less correct understanding","text":"Not really in the intuitive sense: [VIDEO] Someone actually went out and tested this: [LINK] The electric field is very tightly constrained around the wire. The amount of energy that reaches the light through the gap is incredibly small, not nearly enough to turn on a real light bulb. The bulk of the energy does indeed have to take the path through the wire. I find that video usually leads people to an even less correct understanding than the marbles-in-a-tube analogy. The Veritasum video can essentially be summarized, \"fun fact: two parallel wires act like an antenna, and an extremely small amount of energy reaches the other end before the rest of the energy takes the long path through the wire."}],"r":[["qCGofLIzX6g",1],["wo84LFzx5nI",1],["YSfCdBBqNXY",1],["cidchWg74Y4",1],["KTzGBJPuJwM",1],["Og6cTlwBTrk",1],["qhbuKbxJsk8",1],["86gyW0vUmVs",1],["n8m7lFQ3njk",1],["Ygty9HxhFK4",1],["-k3mVnRlQLU",1],["FJJTYQYB1JQ",1],["PC2JOQ7z5L0",1],["N1pIYI5JQLE",1],["UDtlvZGmHYk",1],["PriwCi6SzLo",1]]},"pm5xxlajTW0":{"wy":"HN people recommend it as an unfiltered way to understand Bannon's worldview and the populist movement, and praise PBS for releasing full interviews.","q":{"x":"this is worth at least an hour of your time","i":25724940,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":25724940,"author":"nwienert","month":"2021-01","story":"YouTube bans Steve Bannon's podcast channel","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Understand the other side; worth an hour.","quote":"this is worth at least an hour of your time","text":"If you want to genuinely understand the other side and not comfort yourself by relying on simple slanderous slurs to do the work, this is worth at least an hour of your time: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25744112,"author":"j8hn","month":"2021-01","story":"Two Worlds: So Much Prosperity, So Much Skepticism","stance":"rec","why":"Bannon explains the populist movement; fascinating perspective, worth the watch.","quote":"it's quite fascinating to hear this story and perspective.","text":"After the 2016 election I was really curious as to how Trump could be elected. Most of my peers brushed it off as dumb, racist white people, but I really wanted to understand. Especially since I had family who had voted for him who I don't consider to be dumb or racist. I'm from the coast and live in the city. When taking a road trip through the Midwest, it hit me that people from the rust belt and rural areas had to vote for his message. Honestly it was depressing to see the decaying factories and poverty stricken areas. He was giving them hope and was going to fight for them. Anyway, I may be wrong but that's just my perspective. Someone posted this video here yesterday with Steve Bannon explaining the populist movement and how Trump came to be president. There are multiple parts and this video is over two hours long but worth the watch. I'm not normally one to watch a video over half an hour and especially not Steve Bannon, but it's quite fascinating to hear this story and perspective. [VIDEO]"},{"id":43529983,"author":"bilegeek","month":"2025-03","story":"How each pillar of the First Amendment is under attack","stance":"reference","why":"Short clip supporting a point stated explicitly.","quote":"","text":"Not just an inferred point, it's been explicitly stated before. In the longer form interviews (can't find everything in full at the moment), the context is the news media in particular, but it applies to the general public as well. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] (short clip) [3] [LINK] (another short clip)"},{"id":33444455,"author":"lettergram","month":"2022-11","story":"American society is so focused on race that it is blind to class","stance":"reference","why":"Bannon explains establishment opposition to Trump.","quote":"Steve Bannon actually explains this quite well in a PBS Frontline interview","text":"Lol this is why Trump was elected… it’s what makes the “establishment” of both parties oppose him. It’s why we are constantly reminded by the economist that “republicans and trump are racists”. Steve Bannon actually explains this quite well in a PBS Frontline interview [VIDEO] It’s infuriating to constantly have the Economist pushing the idea “the country is racist” or “so and so is racist” for many years now. Only to point out what most people know - a poor person in west Virginia is likely to be white, and have more in common with a poor black person from DC than a wealthy white person from DC."},{"id":33303742,"author":"lettergram","month":"2022-10","story":"Most Americans think money comes before mission at U.S. news organizations","stance":"reference","why":"Example of PBS releasing full-length interviews.","quote":"I also have to give credit to pbs for often releasing full-length interviews.","text":"I don’t think you’re necessarily wrong. PBS generally has been some of the best news / reporting I’ve seen. That said, it’s still driven by ideology — “good” being defined by someone. You can see it in the titles of their documentaries and programming. If you go through their programming objectively and put yourselves in the mindset the opposing side it becomes rather clear. This includes things like portraying nationalism as bad or Christianity as evil (see some of their frontline documentaries) While the bias is there. PBS is generally one of the least bias in the space, I think that’s what makes it so frustrating. A slight improvement in breadth and depth would make it amazing. I also have to give credit to pbs for often releasing full-length interviews. I’ve really appreciated watching hours long interviews with various people about a specific topic. See “Putin files” [LINK] [LINK] or Steve bannons interview on frontline. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["bpAi70WWBlw",1],["WijoL3Hy_Bw",1]]},"-Rxvl3INKSg":{"wy":"Cited in self-driving threads as a firsthand look at robotaxi rides, mostly to point out its glitches and doubts about readiness.","q":{"x":"is a news reporter's video of taking a ride in one, so you can see what it's like to ride in one.","i":38640554,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38640554,"author":"fragmede","month":"2023-12","story":"On being wrong about AI","stance":"rec","why":"A news reporter rides a Waymo so you can see what it's like.","quote":"is a news reporter's video of taking a ride in one, so you can see what it's like to ride in one.","text":"I summon a Waymo car every few days, and it takes me from point A to point B for about the same price as an Uber or Lyft. This only works so long as point A and B are both in San Francisco, but for an average person in San Francisco with access to the app, self-driving cars are already here. You can argue that person isn't average for a number of reasons, but the cars are real and giving rides to members of the general public. [VIDEO] is a news reporter's video of taking a ride in one, so you can see what it's like to ride in one. She's a news reporter and thus not your average person because she has access to Waymo because she's a reporter, but members of the general public are also getting access and are able to do this as well. After watching that, I think a reasonable average person could believe that self driving cars are here. As was said upthread, the future is here but unevenly distributed."},{"id":38012655,"author":"rapind","month":"2023-10","story":"California suspends GM Cruise's autonomous vehicle deployment","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"an ABC reporter documented her Waymo ride and it had a couple goof ups.","text":"Maybe this is a fluke, but a few months ago an ABC reporter documented her Waymo ride and it had a couple goof ups. [VIDEO] Ultimately, I don't think the driving screw ups were that big of a deal (just my opinion). The big deal though is the moronic lack of customer support. How hard is it really to have a customer support staff a phone call away that can reassure the customer and either debug the issue to get them sorted out or at least offer alternatives. It would do so much to improve the users perception. So, typical Google... Phenomenal engineering, and horrible customer service. Maybe it was a just hit piece though and they cut out some contextual video? No idea."},{"id":39086324,"author":"freediver","month":"2024-01","story":"Self-driving as a case study for AGI","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"It paints not so rosey picture about it","text":"I too did not realize Waymo is operating in SF and looked for some videos on YT and found this. It paints not so rosey picture about it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38005661,"author":"paganel","month":"2023-10","story":"California suspends GM Cruise's autonomous vehicle deployment","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"any car that stops at a green light, on the left-lane, is a danger to all the other drivers it shares the road with.","text":"Based on this recent-ish YT [1] video Waymo is also crap, just as Cruise is, as any car that stops at a green light, on the left-lane, is a danger to all the other drivers it shares the road with. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36630962,"author":"gambiting","month":"2023-07","story":"Robotaxi haters in San Francisco are disabling the AVs with traffic cones","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"it shows the tech isn't ready to be used on public streets and probably shouldn't ever be.","text":"Then there is lady who highlights fundamental issues with the way these vehicles operate. This isn't a teething problem that will be resolved with an OTA - it shows the tech isn't ready to be used on public streets and probably shouldn't ever be. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["BzhbAK1pdPM",1]]},"-ga41edXw3A":{"wy":"HN people recall it with nostalgia as an impressive feat for its era, used as an example of the Amiga's graphics ability.","q":{"x":"I remember what were the effective limits at these times and to me it still looks impressive.","i":12332754,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12332754,"author":"acqq","month":"2016-08","story":"The Amiga Boing Ball Explained","stance":"rec","why":"Boing Ball demo, impressive given era limits","quote":"I remember what were the effective limits at these times and to me it still looks impressive.","text":"The video of the Boing Ball: [VIDEO] I remember what were the effective limits at these times and to me it still looks impressive. As an example, the observer would never get the idea that it's done by changing \"the beginning of the screen\" as the grid background stays on the same place all the time, and just the ball (with the shadow) moves. Also note the change of the rotation direction as the ball hits the wall."},{"id":49096200,"author":"irchans","month":"2026-07","story":"Amiga Graphics Archive","stance":"reference","why":"Amiga bouncing ball demo remembered with amazement","quote":"I remember seeing the Amiga bouncing ball demo and being amazed.","text":"I remember seeing the Amiga bouncing ball demo and being amazed. [VIDEO] The Amiga was the second computer that I ever owned. The first was a C64 that I bought freshman year. (My father bought a TRS-80 before that.)"},{"id":46343768,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2025-12","story":"Big GPUs don't need big PCs","stance":"reference","why":"Example of Amiga's programmable graphics in the mid-80s","quote":"While the Amigas, which I am more confortable with, were doing this in the mid-80's","text":"In the home computer universe, such computers were the first ones having a programmable graphics unit that did more than paste the framebuffer into the screen. While the PCs were still displaying text, or if you were lucky to own an Hercules card, gray text, or maybe a CGA one, with 4 colours. While the Amigas, which I am more confortable with, were doing this in the mid-80's: [LINK] [VIDEO] The original Amiga 1000, had on its motherboard, later reduced to fit into an Amiga 500, Motorola 68000 CPU, a programmable sounds chip with DMA channels (Paula), and a programable blitter chip (Agnus aka early GPUs). You would build in RAM the audio, or graphics instructions for the respetive chipset, set the DMA parameters, and let them lose."}],"r":[["y-uuk4Pr2i8",1],["meiU6TxysCg",1],["Q7jcBGLIpus",1],["GcJxRqTs5nk",1],["36GT2zI8lVA",1],["PLyEO0jNt6M",1]]},"AfKZclMWS1U":{"wy":"Mostly posted as a joke in threads about Polish, Unicode or hard-to-pronounce names, and loved as a comedy scene.","q":{"x":"I love this movie scene","i":46105706,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":46105706,"author":"nosianu","month":"2025-12","story":"Google Antigravity just deleted the contents of whole drive","stance":"rec","why":"Beloved Polish comedy movie scene about pronouncing a difficult name.","quote":"I love this movie scene","text":"Ah, Polish. I love this movie scene, which I learned about here on HN some time ago: \"Grzegorz Brzęczyszczykiewicz\" -- [VIDEO]"},{"id":46374587,"author":"nosianu","month":"2025-12","story":"Nabokov's guide to foreigners learning Russian","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Oh yes, Polish, the difficulty is shown in this 1:19 slice from a movie: \"Grzegorz Brzęczyszczykiewicz \" -- [VIDEO]"},{"id":42231748,"author":"Polizeiposaune","month":"2024-11","story":"Charset=\"WTF-8\"","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"(from the Polish comedy film \"How I Unleashed World War II\")","text":"The interaction brings to mind Grzegorz Brzęczyszczykiewicz: [VIDEO] (from the Polish comedy film \"How I Unleashed World War II\")"},{"id":26909552,"author":"aasasd","month":"2021-04","story":"Unicode Proposal – Textile Care Symbols","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Ah, well, I should've written ‘Russian-Latin confusion’—forgot about the alphabet changeovers. But at least I'm told that: > It can be written in Serbian Cyrillic or Gaj's Latin alphabet, whose thirty letters mutually map one-to-one, and the orthography is highly phonemic in all standards. Though digraphs spoil the clarity somewhat. Which reminded me that I should check what Kazakhstan is doing to its language. They seem to at least have avoided the eldritch monstrosity of Polish with ‘Grzegorz Brzęczyszczykiewicz’ and the like ( [VIDEO] ). But they are still setting themselves up for English-language people pretending they can pronounce anything Latin-looking—despite English itself demanding you memorize the dictionary twice, for speaking and writing. So Kazakhstan will soon have its own great writer Karel Kaypek."},{"id":25056530,"author":"emmelaich","month":"2020-11","story":"A Cyrillic orthography for the Polish language","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"You might like the movie about Grzegorz Brzęczyszczykiewicz","text":"> I always felt that Polish words were just too long You might like the movie about Grzegorz Brzęczyszczykiewicz: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["XVmsfL5LG90",1],["IvUU8joBb1Q",1]]},"Bm7L-2J52GU":{"wy":"Pointed to as a good, detailed explanation of how water heaters work and how to save power with them.","q":{"x":"Technology Connections did a good video about them recently.","i":37534832,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37534832,"author":"pimlottc","month":"2023-09","story":"Everyday Uses for PVC Water Pipe","stance":"rec","why":"How tank water heaters cleverly work.","quote":"Technology Connections did a good video about them recently.","text":"The efficiency of hot water heaters is actually surprisingly good. A modern insulated tank retains heat very well. And it's more flexible for heating options since the water can be heated slowly over time. You can now get heat pump water heaters that are more efficient than tankless heaters. [0] Water heaters are actually pretty clever, in a low-tech sort of way. Technology Connections did a good video about them recently. [1] 0: [LINK] 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45936136,"author":"itopaloglu83","month":"2025-11","story":"Reservoir: Smart electric water heater","stance":"reference","why":"Details of water heaters and saving power by switching off.","quote":"Technology Connections have a video about how water heaters work in detail","text":"Technology Connections have a video about how water heaters work in detail and how much power can be saved by strategically turning them off when not needed. [VIDEO] However, the website has some weird vibes as well, like why would you say something like below? > And some just want Dad Mode for when your teenager needs to finish that shower."},{"id":39007871,"author":"hinkley","month":"2024-01","story":"Harnessing heat from wastewater","stance":"reference","why":"Explains how twin element water heaters work.","quote":"How twin heating element water heaters work","text":"The water heater is always refilling. Otherwise you’d have no hot water pressure. It’s just that the new water goes to the opposite end of the tank from where the output is attached. How twin heating element water heaters work: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["gLDYtH1RH-U",1],["TPg4Vcr56F0",1],["qoyKe-HxmFk",1]]},"Fiu9GSOmt8E":{"wy":"HN cites it as a clear explanation of how industry shifted recycling responsibility onto consumers and why most plastic isn't recycled.","q":{"x":"check out John Oliver's Last Week Tonight on plastics","i":27014604,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27014604,"author":"cableshaft","month":"2021-05","story":"Charlie Munger calls BTC disgusting and contrary to the interest of civilization","stance":"rec","why":"Makes a good case on corporate gaslighting about plastic recycling","quote":"check out John Oliver's Last Week Tonight on plastics","text":"> I'm all for greater accountability of big corp when it comes to climate change, but did I miss where they said \"it's not us, we swear\"? Corporations gaslight consumers all the time for anything inconvenient to them and their profits. If you want to see a good case made for one example of it, plastics and plastic recycling, check out John Oliver's Last Week Tonight on plastics (you probably even believe a few of the lies they've been telling everyone about plastic recycling yourself right now. it even surprised me and I already knew plastic recycling was a little bit bullshit already). [VIDEO] Here's a whole 11 episode podcast put out by the BBC called \"How They Made Us Doubt Everything\" about big business using the same techniques to tell us lies about smoking causing cancer to make us doubt climate change. I just found out about this now but I do intend to give it a listen: [LINK] Here's an article that's an excerpt from a book called \"The New Climate War\" that Michael Mann wrote about this very topic: \"Then there’s the now iconic “Crying Indian” ad. Some readers may recall the commercial from the early 1970s. Featuring a tearful Indian named “Iron Eyes Cody,” it alerted viewers to the accumulating bottle and can waste littering our countryside. The ad, however, wasn’t quite what it appeared to be on the surface. A bit of sleuthing reveals that it was actually the centerpiece of a massive deflection campaign engineered by the beverage industry, which sought to point the fi"},{"id":28469481,"author":"ChrisMarshallNY","month":"2021-09","story":"California aims to ban recycling symbols on things that aren’t recyclable","stance":"reference","why":"Rant that plastics numbered above 2 mostly aren't recycled","quote":"John Oliver did a rant on plastics recycling, a few weeks ago[0].","text":"John Oliver did a rant on plastics recycling, a few weeks ago[0]. TL;DR: Anything with a \"Number in triangle\" over 2, is probably not going to be recycled, and you might as well toss it in the trash, as that's where it will end up, anyway[1]. Different from this symbol, and probably won't be affected, but recycling has a great deal of \"PR spin\" to it. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":27803311,"author":"ashayh","month":"2021-07","story":"Podswap: Keep airpods alive and out of the landfill","stance":"reference","why":"Explains how corporations shifted recycling responsibility onto consumers","quote":"This was recently well explained in a John Oliver segment","text":"Why do we need a separate business for this? Apple should be made to pay for it. If anyone finds an Airpod on the street or in trash/landfills, they should be able to take it to the nearest Apple store or mail it and get 20-40$ back for it. This should be the law for _all_ and any products, not just electronics. Large corporations have cleverly shifted the responsibility of recycling on the consumer, while they get to reap all the profits and benefits. This was recently well explained in a John Oliver segment: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28018394,"author":"lou1306","month":"2021-07","story":"The recycling myth: A plastic waste solution littered with failure","stance":"reference","why":"Segment on plastics recycling, mostly it can't be done","quote":"John Oliver recently did a segment [0] on plastics and how we recycle it","text":"Somewhat related: John Oliver recently did a segment [0] on plastics and how we recycle it (spoiler: mostly we can't). It seems regulations are sorely needed: differentiating domestic plastic waste will not get us very far. Still, research on waste reuse must continue: I'm afraid we won't put effort into really cleaning up our mess unless it becomes a somewhat profitable business. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26607764,"author":"godelski","month":"2021-03","story":"EU experts to say nuclear power qualifies for green investment label: document","stance":"reference","why":"Episode covering the plastics recycling problem","quote":"the recent John Oliver episode was on this topic[0]","text":"\"Not recyclable\" in the literal sense. You have lower yields when you reform plastics and there's always contaminants which are difficult (read \"we don't know how to\") remove. This is a complicated technical challenge. In fact, the recent John Oliver episode was on this topic[0]. There's kinda this weird conspiracy about this, which is more about shifting responsibility. Though the more honest answer is closer to \"mostly not recyclable, a bit not economically viable\". But that also depends what we mean by \"economically viable\". If we're using it in the typical sense of \"slightly more expensive for producers\" then a small percentage increase. But if we're talking about \"if producers had infinite resources\" then well still no, but we'd be able to recycle a bit more (we're still not talking much more). I'm assuming the former because the latter is an absurd position. [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["Bd2bbHoVQSM",1],["x6AOwDttBsc",1],["qPb_0JZ6-Rc",1]]},"L43-XE1uwWc":{"wy":"HN readers point to it as a companion to other Levin material on bioelectricity, goal-directedness in cells and cancer as a breakdown in cell communication.","q":{"x":"here is a similar keynote lecture from the same person, but a bit more recent and with a few other very interesting notes","i":26674952,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":26674952,"author":"ve55","month":"2021-04","story":"Frog skin cells turned themselves into living machines","stance":"rec","why":"Keynote by Michael Levin with further remarkable notes on cell behavior.","quote":"here is a similar keynote lecture from the same person, but a bit more recent and with a few other very interesting notes","text":"For those that enjoyed this (and it is quite amazing), here is a similar keynote lecture from the same person, but a bit more recent and with a few other very interesting notes: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35583393,"author":"ly3xqhl8g9","month":"2023-04","story":"Stanford Medicine study: tiny DNA circles are key drivers of cancer","stance":"reference","why":"Video format of Levin's cancer-as-communication-breakdown research.","quote":"In video format","text":"Levin, M. (2021), \"Bioelectric Signaling: Reprogrammable Circuits Underlying Embryogenesis, Regeneration, and Cancer\", Cell [1] is a general overview how cancer is a breakdown in communications leading to a down-scale of the self for the affected cell, see also Levin, M. (2021), \"Bioelectrical approaches to cancer as a problem of the scaling of the cellular self\", Progress in Biophysics and Molecular Biology [2]; for more articles [3]. In video format: \"Why don't (today's) Robots get Cancer\" [4] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30124731,"author":"flobosg","month":"2022-01","story":"Frogs Regrow Missing Limbs in Lab Study","stance":"reference","why":"One of several Levin resources listed.","quote":"","text":"More about Michael Levin: What Bodies Think About: Bioelectric Computation Outside the Nervous System – [LINK] Why don’t (today’s) Robots get Cancer? – [VIDEO] The electrical blueprints that orchestrate life – [LINK] And a tweet about the article – [LINK]"},{"id":27832352,"author":"Voloskaya","month":"2021-07","story":"Mike Levin on using bioelectricity to study how cells form (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Levin keynote on goal-directedness in biology, a less thorough companion to his review.","quote":"You can also find a keynote from him from the last ALife conference, around the same theme, but less thorough","text":"He also wrote [1], which is a review of the development in that domain in the last 10 years or so. It's amazing how much progress has been made even when compared to OP links which is only 2 years old. This is by far the best thing I have read in the last 12 months. From controlling morphogenesis, re-viewing what life and death really mean, where memory actually live, how to create synthetic lifeforms etc. I would recommend it to anyone. It's strangely relevant to computer science/AI as well in my opinion. The ethical implications are also quite staggering to think about. You can also find a keynote from him from the last ALife conference, around the same theme, but less thorough [2]. [1] \"Life, death, and self: Fundamental questions of primitive cognition viewed through the lens of body plasticity and synthetic organisms\" [LINK] (You can find it in full on Sci-hub). [2] \"Multi-scale goal-directedness in biology as inspiration for robotics and artificial life\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":27416255,"author":"saul_goodman","month":"2021-06","story":"Tips from an Avid Cicadas Eater","stance":"reference","why":"Levin talk on cells forming organs, cited about growing steaks.","quote":"","text":"I suspect we'll be getting cockroach burger articles more frequently, the WEF has been pushing this for a bit now: [LINK] Don't get me wrong, once we can grow real steaks in a petri dish I'm all in. Until then, I'm too old for this $!@#. And we're actually closer to being able to grow steaks than some folks realize. The problem right now is obviously a bunch of muscle cells in a dish is not a steak, you need them forming organs. Badow: [VIDEO]"}]},"SH3lR2GLgT0":{"wy":"HN cites it as the landmark example of a hobbyist landing a rocket, and recommends the channel for following that progress.","q":{"x":"Would recommend, it’s been very cool to follow his progress over the years.","i":32384709,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32384709,"author":"chrisdalke","month":"2022-08","story":"I Landed a (Model) Rocket Like SpaceX. It Took 7 Years","stance":"rec","why":"Primary source channel with many videos on landing model rockets.","quote":"Would recommend, it’s been very cool to follow his progress over the years.","text":"Linking to the primary source, Joe Barnard’s YouTube channel, BPSspace: [VIDEO] . He’s got many videos on this. Would recommend, it’s been very cool to follow his progress over the years."},{"id":44305716,"author":"gpm","month":"2025-06","story":"Honda conducts successful launch and landing of experimental reusable rocket","stance":"reference","why":"Hobbyist orbital hop vehicle example.","quote":"There's a hobbyist ~1m orbital hop vehicle","text":"There's a hobbyist ~1m orbital hop vehicle: [VIDEO] Pretty sure that, or some other hobbyist project, is going to take the prize for \"smallest\"."},{"id":40372976,"author":"Aspos","month":"2024-05","story":"Dragonfly: An optical telescope built from an array of off-the-shelf Canon lens","stance":"reference","why":"Example of garage enthusiasts working toward orbit.","quote":"","text":"But there are actually so so many startups and garage enthusiasts at various stages of readiness to put payloads into orbit [VIDEO]"},{"id":35488866,"author":"joshuahaglund","month":"2023-04","story":"Estes Rockets Model Scale 1:100 Replica of SpaceX Falcon 9","stance":"reference","why":"Hobbyist landing model rockets vertically with legs.","quote":"BPS landing model rockets vertically, with legs.","text":"That's what I was thinking! For those who don't know BPS landing model rockets vertically, with legs. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["qd_hEja6bzE",1],["uqY3FMuMuRo",1]]},"UBVV8pch1dM":{"wy":"HN uses it as a short, clear summary of the fast and slow thinking idea.","q":{"x":"Veritasium did a great job summarizing the idea","i":21906226,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21906226,"author":"bshacklett","month":"2019-12","story":"I Was Wrong About Speed Reading (2015)","stance":"rec","why":"Good summary of Thinking, Fast and Slow idea","quote":"Veritasium did a great job summarizing the idea","text":"This sounds a lot like System 1 and System 2 as described by Thinking, Fast and Slow [LINK] Veritasium did a great job summarizing the idea: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17464055,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2018-07","story":"Is Facebook a publisher? In public it says no, but in court it says yes","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"(edited and fixed after accidental early posting. edit2: added URLs) > does an ad being shown next to something objectionable transfer some of that negative sentiment to the brand? Yes, it does because the negative sentiment will be added to the possible results returned by \"System 1\"[1][2] thinking that tends to be very fast at approximate searches of long term memory and applying energy saving simplifications/heuristics. System 1 strongly favors fast lookup times over accuracy ; this is very useful when you need to decide quickly if that thing you just saw around the corner was a bear or just a strangely shaped shrubbery. Wasting a bit of energy running away from shrubbery is a better survival strategy than waiting for an accurate analysis from System 2 while the bear eats you. The question isn't if the brand will be negatively affected - it will . What we don't know is how this is the size of the effect, which probably varies wildly due to many different factors. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16033346,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2017-12","story":"Call of Duty gaming community points to ‘swatting’ in Wichita police shooting","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> the threshold for the use of lethal force That isn't the problem (at least primarily). Claiming that there is a serious hostage situation with the possibility of serious violence (perhaps even a potential murder) seriously escalates the risk and hostility of the situation. I didn't say potential hostility; the claim itself directly increases the perceived threat level of the situation, even if it isn't credible. A team of people responding en masse with weapons[1] at the ready further escalates the tension of the situation and the hostility of the room, even if their weapons are never used. The end result is a very tense situation with everyone - on all sides - ready to jump at anything that might be a threat. Fortunately there is at least some evidence[2] that in \"most\" swattings, cooler heads eventually prevail and nobody gets shot. However, evaluating potential threats is always going to use the faster but less accurate \"System 1\"[3][4]. The higher the tension level, the greater chance that someone's mind will make a serious mistake, which can easily result in a cascade of everyone recursively responding in ways that probably compound the mistake, which is when people tend to get shot/stabbed/beaten/whatever. The solution is that there needs to respond to situations that are less likely to escalate the situation to greater levels of hostility. Once it turns into a \"charlie foxtrot\"[5], it's too late. However, if a streamer's prior notification to the police that, should "},{"id":13820764,"author":"jimmies","month":"2017-03","story":"Abacus use can boost math skills","stance":"reference","why":"Veritasium on how harder learning improves thinking","quote":"Veritasium also mentioned this recently","text":"It seems like the things that are more painful to learn and use make your brain work harder, and thus make you learn better. Or, maybe it is the case that people who know to use the hard stuff are interested in the subject enough to learn how to do it the hard way. You wouldn't be surprised that those who use Assembly to program tend to have better programming skills compared to those who only can program in Visual Basic, would you? Veritasium also mentioned this recently: [VIDEO] The more interesting question to ask to me is (1) whether it is the abacus that makes children learn better, or it is just that children who choose to use the abacus learn better (2) whether teaching abacus use at the beginning has the same effect as teaching abacus use later on after the students already know how to use the calculator. If children who choose to use the abacus learn better, then it wouldn't surprise me, but it means that teaching abacus wouldn't help. If that is false and (2) is true, then we know we better off teach abacus (or assembly) -- it doesn't matter when. But if (2) is false, it means that we have a huge trade-off to consider. Because either we teach the hard stuff at the beginning and discourage a lot of students, or we don't and have worse learning outcomes."}]},"VLHJUfgxEO4":{"wy":"Cited as a primary source for Google building its own switches and running OpenFlow in production.","q":{"x":"Google has been using OpenFlow for some time now.","i":5676031,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":5689149,"author":"jzelinskie","month":"2013-05","story":"Return of the Borg: How Twitter Rebuilt Google’s Secret Weapon","stance":"rec","why":"Shows Google's OpenFlow network testing integrated with continuous integration","quote":"I watched a talk on OpenFlow","text":"Honestly, everyday I'm continually impressed by posts describing the internal technology powering Google. Having an abstraction layer like this for global deployment couldn't be more convenient. With the news about Facebook the other day, I watched a talk on OpenFlow ( [VIDEO] ) and the fact that they have a test suite they can plug into continuous integration in order to see if your commit breaks your application in any network situation is incredible. I'd love to sign an NDA and just talk to the teams working on these projects for hours. Truly impressive work."},{"id":8911423,"author":"thomseddon","month":"2015-01","story":"How We Failed at OpenStack","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for Google preferring to buy switches but building its own","quote":"Google developed their own hardware (despite preferring to have bought it[2])","text":">> >> You see, physical switch operating systems leave a lot to be desired in terms of supporting modern automation and API interaction (Juniper’s forthcoming 14.2 JUNOS updates offer some refreshing REST API’s!). >> This. Network hardware vendors have no incentive to make their devices more easily automated, and in fact face disincentive not to. There is actually a relatively established roadmap for the solution to this in \"bare metal\" / \"white box\" switches that essentially just talk OpenFlow to a controller. Google moved their entire international internal backbone (more traffic than public facing) to this model[1]. The issue at the moment is that there isn't lots of OS options and subsequently very little hardware support. Google developed their own hardware (despite preferring to have bought it[2]) and my understanding is they wrote their own software too. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":6919803,"author":"dmayle","month":"2013-12","story":"Why Google isn't our Bell Labs","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among Google's influential contributions","quote":"","text":"I may be a bit biased, because I'm a Googler... but looking through this thread and seeing all the people downplaying Google's contributions to Go, Android and Dart. As if they were minor... But what about all of the papers that have changed the way the world does computing? Some choice links below: MapReduce [LINK] BigTable [LINK] Dremel Paper [LINK] Chubby Paper [LINK] Urs Holzle on OpenFlow: [VIDEO] Megastore Paper [LINK] Spanner Paper [LINK] Granted, some of these will take awhile before having implementations outside of Google... but you can't deny the impact of this work..."},{"id":5676031,"author":"Ironlink","month":"2013-05","story":" Facebook aims to knock Cisco down a peg with open network hardware","stance":"reference","why":"Urs Hoelzle explains Google's use of OpenFlow in its networks","quote":"Google has been using OpenFlow for some time now.","text":"For those interested, Google has been using OpenFlow for some time now. This presentation by Urs Hölzle is about a year old: [VIDEO] Slides: [LINK]"}],"r":[["NST3u-GjjFw",1]]},"VsHc91IhzdI":{"wy":"Linked in ant-simulation threads as a compact, open-source example of emergent behavior.","q":{"x":"Take a look at this visualization","i":18668506,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18668506,"author":"newman8r","month":"2018-12","story":"Ant Colonies Retain Memories That Outlast the Lifespans of Individuals","stance":"rec","why":"Ant colony visualization with source code in the description","quote":"Take a look at this visualization","text":"Take a look at this visualization [VIDEO] There's a repo in the description. Might not be specifically relevant to your project though"},{"id":32044921,"author":"hughrlomas","month":"2022-07","story":"Show HN: Ants Sandbox - an ants simulator","stance":"reference","why":"Commenter's own earlier pheromone-gradient ant simulation","quote":"It a fascinating example of emergent behavior from simple rules.","text":"This is great to see. Years ago I created a basic simulation using the same principles of pheromone gradients, one for the nest and one for food. [VIDEO] It a fascinating example of emergent behavior from simple rules. I've been wanting to find time to revisit the concept and produce an updated version, your simulation seems to include everything I wanted to do and more."},{"id":8456280,"author":"hughlomas","month":"2014-10","story":"Simulating the emergent behavior of ant colonies","stance":"reference","why":"Commenter's own pheromone-driven ant colony simulation","quote":"I made something exceedingly similar a few years ago","text":"I loved reading this because I made something exceedingly similar a few years ago, and I'm similarly fascinated with how complex behavior can emerge from such simple rules. [VIDEO] My ants also had no conception of where their home was located, their behavior is entirely pheromone driven. They can \"read\" pheromones from any adjacent cell. They leave a pheromone every movement depending on which state they are in, either \"find food\" or \"return food\". In my system, the pheromones have a strength. Whenever an ant touches the nest, his \"nest pheromone\" is at full strength (255). Each movement decreases the strength by 1. This sets up a natural gradient of nest strength for ants to follow. They also \"deteriorate\" each round as yours do. A similar setup is used with food, such that a high food scent is created when they pick up food, and decreases in strength as they return to the nest. So to find food, they try to move up a food gradient or down the nest gradient (away from the nest), while to return food they move up the nest gradient (towards the nest). Their searching consists of a random walking into cells that have no nest scent, or following a food trail. They have an energy cost for moving, and if it's too low they have to eat a piece of food. Returning a piece of food to the hill spawns a new ant. The lines on the right side show the population of each colony and the total food on the board. From these extremely basic rules, they are very efficient at clearing the \"world\" "}],"r":[["ihkRwArnc1k",1]]},"WA0wKeokWUU":{"wy":"Cited when discussing sensational health journalism and supplement industry misinformation.","q":{"x":"John Oliver has a good video on it","i":25262794,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25262794,"author":"Fishysoup","month":"2020-12","story":"Vitamin D insufficiency may account for almost 90% of Covid-19 deaths","stance":"rec","why":"Good video on supplement industry misinformation.","quote":"John Oliver has a good video on it","text":"There's no such thing as an \"immune booster\". The immune system is incredibly complex and nuanced, and there isn't a knob (or 10) that you can tweak to turn it up or down. It exists in a continuum of largely understood configurations, and it can learn and adapt (not always with good results). If anything, \"boosting\" it as in turning up immune activity is usually terrible (think auto-immune disorders, inflammation, cancer). The \"misinformation\" people are battling is about a) unsubstantiated health claims and b) the fact that supplement manufacturers don't need to provide any evidence or follow any regulations regarding what's actually in the supplements. That means they can say they're selling e.g. Vitamin C but it's just sugar. John Oliver has a good video on it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25882490,"author":"nindalf","month":"2021-01","story":"Larry King has died","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"It’s funny you should blame the government for this. In the 90s people actually died from shady supplements. In response the FDA tried to expand its authority to regulating these supplements. The industry started a scaremongering ad campaign telling people that they wouldn’t be able to get Vitamin C supplements unless they wrote their congressman. Many did. Reps received more letters from their constituents on this one Bill than the entire Vietnam War [1]. In response to pressure from constituents, government reversed course. The unelected experts in Government tried to do the right thing. It’s the people who were so easily misled that forced the end of regulation for dietary supplements. Paraphrasing what Terry Pratchett said, the problem here isn’t that we have the wrong sort of government, it’s that we have the wrong sort of people. [1] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":14815050,"author":"dumbneurologist","month":"2017-07","story":"Scientists Reverse Brain Damage in Drowned Toddler?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Haha. This is an incredibly polite reply, sharpened to a very fine point. I'm grateful I have something to contribute to a site that I've gotten an incredible amount out of. And it's a powerful reminder that very very smart people can have a hard time critically appraising medical claims. As powerful as the internet is, medicine is really complicated, and it's easy for doctors to forget that. I'll let the surgeon speak for him/herself, but for my part: I am raising the issue of industry size and quackery to give other readers context. It doesn't make the article wrong necessarily, but I would want to have that context as a non-expert reader trying to understand how it fits into the larger body of scientific understanding. And I would say yes, that does happen (and is appropriate to have happen) in scientific discourse. On a related note: one amazing site that isn't well known is the OpenPayments[1], maintained by the US government. If you live in the US and have doctor, you can see how much compensation they received from industrial sources. Some of the numbers are shocking. Put your own doctor in, and see where they are. There is no need to resort to conspiracy claims when you can audit them, to some degree, yourself. The physician mentioned in the article, Dr. Paul Harch, has received $74.48. So way to go Dr. Harch! we can worry much less about getting a skewed opinion based on a industry payments. But weighing against that is his CV, which lists publications with \"D Amen\","},{"id":8140571,"author":"gopalv","month":"2014-08","story":"Aspirin could dramatically cut cancer risk, according to biggest study yet","stance":"reference","why":"John Oliver satirizes this style of health journalism.","quote":"John Oliver covered this style of journalism, rather sarcastically a while back.","text":"Next I'll be hearing I should chew willow bark instead of paying for Aspirin :) Even if this worked, the amount of misinformation about this has inoculated me against taking medical decisions myself (other than avoiding the free Twix littered in the office kitchen). John Oliver covered this style of journalism, rather sarcastically a while back. [VIDEO]"}]},"_9LX9HSQkWo":{"wy":"HN cites it as an example of temporally stable AI video stylization built from ordinary production gear.","q":{"x":"let the results speak for themselves","i":34987679,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34987679,"author":"samspenc","month":"2023-03","story":"Introducing ChatGPT and Whisper APIs","stance":"rec","why":"Behind the scenes of making a 7-minute anime with Stable Diffusion","quote":"They recently created a full 7-minute anime using Stable Diffusion with their own models and their existing video production gear","text":"I thought Midjourney was better as well, until I saw some recent videos from Corridor Crew on Youtube. For those who don't know, this is a VFX studio in LA that tries to keep at the cutting-edge of video production techniques and posts content to their Youtube channel, and they have a massive number of followers and several viral videos. They recently created a full 7-minute anime using Stable Diffusion with their own models and their existing video production gear, I'll post the links and let the results speak for themselves The actual 7-minute anime piece produced using SD: [LINK] Behind the scenes: \"Did we change anime forever?\" [VIDEO] \"VFX reveal before and after\" [LINK]"},{"id":39399734,"author":"someperson","month":"2024-02","story":"Sora: Creating video from text","stance":"reference","why":"Anime with consistent characters made from footage of themselves","quote":"They used videos of themselves running through their own models to make the characters.","text":"> There was a video of some guys doing this (sorry can’t find it) to make an anime type animation. With consistent characters. They used videos of themselves running through their own models to make the characters. That was Corridor Crew: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35043203,"author":"Kelamir","month":"2023-03","story":"Anime rock, paper, scissors [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Explains how the AI-processed anime characters were made","quote":"where they demonstrated how it came to be","text":"The characters here are processed with Stable Diffusion. This is an impressive progress in the field of video generation with help of AI, considering how consistent the generated characters look like across frames, in my opinion. Also here is another video [VIDEO] \"Did We Just Change Animation Forever?\" where they demonstrated how it came to be."},{"id":35107879,"author":"yummypaint","month":"2023-03","story":"Wonder Studio: this AI-powered tool might be a preview of the future of VFX","stance":"reference","why":"Technique for turning live action into animation","quote":"It unsurprisingly received alot of pushback from people in the industry but I think it's really impressive.","text":"This reminds me alot of this techniquefor turning live action into animation: [VIDEO] It unsurprisingly received alot of pushback from people in the industry but I think it's really impressive."},{"id":35395152,"author":"dbspin","month":"2023-03","story":"If AI scaling is to be shut down, let it be for a coherent reason","stance":"reference","why":"Example of temporally stable animation with Stable Diffusion","quote":"Corridor digital show off a similar technique here","text":"The issues you mention with Stable Diffusion are teething issues. Here's an example of a current technique to get temporally stable animations with Stable Diffusion - [LINK] Corridor digital show off a similar technique here - [VIDEO] GPT4 with its new plugin architecture is rapidly becoming more capable. I think you're absolutely correct in the tendency of all LLMs to hallucinate is a major issue that we don't know how to correct even in principle. But there are techniques to moderate its impact on responses - triangulating using multiple models, citing sources etc. You could build an assistant like say this, today - [LINK] to make your appointments and answer the phone today, using technology like Uberduck for voice generation, GPT for conversation analysis and DeepSpeech, Kaldi or Whisper for speech to text. I agree with the criticism that we're not likely to develop AGI this way. We're not going to have a wilful agent with it's own goals trying to escape the bonds of its server to destroy humanity any time soon. But that's not necessary for this technology to be incredibly dangerous. A mindless philosophical zombie with the capacity to convince people it isn't could lead to an extinction event too. Given that we don't understand precisely how these models process information, how to imbue them with goals or motivation (or measure those), how to accurately assess how well they've been trained - or any of a dozen other major alignment issues - there's a strong argument that"},{"id":34960386,"author":"sschueller","month":"2023-02","story":"Diffusion with Offset Noise: Finetuning SD to generate very dark or light images","stance":"reference","why":"Corridor Crew stabilizing Stable Diffusion output for video","quote":"what corridor crew[2] did to stabilize the randomness when you want to make videos","text":"The whole thing with SD is extremely interesting and difficult to keep up with as there are so many new things coming in almost daily. Just last week I saw ControlNet[1] which ads a lot more control. Today I saw what corridor crew[2] did to stabilize the randomness when you want to make videos. Very exciting. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["kCc8FmEb1nY",1],["GVT3WUa-48Y",1]]},"awADEuv5vWY":{"wy":"HN mentions it as a reference for Openwater's approach of light-based deep tissue imaging.","q":{"x":"[1] is a good quick place to start and judge if you want to study their brainscanner in more depth.","i":42411389,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":42411389,"author":"morphle","month":"2024-12","story":"Noninvasive imaging method can penetrate deeper into living tissue","stance":"rec","why":"Quick starting point on imaging deep inside living tissue with light","quote":"[1] is a good quick place to start and judge if you want to study their brainscanner in more depth.","text":"Mary Lou Jepsen of openwater.cc [2] has managed to image neurons and other large cell structures deep inside living bodies by phase wave interferometry and descattering signals through human tissues, even through skull and bone[1] using CMOS imageing chips, lasers, ultrasonics and holographics. A thousand times cheaper fMRI. She aims to get to realtime million pixels moving picture resolutions of around a micron. Eventually she will be able to read and write neurons or vibrate and someday maybe burn cell tissues to destruction. There are later presentations at the website where the technique is better described and visualized, but [1] is a good quick place to start and judge if you want to study their brainscanner in more depth. There are patents and a few scientific publications [3] that I'm aware of, but mostly many up to date talks with demonstrations by startup founder Mary Lou [4]. And recently she is open sourcing[5] parts of the hardware and software on github [6] so we can start building our own lab setups and improve the imageing software. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK] [6] [LINK]"},{"id":28402019,"author":"systemsignal","month":"2021-09","story":"DARPA grant to work on sensing and stimulating the brain noninvasively [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Openwater's use of light for imaging","quote":"Claim to use light for imaging","text":"Thoughts on openwater? [LINK] Claim to use light for imaging [VIDEO] There’s also the company Kernel Both companies have pretty successful founders"},{"id":24516271,"author":"throwaway316943","month":"2020-09","story":"Trials begin for a new weapon against Parkinson’s: light","stance":"reference","why":"Research on near-infrared light as a medical imaging technique","quote":"There is research into using this as a medical imaging technique [0]","text":"Interesting to note that NIR light can penetrate deep into the body. There is research into using this as a medical imaging technique [0] and also research on improving age related vision loss [1] [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":18739298,"author":"pontifier","month":"2018-12","story":"Can you use a magnifying glass and moonlight to light a fire? (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Shows light emerging as a beam from opaque material via holography","quote":"It struck me as very similar to the setup in this video.","text":"There is a very interesting image in the article. It shows a bunch of light coming into a block and emerging as a beam. It also has a caption saying this is impossible. It struck me as very similar to the setup in this video. [VIDEO] At about 4 minutes in, a nearly identical setup is shown with a beam of light emerging from a block of opaque material using holographic techniques. It seems plausible to me that a specially designed anti-moon hologram could allow reconstruction of the incident light from the sun, thus allowing a fire to be started without violating any law of thermodynamics."}],"r":[["nYg6jzotiAc",1],["1e8VZlPBx_0",1],["id1WShzzMCQ",1],["jVkLVRt6c1U",1],["P1ww1IXRfTA",1],["FvmTSpJU-Xc",1],["QTJRwKOFddc",1],["N9c7_8Gp7gI",1]]},"ciGQCP6HgqI":{"wy":"Cited in discussions of memory arenas and allocators as the origin of the implicit context idea that later appeared in Odin and other languages.","q":{"x":"Here's that Jonathan Blow stream if anyone else is curious","i":33417510,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":46227538,"author":"nmilo","month":"2025-12","story":"Golang's big miss on memory arenas","stance":"rec","why":"Implicit context solves context-passing across languages.","quote":"was one of the coolest features of a programming language","text":"Implicit context [1] was one of the coolest features of a programming language I’ve ever seen that no one has ever implemented. And I’m really not sure why. Not just Go but most languages have this context passing problem with varying degrees of solution quality, making this implicit and built in could have opened up so many possibilities, more than just arenas. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33417510,"author":"rudedogg","month":"2022-11","story":"Untangling Lifetimes: The Arena Allocator","stance":"reference","why":"Jonathan Blow stream on scratch allocators.","quote":"Here's that Jonathan Blow stream if anyone else is curious","text":"> per-frame \"scratch\" pool allocator Here's that Jonathan Blow stream if anyone else is curious: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32805667,"author":"cyber_kinetist","month":"2022-09","story":"A Review of the Odin Programming Language","stance":"reference","why":"Jai's implicit context idea.","quote":"I remember first seeing this idea from Jai","text":"I remember first seeing this idea from Jai ( [VIDEO] ), and I'm happy it's landing in other languages. Zig went another direction and requires you to specify the allocator explicitly via a function parameter, which is understandable given the language's goal to make everything explicit."}]},"d8BcCLLX4N4":{"wy":"Cited as a quick, visual explanation of IEX's deliberate latency and of artificial delay as a fairness tool.","q":{"x":"Tom Scott did a nice video [1] on IEX's setup with 38 miles of fiber","i":20595004,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":20595004,"author":"m0rphling","month":"2019-08","story":"Goldman Sachs is spending $100M to shave milliseconds off stock trades","stance":"rec","why":"Explains IEX using fiber coil to add fairness delay","quote":"Tom Scott did a nice video [1] on IEX's setup with 38 miles of fiber","text":"Tom Scott did a nice video [1] on IEX's setup with 38 miles of fiber to add enough latency as a mechanism of fairness. Purportedly, this benefits all exchanges after it was successfully deployed. It seems like the incentives are not there for larger, more established exchanges to implement such blanket latency. So, firms like GS see a benefit to this kind of investment. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36716021,"author":"kmeisthax","month":"2023-07","story":"Commercial Interests Petition FCC for High Power Allocation on Shortwave","stance":"reference","why":"Shows exchange delaying trades with a box of fiber","quote":"","text":"There's actually an exchange that did this. In order to avoid software problems that would negate the slowing, they actually plugged their servers into a giant box full of fiber optic cable. If someone manages to get past that, then they don't have an HFT firm, they have a time machine. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31413853,"author":"colejohnson66","month":"2022-05","story":"Riot Games: Artificial Latency for Remote Competitors","stance":"reference","why":"Covers IEX's miles of wire for fairness","quote":"IEX has 38 miles of wire in their building.","text":"Not apocryphal; IEX has 38 miles of wire in their building. Tom Scott did a video a few years ago about it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31416134,"author":"blibble","month":"2022-05","story":"Riot Games: Artificial Latency for Remote Competitors","stance":"reference","why":"Video of IEX's large coil of wire","quote":"there's even a tom scott video on it","text":"the wire thing is definitely real for IEX it's part of the exchange's license and they have several videos of the rather large wire there's even a tom scott video on it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25735276,"author":"bigmattystyles","month":"2021-01","story":"Intruder at the top of the 20 meter amateur band?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"The company profiled in this video add lag","text":"Do exchanges like HF traders? I know HF traders themselves justify their own existence by claiming they provide liquidity to the markets, but I've always just sort-of rolled my eyes at that. They only extract value and provide none, in my opinion, tax the profits heavily. I think every exchange should pad each order by a random number between 0 and 250ms, it would take care of it. The company profiled in this video add lag [VIDEO]"},{"id":24747622,"author":"n3k5","month":"2020-10","story":"Timekeeping in Financial Exchanges","stance":"reference","why":"Short video on the IEX delay cables","quote":"Tom Scott made a short video about these cables","text":"Tom Scott made a short video about these cables: [VIDEO] 61 kilometres of fibre-optics for a 350 microsecond delay."},{"id":23598258,"author":"anfractuosity","month":"2020-06","story":"Companies Pitch Shortwave Radio to Shave Milliseconds Off Trades","stance":"reference","why":"Short video on IEX fiber delay","quote":"I watched this short video about it too","text":"The IEX stock exchange uses fibre to introduce a fixed delay - [LINK] \"This 350 microsecond delay adds a round-trip delay of 0.0007 seconds and is designed to negate the certain speed advantages utilized by some high-frequency trader\" I watched this short video about it too - [VIDEO]"}]},"hTsrDpsYHrw":{"wy":"Linked as concrete evidence that automated battery swapping already works in practice, especially in China.","q":{"x":"Here is a video on how it works","i":30763452,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30763452,"author":"ComradePhil","month":"2022-03","story":"Two-minute battery changes push India’s delivery riders to switch to e-scooters","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how automated battery swapping works.","quote":"Here is a video on how it works","text":"Here is a video on how it works: [VIDEO] While currently, thr stations have to be manned, there is potential for this to be completely unmanned drivethrough where you take the car and never have to get out of it, you pay and the battery is changed for you in 3 minutes (which also looks like it could be improved, and that could come down to less than a minute). I have no doubt that this will absolutely kill the competition. Also, it doesn't have to be one standard size. A standard interface with a few standard sizes will work just fine."},{"id":38955600,"author":"pierat","month":"2024-01","story":"An influx of Chinese cars is terrifying the West","stance":"reference","why":"Example of 3-minute battery swap in China.","quote":"In China, its 3 minutes to SWAP a battery.","text":"Well, I've been looking at Chinese EV's and basically drooling at what they're doing. In this country (USA), an EV is a ball-and-chain over how many miles until you go before a recharge for hours. In China, its 3 minutes to SWAP a battery. You know, the solution the manchild in charge of Tesla said wouldn't work. And here it is, working in USD 12k ev's. [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] But hey, look at the \"bright side\" - we now get $7500 off cars that start at USD 70k for EVs. Real 'deal'."},{"id":28712956,"author":"cronix","month":"2021-10","story":"Always-on Processor magic: How Find My works while iPhone is powered off","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the automated battery swap station in action.","quote":"This is cued up to where he gets to the battery swap station","text":"> A car battery should be swappable in a day or two Nio currently swaps out their car batteries in about 3 minutes, much like you'd get an oil change at Jiffy Lube or something. It's completely automated except someone pulls the car in/out of the bay for you. I imagine that human step will be relatively short lived. > they have to be heavily shielded in case of an accident. Yes, it's just a simple matter of purposefully designing it to be removed easily. This is cued up to where he gets to the battery swap station: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27353763,"author":"martin-adams","month":"2021-06","story":"Why electric cars will take over sooner than you think","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates NIO's 3-minute battery swap in China.","quote":"it's being done right now with the Nio with a 3 minute battery swap in China.","text":"I was about to say that I don't see this happening due to the way batteries are being built into the chassis. Then I looked it up and it's being done right now with the Nio with a 3 minute battery swap in China. [VIDEO] 580km / 360 miles range. I'm very impressed!"},{"id":27390102,"author":"clouddrover","month":"2021-06","story":"7-11 is opening 500 EV charging stations by the end of 2022","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence that NIO battery swapping works at scale.","quote":"Nio has done over two million battery swaps","text":"> No consumer is ever going to agree to this psychologically Nio has done over two million battery swaps: [LINK] [VIDEO] Ample wants to do the same but with fleet cars at first: [LINK]"},{"id":24319043,"author":"theshrike79","month":"2020-08","story":"What can 6,000 electric vehicles tell us about EV battery health?","stance":"reference","why":"Shows NIO battery swap stations and charger-delivery service.","quote":"Fully Charged on YouTube had a video on this","text":"The Chinese auto maker NIO already has battery swap stations and a service that will drive a charger to your location with a container of electrons and charge your car. Fully Charged on YouTube had a video on this: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["j5v8D-alAKE",1],["rQmqVVmMB3k",1]]},"iv-5mZ_9CPY":{"wy":"HN uses it as a clear explainer for diffusion mechanics, such as negative prompts and faster sampling, and for joint text-image encoders.","q":{"x":"WL & 3b1b made a nice video on it","i":48442554,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":48442554,"author":"dilyevsky","month":"2026-06","story":"Playing with Vision Embeddings","stance":"rec","why":"Nice video on training text and image encoders together.","quote":"WL & 3b1b made a nice video on it","text":"> Because you somehow need a giant training set which describes images in natural language, no? That's definitely one way - they train a text encoder together with an image encoder on a labelled set of images. WL & 3b1b made a nice video on it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45151962,"author":"pornel","month":"2025-09","story":"The maths you need to start understanding LLMs","stance":"reference","why":"Explains diffusion speedups from statistical mechanics-based differential equations.","quote":"Breakthrough in image generation speed literally came from applying better differential equations for diffusion","text":"Breakthrough in image generation speed literally came from applying better differential equations for diffusion taken from statistical mechanics physics papers: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44884544,"author":"elliotto","month":"2025-08","story":"Training language models to be warm and empathetic makes them less reliable","stance":"reference","why":"Shows subtracting the 'low quality video' prompt to improve generation.","quote":"they literally subtract the prompt for 'low quality video' from the input, and it improves the quality.","text":"My favourite instantiation of this weird invocation is from this AI video generator, where they literally subtract the prompt for 'low quality video' from the input, and it improves the quality. [VIDEO] I've just migrated my AI product to a different underlying model and had to redo a few of the prompts that the new model was interpreting differently. It's not obseleted, just requires a bit of migration. The improved quality of the new models outweighs any issues around prompting."}]},"mLEOZO1GwVc":{"wy":"Shared as a primary source for Dijkstra's views, such as his approach to writing code on paper.","q":{"x":"demonstrated in this beautiful interview","i":37823711,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37823711,"author":"ZoomZoomZoom","month":"2023-10","story":"Why (and How) I Write Code with Pencil and Paper","stance":"rec","why":"Dijkstra's approach to handwritten code, shown in the interview","quote":"demonstrated in this beautiful interview","text":"Any discussion of handwritten code is incomplete without mentioning Edsger Dijkstra's approach, demonstrated in this beautiful interview: [VIDEO] I'm surprised no one has mentioned it here yet."},{"id":39255412,"author":"rednab","month":"2024-02","story":"Dijkstra's interview on Dutch TV (2000)","stance":"reference","why":"Dutch quote on keeping errors out of programs, with a dry joke","quote":"The translator missed a very dry and very Dijkstra joke.","text":"[VIDEO] The Dutch language quote as displayed: \"We mogen niet uit nonchalance fouten in een programma aanbrengen. Dat moeten we systematisch en met zorg doen.\". Feel free to run that through your favorite translator. The subtitles: \"We should not introduce errors through sloppiness but systematically keep them out.\" The translator missed a very dry and very Dijkstra joke."},{"id":39141512,"author":"eesmith","month":"2024-01","story":"Edsger Dijkstra carried computer science on his shoulders (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Dijkstra's vehicle and part of a longer interview","quote":"It's part of a longer interview with him.","text":"You can see the vehicle at [VIDEO] . He and his wife are driving to some sort of camping area. It's part of a longer interview with him. He also does crossword puzzles."},{"id":34765869,"author":"ly3xqhl8g9","month":"2023-02","story":"Dijkstra comparing computer science to alchemy (1989)","stance":"reference","why":"Source interview for Dijkstra's views on writing programs and Beethoven","quote":"Edsger Dijkstra interview,","text":"For Dijkstra programming meant to think about a problem, then sit for a few minutes at your desk and write with pen on paper \"in one go\" the perfect algorithm/program, no bugs, no errors, no corrections, from the first try, and also, in beautiful handwriting. Some kind of idealized way in which Mozart would write his compositions. Whereas \"Beethoven was a doubter and a struggler who started writing before he finished the composition and then glued corrections on the paper. In one place he did this nine times. When they peeled them, the last version proved identical to the first one. This iterative method of programming is somehow a very Anglo-Saxon custom. British education is pervaded by it. People learn, when they write, not to try to get it right the first time. Just write what's on your mind and then rewrite repeatedly to get the product you want. That's partly why word processors are marketed so aggressively and partly why they have been so successful there.\" [1] [1] Edsger Dijkstra interview, [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["cIQ9IXSUzuM",1]]},"zJOS0sV2a24":{"wy":"HN refers to it for the moment where Knuth asks a question and addresses the Steve Jobs anecdote, a favorite geek moment.","q":{"x":"one of the all-time great geek moments was when Munroe did a tech talk at Google","i":6152681,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":6152681,"author":"pjmorris","month":"2013-08","story":"Creator of xkcd Reveals Secret Backstory of His Epic 3,990-Panel Comic","stance":"rec","why":"Knuth asks Munroe questions at Google talk","quote":"one of the all-time great geek moments was when Munroe did a tech talk at Google","text":"To me, one of the all-time great geek moments was when Munroe did a tech talk at Google, and Don Knuth gets up to ask him if he's thought about doing animated cartoons, and what is his favorite log log n sorting algorithm, about 21:30 in to this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":46542458,"author":"svat","month":"2026-01","story":"The Jeff Dean Facts","stance":"reference","why":"Munroe's Google talk with Knuth question","quote":"Knuth comes up to ask a question at 21:30","text":"FWIW the XKCD talk at Google is here (wow, 18 years ago! I remember watching this video when it was posted): [VIDEO] (Knuth comes up to ask a question at 21:30) (Can't tell from the video where he was sitting otherwise, though there are definitely at least some people sitting on the floor.)"},{"id":34921437,"author":"lIl-IIIl","month":"2023-02","story":"More than half of YC startups don't have a dot com","stance":"reference","why":"Randall explains origin of the xkcd name","quote":"Source: talk by Randall, might be this one but I'm not sure","text":"Randall wanted a unique short string associated with all his work. That way he could Google \"xkcd\" and find everything he ever did online. I believe he had a script that generated all possible 4-letter combinations and filtered out ones with any search results, and settled on \"xkcd\". But he became a victim of his own success when the comic became so popular and \"xkcd\" is now synonymous with the comic. Source: talk by Randall, might be this one but I'm not sure: [VIDEO] ."},{"id":24023484,"author":"pjmorris","month":"2020-08","story":"The Yoda of Silicon Valley (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Knuth asking Munroe questions during Q&A","quote":"he showed up at Randall Munroe's Google tech talk [0]","text":"There are many more important things about Knuth and his work, but one of my favorite stories about him is that he showed up at Randall Munroe's Google tech talk [0], and, during Q&A, asked Munroe \"Have you thought about animated cartoons?\" and \"What is your n log n algorithm for searching?\" I have a special respect for people who are both brilliant and humble, and Knuth is my poster person for that. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18699604,"author":"Permit","month":"2018-12","story":"The Yoda of Silicon Valley","stance":"reference","why":"Knuth himself doubts the story","quote":"FWIW Knuth himself doesn't think this happened","text":"FWIW Knuth himself doesn't think this happened nor does he sound like the kind of person (to me) who would try to cut someone down like that: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8390949,"author":"nighthawk454","month":"2014-09","story":"Work Habits at Apple","stance":"reference","why":"Talk with real-time translated subtitles","quote":"it might be this one with Randall Munroe (xkcd).","text":"Google has some pretty good software for this, thought it might be internal. When they have their \"Talks at Google\" events, there's a screen off to the side that provides real-time translated subtitles. There's at least one talk where the speaker addresses this system, and tries to get it to transcribe funny stuff. Can't remember which one exactly, but it might be this one with Randall Munroe (xkcd). [VIDEO]"},{"id":5311284,"author":"tompko","month":"2013-03","story":"What is your best programmer joke?","stance":"reference","why":"Knuth debunks the story; Guido asks Python questions","quote":"There's a talk by Randall Munroe (xkcd) at google where this is debunked by Knuth himself.","text":"There's a talk by Randall Munroe (xkcd) at google where this is debunked by Knuth himself. It also features him being asked questions about Python by Guido. [VIDEO]"},{"id":3060923,"author":"philfreo","month":"2011-10","story":"Find apartments by commute times","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"YES!!! I was literally searching everywhere for this only 1 week ago and determined that there was nothing good out there. Relevant: [VIDEO] Now, if only there was a way to enter 2 addresses (for spouses/roommates) and have it give you the places with the best cumulative commute times. EDIT: Just saw Mapnificent does this! Would also love to see this fully integrated into [LINK] ."},{"id":1266572,"author":"tomhoward","month":"2010-04","story":"Steve Jobs meets Don Knuth","stance":"reference","why":"Knuth and Munroe exchange about Jobs story","quote":"This story is the subject of a fascinating exchange between Knuth and Randall Munroe","text":"This story is the subject of a fascinating exchange between Knuth and Randall Munroe in Munroe's Authors@Google talk: [VIDEO] Knuth neither confirms nor outright denies the story, but says \"I've only met [Jobs] a couple of times and in each case I was impressed by him probably more than he was impressed by me\"."},{"id":336335,"author":"antiform","month":"2008-10","story":"When Steve Jobs met Don Knuth","stance":"reference","why":"Knuth comments on the Jobs anecdote","quote":"Knuth's response is \"I've been told this story a number of times...but I was impressed by him more than he was impressed by me.","text":"Maybe Knuth was more of a rebel back in the day, but somehow I can't imagine him having that sort of response. In Randall Munroe's Authors@Google talk [ [VIDEO] ], Don Knuth asks him a question at ~21:30 and at ~25:30 Randall asks Knuth about this very anecdote. Knuth's response is \"I've been told this story a number of times...but I was impressed by him more than he was impressed by me.\""}]},"02Or-Hx3yqc":{"wy":"Posted as a joke or pop-culture reference whenever threads touch on backward or barbaric medicine.","m":[{"id":43916671,"author":"toomuchtodo","month":"2025-05","story":"WeightWatchers files bankruptcy","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Shades of","text":"What's wild to me is the amount of effort and resources that have gone into weight loss around exercise and diet only for us to discover it is a hormone and brain chemistry issue around the gut and reward centers (GLP-1s). Shades of [VIDEO]"},{"id":13103311,"author":"tcj_phx","month":"2016-12","story":"A Few Billionaires Are Turning Medical Philanthropy on Its Head","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"A classic fun movie clip","text":"I can't match you on credentials or acronyms, but I'm pretty good at figuring out what matters for improving my own health. \"Should cardiologists evaluate their obsession with LDLs and HDLs?\" - [LINK] (April 2016) Saw this link on the sidebar: \"Slow Medicine: Pressing Pause on Statins For Primary Prevention\" - [LINK] (December 4 2016) I would estimate that the cholesterol fetish has about 2 more years before it begins to slide into irrelevance, and the field begins to refocus on the causes behind the symptom. A classic fun movie clip: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9735499,"author":"justifier","month":"2015-06","story":"Aneurysm","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"personally, i look forward to bones","text":"\" I rarely clip aneurysms now. All the skills that I slowly and painfully acquired to become an aneurysm surgeon have been rendered obsolete by technological change. Although neurosurgery is no longer what it once was, the neurosurgeon’s loss has been the patient’s gain. \" so nice to read this many of the entrenched remain so through superstition at the loss of progress, but to read someone speak highly of a practice that rendered a section of their skill set obsolete is heartening i have family who work in trauma and they condescendingly balk at my excitement over new medical technology: [LINK] [LINK] personally, i look forward to bones: [VIDEO]"}]},"6VixqvOcK8E":{"wy":"Mentioned as an example of Shapiro struggling under tough questioning, and sometimes in critical political comments.","m":[{"id":33686104,"author":"hotpotamus","month":"2022-11","story":"While crypto bro scammed clients, reporters scammed readers","stance":"reference","why":"Example of Shapiro struggling in a British interview","quote":"It was really illuminating on a few levels.","text":"If you want a striking example of this, look at the interview Ben Shapiro gave to the BBC a few years back. Whatever you think of Shapiro, he has a masterful understanding of how American media works (I believe his Daily Wire is among the top shared sites on Facebook for instance), but he is clearly off-balance in a British interview. The interviewer repeatedly asks questions and doesn't allow Shapiro to dodge them which eventually results in Shapiro becoming too agitated to finish. It was really illuminating on a few levels. [VIDEO]"},{"id":45230946,"author":"thepryz","month":"2025-09","story":"Charlie Kirk killed at event in Utah","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Modern political commentators and influencers are strategic about who they will engage with and how they engage. You can see this with Ben Shapiro when he walked out of an interview with conservative BBC host, Andrew Neil. Shapiro was unprepared for a real challenge and his go-to of speaking fast, gish galloping, and calling out the “radical left views” of his opponent didn’t work because the host was a conservative. [VIDEO] If you want a someone who would be an effective counter, look to Mehdi Hasan of Zeteo."},{"id":24815986,"author":"cycomanic","month":"2020-10","story":"The Problem of Free Speech in an Age of Disinformation","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"The argument that the media is left leaning seems to be based largely on the notion of \"if you don't agree with everything I say and ask difficult questions you must have a left bias\". Have a look at this Shapiro interview [VIDEO] . For some context Andrew Neil the journalist being accused by Shapiro of a left wing bias, is an ultra conservative. Anyone with some knowledge of the UK media would fall over laughing hearing this accusation."}]},"B6vr1x6KDaY":{"wy":"Cited as a harder real-world version of the pole-balancing problem and as proof that chaotic systems can be controlled.","q":{"x":"Triple!!! inverted pendulum balancing with a physical cart (2011), a much much much harder task","i":12476108,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":38019035,"author":"chr1","month":"2023-10","story":"SpaceX Starship Super Heavy Project at the Boca Chica Launch Site","stance":"reference","why":"Shows chaotic system can still be controlled","quote":"Chaotic behavior does not mean that system is impossible to control","text":"N1 designers did not have good computers. Chaotic behavior does not mean that system is impossible to control (e.g. double pendulum [VIDEO] )."},{"id":12476108,"author":"imaginenore","month":"2016-09","story":"Neuromorphic computing implementation of pole balancing","stance":"reference","why":"Physical double inverted pendulum on a cart, a harder task","quote":"Double inverted pendulum balancing with a physical cart (2011), a much much harder task","text":"Two-pole balancing (2009): [LINK] Double inverted pendulum balancing (2015), a much harder task: [LINK] Double inverted pendulum balancing with a physical cart (2011), a much much harder task: [VIDEO] Triple!!! inverted pendulum balancing with a physical cart (2011), a much much much harder task: [LINK]"},{"id":8007595,"author":"fjarlq","month":"2014-07","story":"Double Pendulum in fewer than 100 lines of JavaScript","stance":"reference","why":"Example of balancing a double pendulum on a cart","quote":"It'd be fun to write software to balance the double pendulum.","text":"Cool. Feature request: mount the pivot on a programmable cart. It'd be fun to write software to balance the double pendulum. e.g. [VIDEO] and, balancing a triple pendulum: [LINK]"}]},"B9tzoGFszog":{"wy":"HN quotes it jokingly whenever the BBC or government control of media is in the news.","m":[{"id":48299435,"author":"fuoqi","month":"2026-05","story":"Germany Law to Force Algorithm Boost for State-Approved News","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Because it is a \"public broadcast\", so it obviously \"Cannot Give In To Government Pressure\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":45948147,"author":"abtinf","month":"2025-11","story":"Iran begins cloud seeding operations as drought bites","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"there can be no question of the BBC ever giving in to government pressure.","text":"[VIDEO] > But I must make one thing absolutely clear: there can be no question of the BBC ever giving in to government pressure."},{"id":30290273,"author":"FabHK","month":"2022-02","story":"BBC cryptocurrency documentary pulled from air at last minute","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"See the wonderful documentary^W comedy Yes, Minister.","text":"Indeed, the BBC cannot give in to government pressure. It sets great store by factual accuracy. And if the defense of the realm is at stake, it has to be very responsible. See the wonderful documentary^W comedy Yes, Minister. [VIDEO]"}]},"BRqZBuu_Ers":{"wy":"Cited as a real-world experiment showing that banning investors did not lower purchase prices and may have raised rents.","q":{"x":"Rotterdam did just this, and the effect was that the price to purchase a house did not measurably decrease, and that rents rose.","i":37643827,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":42412521,"author":"throw0101d","month":"2024-12","story":"Amid cuts to basic research, New Zealand scraps all support for social sciences","stance":"reference","why":"Example of an experiment banning investor-owned housing","quote":"","text":"> Economics is the worst social science, an economist is never considered wrong, only of a different “school of economics”. Economists speak as if they have authority and as if their field is objective, but at the same time have those “schools”. Is there such a thing as an unideological economist? Economics is falsifiable. For example, a bunch of folks made a prediction (\"currency debasement and inflation\") when the US Federal reserve started QE: > We believe the Federal Reserve’s large-scale asset purchase plan (so-called “quantitative easing”) should be reconsidered and discontinued. We do not believe such a plan is necessary or advisable under current circumstances. The planned asset purchases risk currency debasement and inflation, and we do not think they will achieve the Fed’s objective of promoting employment. * [LINK] A bunch of other folks (e.g., Keynesians like Krugman) made a bunch of prediction as well. One group turned out right, another did not. Another experiment where one group predicted tax cuts would spur growth: * [LINK] Others predicted it would not, and were right. There was also sorts of folks talking that cutting government spending would spur growth, i.e., \"expansionary austerity\": * [LINK] Others predicted it would not, and turned out correct: * [LINK] Amsterdam ended up running a bit of an 'experiment' on what happens when you tried banning houses owned by investors: * [LINK] * [LINK] * Via: [VIDEO] There are \"schools\" that put forward various models"},{"id":37820584,"author":"throw0101c","month":"2023-10","story":"2023 Nobel Prize in economics awarded to Claudia Goldin","stance":"reference","why":"Rotterdam as a real-world economic experiment","quote":"Rotterdam is running an experiment right now with regards to real estate markets and regulation","text":"What was the controllable with Eddington? * [LINK] Einstein's models made a prediction, people 'tested' that prediction in Nature, and it turned out accurate. General theory has made other predictions, and empirical observations have found them to be accurate as well. Plenty of \"real\" science is/was done outside of controlled experiments in a lab. A phenomena was going to happen (e.g., tax law changes): some economic models predicted X would happen, and some predicted Y would. We can see which were more accurate and which were falsified: * [LINK] Rotterdam is running an experiment right now with regards to real estate markets and regulation: * [VIDEO] * [LINK] Every time the Federal Reserve makes a policy change there are people running their models on what will happen, and if their models are right they make a lot of money, and if they're wrong they lose a lot of money: > But why was Gross betting so heavily against Treasuries? Brad DeLong tries to rationalize[1] Gross’s behavior in terms of a coherent story about an impending U.S. recovery, which would lift us out of the liquidity trap. But Gross wasn’t saying anything like that. Instead, he was claiming that the Fed’s asset purchases — QE2 — were holding rates down, and warned that the impending spike in rates when QE2 ended would derail recovery. > So why did he believe all that? It all comes down, I’d argue, to liquidity trap denial. > Since 2008 the basic logic of the economic situation has been that the private sector "},{"id":37643827,"author":"ryukafalz","month":"2023-09","story":"Baby boomers are becoming homeless at a rate not seen since the Great Depression","stance":"reference","why":"Rotterdam banned housing investors with mixed results","quote":"Rotterdam did just this, and the effect was that the price to purchase a house did not measurably decrease, and that rents rose.","text":"> But other than zoning it seems it would help more if the government would step in to disallow large leasing companies buying up giant swathes of the housing stock just to flip into rentals. There was a timely video about this yesterday from Oh the Urbanity: [VIDEO] Summary is that Rotterdam did just this, and the effect was that the price to purchase a house did not measurably decrease, and that rents rose. The neighborhoods regulated in this way had fewer immigrants and skewed wealthier than those that were not. So, not the effect we're looking for."}]},"D2SoGHFM18I":{"wy":"HN commenters cite the footage as anecdotal evidence in threads about animals sensing the Earth's magnetic field, and for the head-tilt listening behavior.","q":{"x":"the fox is much more accurate when diving when facing north.","i":6998389,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":29034571,"author":"mojomark","month":"2021-10","story":"Why do dogs tilt their heads? New study offers clues","stance":"reference","why":"Footage of a canine tilting its head to listen.","quote":"Watch [1] @ 1:08.","text":"Watch [1] @ 1:08. Is it really a mystery as to why canines tilt their heads when spoken to? It's always seemed self-evident that they are hearing a sound that is of interest and they are evaluating to see if it's useful information. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22277578,"author":"Xylakant","month":"2020-02","story":"Dogs poop in alignment with Earth’s magnetic field, study finds (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"Fox hunting footage cited as evidence of magnetic alignment.","quote":"Snow foxes seem to hunt better when oriented in direction of the magnetic north.","text":"> any plausible reason for a non-migrating animal to align with the magnetic field, when defecating or at any other time. Snow foxes seem to hunt better when oriented in direction of the magnetic north. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":6998389,"author":"cdeonier","month":"2014-01","story":"Dogs are sensitive to small variations of the Earth's magnetic field","stance":"reference","why":"Fox diving into snow, cited as magnetic sensitivity example.","quote":"the fox is much more accurate when diving when facing north.","text":"I remember seeing a show remarking on similar sensitivity being observed in foxes when hunting mice in the snow. Apparently, the fox is much more accurate when diving when facing north. [VIDEO]"}]},"DXUOWXidcY0":{"wy":"Cited in StarCraft AI threads to show how much perfect micro matters, though one commenter finds such play boring.","q":{"x":"anyone who says that advantage in micromanagement is not a big thing should see \"Automaton 2000\" videos","i":11325855,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12895260,"author":"nickpsecurity","month":"2016-11","story":"Show HN: DeepRhyme (D-Prime) – Generating dope rhymes with machine learning","stance":"reference","why":"Example of bot micro","quote":"","text":"It's possible but it's a lot more context-driven than you think with a human involved. The bots do great against each other with their strategies and muscle-memory stuff. Throw a human in the mix, they start noticing patterns in how the units are managed, what strategies are likely, etc. They've exploited these in the competitions to hilarious effect. Here's the main site on prior work & results at the competitions: [LINK] Here's a few examples of how the muscle-memory approach, esp if focused on unit vs unit, can fail against humans. \" In this example, Bakuryu (human) notices that Skynet's units will chase his zerglings if they are near, and proceeds to run around Skynet's base to distract Skynet long enough so that Bakuryu can make fying units to come attack Skynet's base. This type of behaviour is incredibly hard to detect in a bot, since it requires knowledge of the larger context of the game which may only have consequences 5 or more minutes in the future. \" (2013) Note: At this point, they also sucked at building and expansion strategies which surprised me since I thought a basic planner would be able to do that. The constraints between rate of expansion, where to place stuff, what units to keep/build, and so on get really hard. The other thing they weren't good at was switching strategies mid-game based on what opponents are doing. \" despite Djem5 (pro) making the bots look silly this year... they were able to defeat D-ranked, and even some C-ranked players. After the "},{"id":12498687,"author":"pkfrank","month":"2016-09","story":"Deep Reinforcement Learning to Play StarCraft","stance":"reference","why":"Marines split perfectly against banelings","quote":"Pretty cool.","text":"Wow, I hadn't seen this before. Here is \"Automaton 2000\" controlling 20 marines vs 40 banelings, without losing a single unit. [VIDEO] Pretty cool."},{"id":11325855,"author":"Monory","month":"2016-03","story":"DeepMind Challenges for StarCraft","stance":"reference","why":"Bot with perfect micro beats banelings with marines","quote":"anyone who says that advantage in micromanagement is not a big thing should see \"Automaton 2000\" videos","text":"Well, anyone who says that advantage in micromanagement is not a big thing should see \"Automaton 2000\" videos. Given that automaton is a map script and thus can have virtually unlimited APM, it beats people from a horribly disadvantageous positions (40 banelings vs 21 marine [1], or 100 zerglings vs 20 sieged tanks [2]). [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":16882126,"author":"AmericanChopper","month":"2018-04","story":"StarCraft Micromanagement with Reinforcement Learning and Curriculum Transfer","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"A 'perfectly' micro-ing AI is both boring to implement and boring to play against.","text":"A 'perfectly' micro-ing AI is both boring to implement and boring to play against. Somebody made one for SC2 back when that was popular, take a look at these videos to see how uninteresting it would become after the first attempt. [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO]"}]},"EGTRX6pZSns":{"wy":"HN uses it as the standard debunking to cite whenever a new water-from-air device is announced.","q":{"x":"The TLDW version is these devices are fancy dehumidifiers.","i":30719875,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33201965,"author":"RaiausderDose","month":"2022-10","story":"White House is pushing ahead research to cool Earth by reflecting back sunlight","stance":"reference","why":"More on energy cost of air water harvesters","quote":"More about it here","text":"These dehumidifiers produce half a liter a day and need around 72 watt which is maybe 1€ per liter drinking water for my area The tech is cool but not really new or a solution to all water problems, the energy cost is relatively high. More about it here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":48498963,"author":"jojobas","month":"2026-06","story":"This Jacket Pulls Drinking Water from Thin Air","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I believe everything in this video still applies.","text":"You can wear silica gel since about 1918 - only needs some heat to get the water out and cold to condense it. Then again, why would you want to wear your dehumidifier (ok ok water harvester)? Is it for excursions into damp areas, so that you can then return to your dry home to extract water? Then, I believe everything in this video still applies. [VIDEO]"},{"id":48499735,"author":"jojobas","month":"2026-06","story":"This Jacket Pulls Drinking Water from Thin Air","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"It is a dessicant dehumidifier, useless for the same reason","text":"It is a dessicant dehumidifier, useless for the same reason as this MIT/Berkley thing from 9 years ago. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30719875,"author":"torginus","month":"2022-03","story":"Google open sources solar atmospheric water generator","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The TLDW version is these devices are fancy dehumidifiers.","text":"None. Or alternatively, gathering Reddit/HN karma or channeling NGO funds into overpriced useless science projects instead of helping people. Thunderf00t has made a ton of videos about devices like this, for example: [VIDEO] The TLDW version is these devices are fancy dehumidifiers. They need a ton of power, and lots of air humidity to be practical, which are exactly not the conditions these devices are advertised to operate in. And even then, the water they produce is dirty, so it needs to be purified and treated to be actually drinkable. Even if we solve all these challenges, it turns out that doing something dumb and low tech, like transporting drinking water on trucks is actually a lot more efficient. This is another one in the long list of devices designed to help those hypothetical poor people, like OLPC and the Gravity Light. It turns out these people have already solved these issues, and much better, usually with off-the-shelf alternatives and a bit of ingenuity."},{"id":28064221,"author":"imglorp","month":"2021-08","story":"Spanish engineers extract drinking water from thin air","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Thunderf00t has debunked a bunch of other enterprises doing water from the air","text":"Nope, there's no magic or free lunch. It depends heavily on local conditions which might not always be suitable. Thunderf00t has debunked a bunch of other enterprises doing water from the air: [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] etc"},{"id":28068155,"author":"Confiks","month":"2021-08","story":"Spanish engineers extract drinking water from thin air","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"He declares it a hoax, but ignores pretty much everything from the paper.","text":"Thanks for the links! Mostly scams and very bad science journalism indeed. However, he completely misses the mark in his video about the Science paper \"Water harvesting from air with metal-organic frameworks powered by natural sunlight\" [1]. He declares it a hoax, but ignores pretty much everything from the paper. Surprisingly, far down the comment section, there is a comment explaining that [2]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20873558,"author":"aaron695","month":"2019-09","story":"Crystalline nets harvest water from desert air, turn CO2 into liquid fuel","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"You can't get water from air in deserts or places needing water of any practical, non military use","text":"> Crystalline nets harvest water from desert air, turn carbon dioxide into liquid fuel It's like we are stacking crazy, onto crazy, onto crazy. You can't get water from air in deserts or places needing water of any practical, non military use - Here's a start - [VIDEO] We don't need to turn CO2 into fuel, just make fuel/energy without fossil fuels that's also used, and you're solving global warming in a amazing way. It's worth trillions, so it's always AMAZING to see some school kid or amateur scientist or researcher who just needs a little more research beat the multi nationals. It's a shame the multi nationals are to dumb to not hire school kids and these smart ideas people. Just shitty people who spend years studying at university then years working in the industry doing hard work to gradually make change."}]},"EHqPrHTN1dU":{"wy":"HN mentions it mainly as an example of Google Japan's habit of making joke keyboards, with some complaints that it was misleading.","m":[{"id":45506858,"author":"GauntletWizard","month":"2025-10","story":"Google Japan keyboard design switches keys for dials","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Google Japan has a long history of humorous keyboards","text":"Not only that, but Google Japan has a long history of humorous keyboards, such as the \"Double Sided\" keyboard: [VIDEO] , the \"Single Row\" keyboard: [LINK] and the Spoon Bending keyboard: [LINK] It's been their running April Fools joke for years and they've upped the rate to every six months."},{"id":43217011,"author":"gamedever","month":"2025-03","story":"Japanese Toshiba Typewriter Model BW-2112 (2020) [video]","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Google Japan makes joke keyboards.","text":"tangentially, Google Japan makes joke keyboards. Here's their latest [VIDEO] you can see some others here [LINK] They had one with 1000+ keys but I don't see a video for it"},{"id":42000532,"author":"Cthulhu_","month":"2024-10","story":"M4 MacBook Pro","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"at least with Google there's still room for some comedy in some of their departments","text":"If only in some shots, but they are such a valuable company that they simply cannot afford the risk of e.g. criticism for the choice of people they display, or inappropriate outfits or behaviour. One blip from a shareholder can cost them billions in value, which pisses off other shareholders. All of their published media, from videos like this to their conferences, are highly polished, rehearsed, and designed by committee. Microsoft and Google are the same, although at least with Google there's still room for some comedy in some of their departments: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41786207,"author":"mazsa","month":"2024-10","story":"Circuit diagrams and firmware source code for Gboard DIY keyboards","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"This is misleading customers","text":"This is misleading customers: they are trying to sell a (single-sided) Moebius keyboard as a double-sided one [VIDEO]"}]},"GiPe1OiKQuk":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as the source of the known/unknown unknowns framing, sometimes noting he was unfairly ridiculed for it.","q":{"x":"He was ridiculed for talking about","i":9292041,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":13342148,"author":"danso","month":"2017-01","story":"The Art of Pitching: How I Got Published in The Atlantic","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"No, taking a quote of context is not the same as not running a quote with the source prior to publication. The former is unethical, the second is an optional practice. I dont't run quotes by sources when I know that I have recorded it correctly and when I feel certain of the speaker's intent and context. Most photojournalists don't let the subjects see the photos prior to publication. Not just in the past days of film, when it would have been logistically difficult, but even today with digital cameras. Most subjects have strong opinions about what photos capture them nicely. But photographers generally believe that photos capture truth, at least in a limited technical sense. edit: It's worth throwing in a hypothetical cases based on real quotes. Pretend these happened in a private interview that you have taped: - Case 1: \"Rarely is the question asked, is our children learning?\" [0] If you were not a journalist sympathetic to President Bush, you would print this quote ad verbatim (minus the stutter before \"is\"), because it fits a narrative that Bush is a doofus, even when speaking about our children's education. I think if you ran this by him, Bush would politely request that \"is\" be changed to \"are\". But including the quote verbatim isn't a distortion of the truth, it's literally what's on tape. - Case 2: \"It depends on what the meaning of is is\" [1] Here's a case where a neutral reporter might ask for clarification, because the reporter is genuinely confused. However, I thin"},{"id":12996613,"author":"DanielBMarkham","month":"2016-11","story":"Alien life could be so advanced it becomes indistinguishable from physics","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Epistemological point: if from the modern world you dropped in on Christopher Columbus with all your gadgets and tried to explain them, it might sound like magic. If from the modern world you dropped in on a hominid 100k years ago in his cave with all your gadgets and tried to explain them? You'd be a god. Things that exist outside of our ability to have language to describe them are godlike. (There is an open question about whether the set of such things is irreducible or open to conquest at some point in time) If the caveman trapped you and tried to figure out what you were up to? Yes, to him it would be physics. However -- this is just another way of saying that he'd have no symbols to grasp your behavior and would be forced to create various mental models that he could share with others. Donald Rumsfeld famously talked about knowns and unknowns[1]. But there is another dimension of things we have no symbology to begin discussing. There be dragons. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":9292041,"author":"aero142","month":"2015-03","story":"Choose Boring Technology","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"He was ridiculed for talking about","text":"He was ridiculed for talking about \"unknown unknowns\" because of the ridiculous phrasing. See [VIDEO] Interestingly, this ridicule became so common that now people know more about known unknowns, and unknown unknows, which is perfectly sound logic, but just weirdly associated with Rumsfeld now."}]},"H110vCGvTmM":{"wy":"HN mentions it mostly as an amusing look at flat earthers in their own words, not as a recommendation.","m":[{"id":24769882,"author":"tehjoker","month":"2020-10","story":"Norway officially accuses Russia for cyberattacks on parliament","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"they were happy to talk to the guy from All Gas No Brakes on camera.","text":"Flat earth started from YouTube recommendations. They had a whole non-annonymous conference where they were happy to talk to the guy from All Gas No Brakes on camera. [VIDEO] Pizzagate and QAnon started because many people understand something is fucked up but lack the correct tools to understand the systemic problems of society (of which you and I would probably disagree as I blame capitalism and imperialism) so they invent conspiracies to explain the world around them. In many cases, their conservative, libertarian, or liberal ideology does not permit them to critique the systems around them, so they must find some other way to explain the situation, which revolves around (non-system challenging) elite conspiracies. It's difficult to disabuse people of the fake conspiracies because real conspiracies against the public are routine. Some are boring (such as the SV conspiracy to depress software engineer wages). Some are exciting, such as what the CIA is publicly documented to have done overseas by selling cocaine to fund their black operations in the Iran-Contra scandal. Also, there was a kernel of truth to Pizzagate and QAnon. Look at the Clinton's relationship to Jeffery Epstein. However, the right was unable to metabolize real existing issues and started fantasizing about a fascist coup executed by the president (whom they say rejected Epstein after he found out what he was up to... but didn't do anything about it). They make up concepts like he's tagging and bagging holly"},{"id":29973220,"author":"mrtesthah","month":"2022-01","story":"In defense of flat earthers (2020)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"The Jews[1], apparently. Somehow when you follow any conspiracy theory for long enough you find antisemitism at the root of it. 1. Flat Earth Conference, All Gas No Breaks: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29977050,"author":"Cthulhu_","month":"2022-01","story":"In defense of flat earthers (2020)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Here's a neat video where flat earthers spout their Truth","text":"Here's a neat video where flat earthers spout their Truth and reveal themselves to actually be antisemites: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27865908,"author":"saltminer","month":"2021-07","story":"Google Drive bans distribution of “misleading content”","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":">People are much nicer when their reputation is at stake. I think a more accurate statement would be \"People are much nicer when their money is at stake.\" And I don't mean \"nicer\" as in \"genuinely better,\" it's more \"I'll paint on a smile and not say anything bad\" (just ask a waiter and they'll have plenty of tales where they had to do this for a tip). Today, there are plenty of online hangouts for people with all sorts of ideas. This is great since people can easily form communities around a TV show, hobby, etc, but it also enables flat earthers and anti-vaxxers, whose views are often rooted in bigotry (see: All Gas No Brakes video on the flat earth convention [0]). Those communities tend to encourage an \"us vs them\" mentality and to cut out those who seek to \"hold them back\" (basically modern cults - alienate yourself from your friends and loved ones, we will provide all the community you need). In the past, joining groups like the Klan was a much more difficult endeavor (they tended to operate much more in the shadows), and the groups tended to be on the smaller side. Today, it's just a matter of joining a Facebook group about \"race realism\", \"the truth about George Floyd\", or whatever, and bam, you have access to thousands of like-minded individuals to build your own personal echo chamber. The traditional tactics of people avoiding those they dislike IRL don't work so well as a form of collective shaming when you've got someone who is terminally online and has tons of peo"}]},"HynsTvRVLiI":{"wy":"Mostly mentioned as a joke or as an example of using humor to turn a problem into publicity.","m":[{"id":25226337,"author":"tomcooks","month":"2020-11","story":"Fucking, Austria changes name to Fugging","stance":"reference","why":"Example of using humor to turn a problem into publicity","quote":"Instead of a hissy fit, use humor to point at a problem.","text":"Considering you cannot educate English speaking idiots, make a living out of them: Based on social media analysis see when there's a \"high season\" of idiots Buy domain fuckingaustria.com or something more clever. Get 100 tshirts with a town sign illustration, bumper stickers, mugs, wool hats and jumpers (which will be worn across all Europe by EU roaming English speakers on road tours), a roadside market permit, sell them and on the side offer informative pamphlets and mailing list subscriptions about the town and what locals do. Rinse and repeat while pushing what Fucking really is about, always with a deadpan take that leaves wondering wether you're serious or not. It's what New Zealand's president did with the country not being shown on several maps. Instead of a hissy fit, use humor to point at a problem. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24847348,"author":"delineator","month":"2020-10","story":"Show HN: TidalWaves API – live, tokenized news metadata from around the world","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Asking on behalf of Rhys Darby.","text":"Is New Zealand left off TidalWaves' world map by design, or by accident? Asking on behalf of Rhys Darby. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20490694,"author":"rgoulter","month":"2019-07","story":"Why wasn't this page found?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Jacinda Ardern featured in a tourist ad.","text":"Also, current Prime Minister Jacinda Ardern featured in a tourist ad. [VIDEO] (Though AFAIK it was reddit which popularised the joke first)"}]},"KO8L9tKR4CY":{"wy":"Cited as real footage of someone surviving brief vacuum exposure.","q":{"x":"He lost consciousness in less than 15 seconds, but fully recovered after depressurization.","i":19657423,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":19657423,"author":"darkpuma","month":"2019-04","story":"Proliferation of hydrocarbon-degrading microbes at the bottom of Mariana Trench","stance":"reference","why":"Footage of man surviving accidental vacuum chamber depressurization","quote":"He lost consciousness in less than 15 seconds, but fully recovered after depressurization.","text":"NASA once accidentally depressurized a man while testing space suits in a vacuum chamber. He lost consciousness in less than 15 seconds, but fully recovered after depressurization. He reported feeling his saliva boil which is... exciting. [VIDEO] [LINK] During Project Excelsior, Joseph Kittinger's right hand was depressurized at 100,000 feet and swelled up, but he was ultimately unharmed. [LINK]"},{"id":10821387,"author":"HCIdivision17","month":"2016-01","story":"Survival in Space Unprotected Is Possible, Briefly (2008)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I think this is the actual study: [LINK] I fundamentally dissagree that it was trivial, but I worked on large vacuum systems and feel a bit close to the topic. But I also fundamentally agree that the experimentation was barbaric and cruel. But... I also don't see how we could have worked around it. I hope it's some consolation that they never doubly-exposed the dogs; I'm actually surprised that wasn't attempted. Unshielded hard vacuum is horrific. The problem isn't that it's awful - that's obvious - but exactly how does it affect the body? (I've sealed a hole with my hand and got a hickey, but I've never been immersed in it.) Even today, studying things in a vacuum is difficult; it's always behind a think layer of material that's hard to observe through, and even with cleverness you're testing through the neck of a bottle or only looking back on data logged by some gizmo. And there are a lot of confounding variables; narrowing the time-to-unconciousness/death was the most critical, and each test batch needed multiple samples to make sure no data was wasted. One note that came out was that it seems clearly better to recompress with pure oxygen; would that fire hazard be worth the risk in space (especially after Apollo 1), or would they need to design a mechanism to temporarily mange it? I could see that argument having a significant impact on the cabin design. But yeah. We can do better. A damn sight better. We can illuminate and record and monitor so much better. A couple yea"},{"id":10819055,"author":"curtis","month":"2015-12","story":"Survival in Space Unprotected Is Possible, Briefly (2008)","stance":"reference","why":"Film of NASA technician surviving accidental depressurization","quote":"There's a YouTube video describing the accident which includes film of the accident itself","text":"There was a notable incident in the 1960s where a NASA spacesuit technician named Jim LeBlanc survived an accidental (but brief) depressurization. There's a YouTube video describing the accident which includes film of the accident itself: [VIDEO] ."}]},"OYecfV3ubP8":{"wy":"HN mostly uses it as a cultural reference, either as a favorite tech ad or as irony when criticizing Apple's later restrictiveness.","q":{"x":"The Apple 1984 commercial is my favorite","i":461049,"y":"2009"},"m":[{"id":14538200,"author":"0xdeadbeefbabe","month":"2017-06","story":"Gopher-OS: A Proof of Concept OS Written in Go","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Why care about OS:es when there is no room for anyone on that level of abstraction apart from a handful developers at MS, Apple and Google? There is as much room as there are people frustrated with the \"state of the art.\" Edit: Have you seen this apple commercial [VIDEO] . Yes it is from 1984 and you might think it doesn't apply."},{"id":1162481,"author":"jmatt","month":"2010-03","story":"Apple Sues HTC for Patent Infringement","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"For those you who weren't around in 1984... Or didn't pay attention in high school english or decades old super bowl ads. Apple's commercial: [VIDEO] And the obligatory wiki link: [LINK]"},{"id":461049,"author":"sarvesh","month":"2009-02","story":"Ask HN: Favorite Technology Company Superbowl Ads?","stance":"reference","why":"Favorite tech company Super Bowl ad.","quote":"The Apple 1984 commercial is my favorite","text":"The Apple 1984 commercial is my favorite [VIDEO] you are releasing something as significant as the Macintosh I think it's too much money."},{"id":301979,"author":"froo","month":"2008-09","story":"Apple applies for patent on \"sneakers\" DRM","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"You sir, are awesome, I would give you triple karma if I could. For those who don't \"get\" the quote, it's the infamous 1984 Apple ad, directed by Ridley Scott. Links below for reference [VIDEO] [LINK] (television_commercial)"},{"id":1085017,"author":"mdg","month":"2010-01","story":" On the iPad","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"The future of personal computing that the iPad shows us is both seductive and dystopian. Upon reading that, I was immediately reminded of this: [VIDEO] minus the sledge."},{"id":982967,"author":"sparky","month":"2009-12","story":"Things I learned from iPhone and Droid ads","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Hopefully this was intended as humor (and it was funny), and not as a straight-faced critique. It is not news that different styles of ad campaign can be effective; Apple has launched a fair few smarmy (Get a Mac [LINK] ) or fanciful (1984 [VIDEO] ) salvos at the competition itself. What I learned from the 1984 ad: The CEOs of all Apple competitors have monkey-like visages and enjoy sitting very close to the camera as they brainwash the world. The Macintosh makes you proficient at the hammer throw. Incidentally, it also turns you into a woman with orange shorts."},{"id":838491,"author":"jhancock","month":"2009-09","story":"Our app got rejected again. We're trying to figure out why.","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"Four legs good, two legs bad!\" [VIDEO] sorry if you feel I'm slightly mixing metaphors. Sometimes I feel like I'm chatting in a forum of frogs in a pot of rising heat."},{"id":416306,"author":"tlrobinson","month":"2009-01","story":"Happy New 1984","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Coincidentally, I'm reading the book at the moment (again). Is the movie any good? And of course, don't forget the epic Macintosh ad: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4536796,"author":"frugalmail","month":"2012-09","story":"Samsung Galaxy S4 due in February","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'm in America, an American, and not Asian. I find what apple is doing repulsive. - They are using highly questionable patents to discourage competition, are ruling their ecosystem like a fascist regime, - forcing you to swallow their whole stack to work with any part of their ecosystem (e.g. you can only build ios apps on mac) - their bundled applications suck (damn the \"rainbow wait wheel\" - making disposable laptops, ipods, and iphones which is counter to the environment and very anti-consumer but pro apple pocketbook. And all this is especially funny when you look back: [VIDEO] Although that article is definitely not substantiating their bias it's just a shame that brand marketing here has so clearly pulled the proverbial wool over the American public's eyes."},{"id":4124602,"author":"trustfundbaby","month":"2012-06","story":"Why are Apple laptops becoming harder to take apart?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"What I don't understand is why this is an either-or proposition ... was it really that impossible to build these laptops without alienating the 'enthusiasts' ... people who actually want to swap out hard drives, or RAM? I just spent two years transitioning my life away from windows, which I used for about 15 years, to OS X just to suddenly find that I can't run out and buy a new SSD hard drive to try on my system if I wanted to ... brilliant. The best part is, If reading HN threads where people are basically agreeing with this nonsense, because Apple made the decision. Thats right, just keep taking away choices from the user until the entire company and its customers become one huge parody. oh, hang on ... we're already there. yuck. PS: [VIDEO]"},{"id":1252679,"author":"vaksel","month":"2010-04","story":"Apple takes stance on consciousness","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Apple lately has been reminding me of what they were supposedly railing about in that 1984 commercial.","text":"Apple lately has been reminding me of what they were supposedly railing about in that 1984 commercial. [VIDEO]"},{"id":1271914,"author":"elblanco","month":"2010-04","story":"App Store Bans Pulitzer-Winning Satirist. For Satire","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"> The whole hammer throwing thing from the 80s just seems so ironic now. [VIDEO] I'm not even sure how to reinterpret it today. The Big Brother figure speaks of a garden of pure ideology, and it appears the woman is an app who is about to get rejected by the app store cops. Is the meaning of the hammer throw that I should go get an Android phone so I don't have to subject myself to the approval of the Big Brother character?"},{"id":838484,"author":"jhancock","month":"2009-09","story":"Our app got rejected again. We're trying to figure out why.","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"1984\" Apple ad. [VIDEO] It really does not matter if you think they are right or wrong. Watch the vid and decide if that's what you want to attach your startup fortunes to. I can understand why some funds would have been motivated to focus on iPhone apps last year. At this point, I wouldn't touch an iPhone startup without an ironclad insider contract with Apple."}]},"RnofCyaWhI0":{"wy":"HN cites it as footage of what thruster-driven interceptors look like, including as a mundane comparison for odd-looking motion in UFO videos.","m":[{"id":41144090,"author":"samstave","month":"2024-08","story":"Magnetically levitated space elevator to low-earth orbit (2001) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"What would the mass of said asteroid have to be to accommodate such a design? Maybe you could have a HALO of THORNS (Telemetry homogeneous orbital restrainer nano-lattice stabilizer) - that have 13 satellite thrusters that can maintain the alignment of the nano-pillars... and have them sectionaly distributed as rings on the Z -- with tethered lattice of tubes tying it all together like a Chinese finger trap. [VIDEO] <-- These things... [LINK] and the carbon lattice is also a fuel-capillary system - that feed like a live thing..."},{"id":31108916,"author":"mgdlbp","month":"2022-04","story":"HUDS and GUIs: an inspiration resource site featuring Future User Interfaces","stance":"reference","why":"Missile interceptor reaction control thrusters in action","quote":"like those from missile interceptors","text":"The realistic detail I remember from that movie is that the drones had plausible-looking reaction control thrusters (like those from missile interceptors [1]). There's a behind-the-scenes video that mentions the idea and has some (spoiling) shots from the film. [2] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] == Tangential physics nitpick == In a scene (spoilers, [3]) where a hovering drone is firing its side-mounted autocannon turrets, the reaction thruster animation doesn't match what's needed to counter the torque from the recoil: 1. The drone begins by firing both port and starboard turrets forward--no torque from recoil here, only backwards force that is countered by the drone's gimballed main thruster. 2. The drone ceases firing its starboard turret to slew it towards a target directly behind it. Of the aft-facing reaction thrusters visible in the shot, only the starboard ones are firing. But this would exacerbate the torque of the still-firing port turret instead of countering it! 3. The starboard turret finishes targeting and begins firing aft, but the reaction thruster animation remains unchanged. Of course, this level of detail already far exceeds expectations, considering that this all happens in less than a second, and that there's no impact on viewing experience when fast-moving effects don't stand up to frame-by-frame scrubbing. [3] [LINK]"},{"id":30711178,"author":"bally0241","month":"2022-03","story":"Famous Navy UFO video was camera glare, evidence suggests","stance":"reference","why":"Kill vehicle hover tests show mundane explanation for UFO motion","quote":"this video shows SDI technology from the 80s","text":"There are plenty of \"mundane\" explanation for the motion of the object to be real as well, and not just camera glare. No need to invoke aliens. For example, this video shows SDI technology from the 80s: [VIDEO] which appears perfectly capable (albeit in test) of in-place rotation/motion very similar to that of the object. It could very well have been a drone, but I find it incredible that people dismiss first hand accounts in preference to some internet armchair quarterback."}]},"S8MAV9jhf04":{"wy":"Posted as a joke in threads about VR hardware, failed consumer tech, and GenAI hype.","m":[{"id":48422653,"author":"jldugger","month":"2026-06","story":"What was your \"Oh Shit\" moment with GenAI?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> But then I told it that I wanted to leave, got to an inn, where I flirted with the bar waitress, and soon we were watching the sunset in some meadow. Immediate Silicon Valley vibes: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33174465,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2022-10","story":"Meta Quest Pro","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"You earned my upvote with the PKD 3SOPA reference. The Three Stigmata of Palmer Luckey: an evil alt-right-wing extremist pulling the wool over people’s eyes with his robotic right hand, artificial eyes, and steel teeth, who returned from an expedition to the Prox system in possession of a new alien hallucinogen Chew-Z to compete with Can-D, to perpetrate an uncanny creepy plot about adults on drugs playing together with physical miniaturized doll houses in order to escape their dystopian existence by retreating into to a mega-corporation controlled virtual reality. [LINK] Silicon Valley Keenan Feldspar's VR (S4E8) [VIDEO] Silicon Valley (HBO) - HooliVR Goggles (Phone Exploding) S04E09 [LINK]"},{"id":22947760,"author":"geerlingguy","month":"2020-04","story":"Magic Leap reportedly slashes jobs and steps away from consumer plans","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Reminds me of Silicon Valley's fictional Keenan Feldspar and his VR demo (likely based on Magic Leap): [VIDEO]"}]},"_47utWAoupo":{"wy":"HN cites it as an example of the limits of driver-assist safety systems, often with the caveat that the pedestrian-detection option was not fitted.","m":[{"id":12543737,"author":"Animats","month":"2016-09","story":"Self-Driving Cars Must Meet 15 Benchmarks in U.S. Guidance","stance":"reference","why":"Volvo demo crash where the pedestrian safety option wasn't purchased.","quote":"There was an accident with a Volvo where someone was showing off the pedestrian safety system, and hit a pedestrian.","text":"The NHTSA is hinting in that direction, indicating that manufacturers must clearly distinguish between driver assistance systems (levels 1 and 2) and real self driving (levels 3 - 5). There will probably be some standard on this. There has to be; consider what happens when car rental fleets start having some of these features. There was an accident with a Volvo where someone was showing off the pedestrian safety system, and hit a pedestrian. It turned out they hadn't purchased the pedestrian safety system.[1] Something is needed to prevent problems like that. Then there's mode switching trouble. Classic problem with aircraft control systems. Tesla disengages the \"autopilot\" if the driver touches the brake. The trouble is that this also disables automatic braking, as the driver is assumed to now be in control. So tapping the brake without applying it fully in a hazardous situation causes a crash. All these driver behavior problems with shared control authority are hard. Maybe harder than going for level 3 and letting the automation do it. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12120089,"author":"ploxiln","month":"2016-07","story":"It’s No 'Accident:' Advocates Want to Speak of Car 'Crashes' Instead","stance":"reference","why":"Volvo auto-brake failure video as example of safety tech limits.","quote":"You can try to save people from themselves, but it's never quite as effective as you expect.","text":"You can try to save people from themselves, but it's never quite as effective as you expect. [VIDEO] Imagine the ridiculous driving when people \"know their car will stop before a crash anyway\". Yeah, more people will be saved overall... but there will be a lot of \"funny\" results too. Personally, I'd rather have strict penalties than being forced to do the equivalent of trading a linux laptop for an iPad Pro just so I can't hurt myself or anyone else. (I drive a stick shift of course, and can turn off traction control :)"},{"id":17126554,"author":"John_KZ","month":"2018-05","story":"Boeing's folding wingtips get the FAA green light","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Neither does Volvo, apparently","text":">Cars brake themselves now Uber clearly doesn't follow this trend. Neither does Volvo, apparently [VIDEO] >Pipers have parachutes Only because they're prone to a certain unrecoverable spin in some configurations. You can get a ballistic chute on a Cessna too if you like. >The Cessna 172 is a very old design That's because it's a great design, and one that has been tested for 50 years. I'd take it over modern ones. >Surviving an accident due to going too slow to die isn't \"safe\". It is if you don't die."}]},"chPanW0QWhA":{"wy":"Cited as the reference example of a real fast legged robot, often to distinguish it from MIT's Cheetah.","m":[{"id":10028532,"author":"minot","month":"2015-08","story":"Japanese mini Segway “WalkCar”","stance":"reference","why":"Cheetah robot peaks at over 29 mph","quote":"that can keep up with the pace peaking at over 29 miles per hour","text":"Is this where Boston Robotics comes in? For heavy load, they have wild cat[wildcat] and for lighter loads, they have spot [spot]. For Ussain Bolt, there is the cheetah [cheetah] that can keep up with the pace peaking at over 29 miles per hour. All these are at various stages in development and as far as I understand not ready for production. spot [LINK] wildcat [LINK] cheetah [VIDEO]"},{"id":9626452,"author":"vlasev","month":"2015-05","story":"MIT cheetah robot lands the running jump [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Non-staggered running strains the robot at speed","quote":"You can practically feel the second robot nearly tear itself apart even at lower speeds.","text":"I also forgot to point out that their robot is capable of running in a staggered manner [1] and the results are much cleaner than non-staggered action [2]. You can practically feel the second robot nearly tear itself apart even at lower speeds. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9626815,"author":"Animats","month":"2015-05","story":"MIT cheetah robot lands the running jump [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Clarifies this is Boston Dynamics' Cheetah, not MIT's","quote":"That's not Boston Dynamics' Cheetah. This is.","text":"That's not Boston Dynamics' Cheetah. This is.[1] This new video is MIT's cheetah. It's good to see MIT doing serious legged locomotion work again. Things stalled out there after Raibert left and Gil Pratt took over. Looks like DARPA is funding MIT, now that Google bought Boston Dynamics and stopped taking DoD contracts. Not sure why they chose a pronk gait, except that they're doing all straight line work. A gallop has more turn options, but roll control is easier for the pronk. Hopping over an obstacle has been done before, by some of the early MIT planar bipeds.[2] It took about $125 million in DoD funding to get a usable Big Dog and Atlas. It's quite possible to do a lot more in legged robotics, but it is not yet cheap. It's still not clear if Google will achieve commercial legged robots, or decide to \"put more wood behind fewer arrows\" again and focus on their core business, advertising. The commercial payoff is a long way ahead. Academic projects rarely have the funding and staff to get beyond the demo level. You need some big assets, like machine shops and skilled machinists. Note that they're borrowing MIT's gym. To get real work done, you need full time access to your own test area. It's hard to get that on a college campus. The typical academic robotics project is one professor and three grad students. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":5826334,"author":"incision","month":"2013-06","story":"Miniature Quadruped Robot Is Blazingly Fast","stance":"reference","why":"Boston Dynamics cheetah as true legged robot example","quote":"something more like Boston Dynamics cheetah","text":"Well, the article at least acknowledges how questionable this application of the term is. \"Whether or not this really is a legged robot (or a quadruped) is perhaps debatable: these are wheel-legs, more commonly known as whegs.\" I was certainly expecting/hoping for something more like Boston Dynamics cheetah [0]. 0: [VIDEO]"}]},"psL_5RIBqnY":{"wy":"HN links it as the unedited primary source, for instance for the booing at the monitor stand price and for Craig Federighi's explanation of Find My.","q":{"x":"About two full minutes of Craig explaining it.","i":28712681,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28712681,"author":"judge2020","month":"2021-09","story":"Always-on Processor magic: How Find My works while iPhone is powered off","stance":"reference","why":"Craig explains Find My offline location over two minutes.","quote":"About two full minutes of Craig explaining it.","text":"[VIDEO] > Now, Find My combines Find My iPhone with Find My Friends... And it has a new twist because it can now even locate Apple devices that are offline... Let's say you have misplaced your MacBook. So, even when it's offline and sleeping, it sends out a secure Bluetooth beacon that can be detected by other people's Apple devices nearby. Now, they can relay your MacBook's location to the network and ultimately back to you so you can find it. Now, what's amazing is that this whole interaction is end to end encrypted and anonymous. About two full minutes of Craig explaining it."},{"id":20189741,"author":"czr","month":"2019-06","story":"Apple strips clips of devs booing $999 monitor stand using copyright claims","stance":"reference","why":"Full unedited keynote including the moment in question.","quote":"Apple includes the full WWDC keynote, unedited, on their YouTube channel","text":"For anyone who want it: Apple includes the full WWDC keynote, unedited, on their YouTube channel, including the moment in question (at [VIDEO] )"},{"id":20891399,"author":"AceJohnny2","month":"2019-09","story":"Apple Music Web Client","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Uses 50MB less memory as the sole tab in Firefox than iTunes does on my Mac. Well that's because iTunes also includes Calendar, and Mail, and Safari... ;) [VIDEO]"}]},"qvNLNl7oJnM":{"wy":"Cited as a reference in threads on telomeres, lifespan extension and longevity research funding, mainly for the nutrigenomics and epigenetics material.","m":[{"id":11001889,"author":"KasianFranks","month":"2016-01","story":"Ask HN: What should we fund at YC Research?","stance":"reference","why":"Part of a chain of sources linking nutrigenomics and lifespan.","quote":"Nutrigenomics, Epigenetics, and Stress Tolerance","text":"AI applied to understanding the hidden relationships linking epigentics and extending human lifespan via algorithmically engineered diet, nutrition and longevity genomics: The precedent: Laura Deming: Making a business out of fighting the ills of aging [LINK] Cynthia Kenyon: Experiments that hint of longer lives (Cynthia now works at Google's Calico Labs) [LINK] Now connect this: Nutrigenomics, Epigenetics, and Stress Tolerance [VIDEO] Now combine with: What is Epigenetics? with Nessa Carey The last 10mins are very important, in particular minute 35:06 [LINK] Statistical modeling of biomedical corpora: mining the Caenorhabditis Genetic Center Bibliography for genes related to life span - Blei DM1, Franks K, Jordan MI, Mian IS. - [LINK] Combining the worlds variants of pattern recogition, machine learning and AI with data associated to epigentic and standard genomic experiments and information, we should be able to discover hidden relationships that will enable advancement in extending human lifespan. Revenue is something we'll need to focus on and that may come in the form of consumer applications that algorithmically engineer their epigenome with data from companies like 23andMe, Navigenics, Ubiome etc. connected to Nutrigenomics including plant compounds, phytochemicals mapped all the way to the consumer through foods connected to apps connected shopping and recipe experiences. Finally connect to CRISPR and Gene Therapy Jennifer Doudna, inventor of CRISPR: We can now edit o"},{"id":9575126,"author":"meeper16","month":"2015-05","story":"Russian Billionaire Dmitry Itskov Plans on Becoming Immortal by 2045","stance":"reference","why":"On epigenetics and extending human lifespan.","quote":"Epigentics and extending human lifespan","text":"Were almost near the starting line with research and companies like Human Longevity, CalicoLabs(Google,Genentech), GenoPharmix, Buck Institute, SENS Foundation and this: Cynthia Kenyon: Experiments that hint of longer [LINK] Epigentics and extending human lifespan: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9457368,"author":"cymetica","month":"2015-04","story":"Large New Survey Tracks Telomere Length and Mortality","stance":"reference","why":"Explains micronutrients like vitamin D and magnesium affecting telomerase and FOXO3.","quote":"using micronutrients such as Vitamin D, magnesium as described here","text":"It's also interesting to note how we can affect the length of our Telomeres via Telomerase and FOXO3 genes using micronutrients such as Vitamin D, magnesium as described here [VIDEO] This is being worked on by companies in the epigenetics and human life extension areas: [LINK] (Google and Genentech) [LINK] (Berkeley Lab) [LINK] (Aubrey De Grey & Peter Theil)"}]},"tNmgmwEtoWE":{"wy":"Cited as the debunking of Devin's marketing claims in skeptical discussions of AI software engineers.","m":[{"id":42387661,"author":"Topfi","month":"2024-12","story":"Devin is now generally available","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"a company that has been caught lying about this very product","text":"No public testing, no benchmarks, no clear information on context window size or restrictions for extensive use, no comparison with the newest Claude Sonnet 3.5 or O1, nothing. What we do get is a price of $ 500,- per month from a company that has been caught lying about this very product [0] and has never allowed independent testing. Cognition, I am sorry to tell you, but there is no reason to trust you. In fact, there are multiple good reasons no to, even if you offered Devin at a fraction. If this were e.g. Anthropic launching a new beyond Opus size model that was still performant and came with \"chain-of-thought\" capabilities, a far more extensive context window that still fully passes needle in haystack and is absolutely solid in sourcing from provided files, keeps on track even when provided with large documents, has few or no restrictions on usage and comes with extensive, verifiable benchmarks that showcase this offering being a significant upgrade over other models, maybe such a price could be justified. You know why Cognition? Because they haven’t actively lied. What they did instead was let people use their models and actually test the advantages. Even Claude Instant way back when had certain use cases that made them have their own niche and showed they could execute before expanding with 2 and the larger context, then 3 with more applications. You never did any of that, you never gave anyone reason to believe what you claim, you didn’t even release benchmarks. See "},{"id":41609216,"author":"jaredsohn","month":"2024-09","story":"What the hell happened to Devin AI?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"calling Devin an AI software engineer that does Upwork tasks is a lie","text":"The last thing I saw on it was a video saying that calling Devin an AI software engineer that does Upwork tasks is a lie based in what was shared in an earlier video. [VIDEO] \"I broke down the Devin Upwork video frame by frame, and here I show what Devin was supposed to do, what it actually managed to do instead, and how bad a job of that it did. On the whole that's not surprising given the current state of Generative AI, and I wouldn't be bothering to debunk it, except: 1) The company lied about what Devin could do in the video description, and 2) a lot of people uncritically parroted the lie all over the Internet, and 3) That caused a lot of non-technical people to believe that AI might replace programmers soon. \""},{"id":40250133,"author":"siliconc0w","month":"2024-05","story":"I Spent 24 Hours with GitHub Copilot Workspaces","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"It created a file in the repo and spent a lot of time debugging its own unnecessary code","text":"This debunking video( [VIDEO] ) of Devin really questioned the usefulness for me. It created a file in the repo and spent a lot of time debugging its own unnecessary code rather than reading the Read Me to understand that the code it needed to use already existed and just needed to be run with different inputs. It's not clear it we're even near a point where it can independently and meaningfully contribute to an existing codebase rather these greenfield demos. Feels similar to the self-driving AI hype where level 5 is still pretty far from realized (Waymo is closest but AIUI still uses a lot of remote human intervention)."}]},"u8qgehH3kEQ":{"wy":"Used as a joke or meme whenever threads mention fake TV hacking or pair programming.","m":[{"id":47084582,"author":"globular-toast","month":"2026-02","story":"A Beginner's Guide to Split Keyboards","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I actually want to try that just to see the looks on people's faces when I'm typing on two keyboards. It's the opposite of this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41724572,"author":"tivert","month":"2024-10","story":"GitHub's Copilot lies about its documentation. Why would I trust it with my code","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"even more ridiculous nonsense fantasy than a typical doctor or a lawyer show","text":"> Most non-technical business stakeholders don't really understand what we do. You don't see shows about awesome devs doing awesome shit, like you do for lawyers, doctors, business people, etc. All they know about us is that we're nerds who work like two hours a day and can't be bothered to go into the office. You do, but it's even more ridiculous nonsense fantasy than a typical doctor or a lawyer show (e.g. all the hacker girl scenes like [LINK] , [VIDEO] )."},{"id":39418141,"author":"MissTake","month":"2024-02","story":"Apple Watch Ultra 2 Hacked","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"shows like NCIS and their ilk are factual based docudramas","text":"This has nothing to do with hacking and everything to do with the ghost touch issues that apparently affect multiple Series 9 and Ultra 2 users (although I’ve not seen it on my series 9 myself). Way back in the early days of Android (pre Ice Cream Sandwich), I spoke to one co-worker who told me that when they looked at their Google Map app while driving it would show their actual physical car (and all the other surrounding vehicles etc.) on the satellite view - in motion - in real time. No manner of attempts by me to state why this was impossible would dissuade them and they went away thinking I was the technical idiot as a result. So “we are in control”? Yeah, no. This is what happens when folk think that shows like NCIS and their ilk are factual based docudramas. Obligatory link: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32485783,"author":"LinuxBender","month":"2022-08","story":"Ask HN: Does anyone here not own a phone?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"the meme from the dueling-hackers from that show","text":"At first I thought you were kidding. I had to look up this person had never watched NCIS and we do have some eerie resemblances. I served in the same time-frame as the fictional character and have some of the same background, though different branch. Suffered the same young tragedy except I had no other family at the time. Very similar training. That show has 19 seasons. Why was it so popular? I've only seen the meme from the dueling-hackers from that show. [1] [1] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":23858634,"author":"wheelerwj","month":"2020-07","story":"European Police Hacked Secret Phone Network, Used AI for Major Bust","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"nothing beats NCIS","text":"> Reminds me of that video where the caption goes “they used coding and algorithms”. nothing beats NCIS: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18440072,"author":"dragontamer","month":"2018-11","story":"An 1861 illustrated Japanese book on the American revolutionary war","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"no one watches NCIS to get accurate depictions of hackers","text":"John Adams is fighting a giant snake. [LINK] --------------- I guess you can say that the typical American doesn't know about computers in the `00s: [VIDEO] But no one watches NCIS to get accurate depictions of hackers. People watch NCIS to get entertained. Pop-culture in general doesn't actually CARE how things work, its entertainment value, NOT accuracy."},{"id":12102078,"author":"calgoo","month":"2016-07","story":"Mr Robot S02E01 easter egg","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"My favorite hacking scene is still from Scorpions (well there are two, the one below and the one where they are hacking a jet, with a RJ45 cable, from a car while landing). \"We need to hack NORAD!\" Guy reaches for keyboard (without touching it), \"Done!\". Sarcasm apart, while Mr.Robot has good tech, I got bored by the story line around episode 4 or 5. I think it was just too slow for my taste. Edit: Link to first \"Hack\" mentioned: [LINK] Edit2: Link to the double keyboard NCIS video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7195422,"author":"fuzzix","month":"2014-02","story":"NBC Story about Hacking at Sochi is 100% Fraudulent","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"So this is what the pair programming experience is like!","text":"> [VIDEO] So this is what the pair programming experience is like! I like to imagine the next scene is the two >1000 hacksaws explaining ssh, remote X and basic Cisco admin to the yank-happy cord monster. Ever been on IRC? It's exciting stuff: [LINK]"},{"id":4818411,"author":"rmc","month":"2012-11","story":"Make legal documents subject to software patent laws","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"2 people typing on the same keyboard to try to fight off a hacker","text":"Or how Hackers was a riot because of how hilariously wrong it got ... well ... everything that matters? Compared to some TV shows, Hackers is accurate. I give you 2 people typing on the same keyboard to try to fight off a hacker. [VIDEO]"}]},"uMgTIlgYB-U":{"wy":"HN links it as the original announcement of Lumen (later Firefly), for people wanting technical detail on the project.","q":{"x":"If you are looking for more substantial technical information","i":32706749,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":35625137,"author":"bcardarella","month":"2023-04","story":"Firefly – A new compiler and runtime for BEAM languages","stance":"reference","why":"Core dev's earlier presentation of the project now named Firefly.","quote":"This is a project formerly known as Lumen, which myself and the other core devs presented on at ElixirConf a few years ago","text":"This is a project formerly known as Lumen, which myself and the other core devs presented on at ElixirConf a few years ago: [VIDEO] A lot has changed since then. The name change came about because we found too many projects named \"Lumen\" also were launched around the same time or shortly thereafter. Originally I envisioned a path towards creating a new WASM framework to build web applications using Elixir. Since then LiveView has come out and has made this less of a concern for us. While WASM compilation will be possible and we hope to backfill the runtime needs for web development the real focus and interest in this project for me is... WASI compilation. With Firefly we will be able build self-contained binaries that will have all (many) of the benefits building applications with the BEAM and distributed systems. If WASI can run on something you should be able to compile your Elixir applications with Firefly and target your desired chipset. We recently reached a milestone that we are hoping to release a blog post about it in the near future."},{"id":32706749,"author":"hosh","month":"2022-09","story":"DockYard R&D: FireFly Optimizes Your Elixir Compilation","stance":"reference","why":"Original Lumen announcement with technical detail and demo.","quote":"If you are looking for more substantial technical information","text":"If you are looking for more substantial technical information, they are found in the ElixirConf ‘19 keynote speech and demo when Lumen was announced. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20850870,"author":"juhatl","month":"2019-09","story":"Lumen: An alternative BEAM implementation, designed for WebAssembly","stance":"reference","why":"Keynote introducing Lumen, an alternative BEAM implementation.","quote":"I was left excited about Lumen in general","text":"I watched the ElixirConf 2019 keynote [1] as soon as it came up. While I was left excited about Lumen in general, I wasn't entirely sure what kinds of things Lumen would be most useful for. Being able to ship a single binary (like with Go or Rust) sounds handy, but shipping a tarball hasn't been that much of a challenge either. Being able to run BEAM code in the browser also sounds handy, but due to size issues I probably wouldn't want to ship the entire BEAM to my users either, right? Can someone with a more thorough understanding of Lumen share some thoughts on this? [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20851601,"author":"lxrbst","month":"2019-09","story":"Lumen: An alternative BEAM implementation, designed for WebAssembly","stance":"reference","why":"Presentation on Lumen; commenter still unsure of its use case.","quote":"Cool idea, cool technology, cool project.","text":"Cool idea, cool technology, cool project. I really just don't get the \"why\", even after watching the ElixirConf presentation [1] about it. Like what is the use-case for this, specifically? [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"ucEs0nBuowE":{"wy":"HN cites it as a glimpse of a fearful, disorganized Russian leadership just before the invasion of Ukraine.","q":{"x":"it subtly presents a leadership structure within the Russian government that is both disorganized, and systemically fearful of Putin","i":30531044,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31094883,"author":"coolhoody","month":"2022-04","story":"War of memes: Why Z-war won't end with peace","stance":"reference","why":"Clip of the spy chief humiliated by Putin before the invasion.","quote":"Even his spy chief was fucked up","text":"(I'm Russian). This war was started by a single madman. If not him, it would never happen. Even his spy chief was fucked up [0] when he realized what's going to happen. Yes — at least 70% support whatever he does — and I tried hard to disprove this painful fact to myself. But couldn't. The only excuse is the 20 years of hardcore TV brainwashing — but it's not too great of an excuse. Better than in nazi Germany, but not enough. All that cultural banter the article mentions never caused any actual sense of war — it was more like a Texan joking about a Canadian. I don't know how to live with this shame. Can't even post in Russian, at local forums. All those stupid faces in the 'do you support the war' Youtube steet interviews… Yes, there are others — Navalny was poisoned, and is now behind the bars. Nemtsov was murdered in front of Kremlin long before the war. Kasparov, Nevzorov condemned this crime. Hundreds of thousands of IT left the country — unable to fight unarmed. But still… Trying to drown myself in English, jokes, work to keep the sanity. Doesn't work half of the times. Jokes clumsy, work fucked. Ukraine is now a moral leader of Europe. It will be restored — every single broken window. And people will live free and proud. It's my only bright cloud now. [0] Fucked up spy chief — [VIDEO]"},{"id":30531044,"author":"015a","month":"2022-03","story":"What happened to Russias Air Force? U.S. officials, experts stumped","stance":"reference","why":"Shows a disorganized Russian leadership fearful of Putin.","quote":"it subtly presents a leadership structure within the Russian government that is both disorganized, and systemically fearful of Putin","text":"Even this recent video, which presents a bad look for their leadership, they were alright with it being public; it must be the best case, and it subtly presents a leadership structure within the Russian government that is both disorganized, and systemically fearful of Putin. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30493807,"author":"NicoJuicy","month":"2022-02","story":"Neutral Swiss poised to freeze Russian assets – president","stance":"reference","why":"BBC clip of Putin dressing down his spy chief as sign of internal trouble.","quote":"Something really seems off internally","text":"Don't underestimate the reliance a dictator has on his cronies. There's a really good video about this by CGP Grey that talks about dictatorships: [LINK] And then think about some of the latest updates: - nuclear threat - increasing embassy protests - seemingly total reliance on a new actor with a yet unknown trust wordiness ( i don't think anyone wants to be associated with Russia currently) - travel restrictions for the rich + family ( superyacht, flights, ... ) - real estate seizure - money seizure - reduced currency value - halved bank reserves ( because of seizure) - potential family issues ( a lot of Russians have Ukraine ties/origin) - family protests on the streets, civilian unrest ( anti-war), as I think most Russians don't want to be related as an aggressor - neglect of his cronies worries - booting off Swift/Switzerland and large expansion of targetted individuals Something really seems off internally: eg. [VIDEO]"}]},"vqeIeIcDHD0":{"wy":"Shared in threads on chronic fatigue and her return as an update on how she is doing.","q":{"x":"Her latest video, showing her out of bed and going for short walks, is here","i":44745987,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":47237180,"author":"ck2","month":"2026-03","story":"My first science video in 3 years (Pysics Girl)","stance":"reference","why":"Her long-covid battle and recovery, rough at first.","quote":"caution for those currently sick as it's a rough watch at first","text":"video about her long-covid battle and recovery here: [VIDEO] (caution for those currently sick as it's a rough watch at first)"},{"id":44745987,"author":"jjraden","month":"2025-07","story":"Problem solving using Markov chains (2007) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Dianna Cowern out of bed and walking after long covid.","quote":"Her latest video, showing her out of bed and going for short walks, is here","text":"Speaking of eyes, Veritasium's occasional collaborator Dianna Cowern (Physics Girl) is doing much better after complications from long covid left her bedridden for two years. It's good to see her up and at 'em. Her latest video, showing her out of bed and going for short walks, is here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44382767,"author":"kgwxd","month":"2025-06","story":"PEM Fatigue Can Shatter a Person","stance":"reference","why":"Update video on Physics Girl's recovery.","quote":"looks like things are improving!","text":"Hadn't thought to check for updates on Physics Girl in a long while, looks like things are improving! [VIDEO]"}]},"wCrtk-pyP0I":{"wy":"Mentioned as a classic physics demo in threads on microwaves and related resonance effects.","m":[{"id":42546683,"author":"gsf_emergency","month":"2024-12","story":"Coupling nitrogen-vacancy center spins in diamond to a grape dimer","stance":"reference","why":"Shows grape plasma needs no cutting, just a watch-glass","quote":"No cutting needed whatsoever, but you do need a watch-glass.","text":"Pedantry, from a promateur, but also see the Veritasium tube linked elsewhere here: 0. No cutting needed whatsoever, but you do need a watch-glass. (Keeps the grapes in contact) [VIDEO]"},{"id":39857005,"author":"1letterunixname","month":"2024-03","story":"We made a hot dog talk with RF [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among related classics","quote":"Related classics","text":"Related classics: (2019) BigClive overclocks a 120v Presto hot dogger to 250v [LINK] (2019) Veratasium grape microwave plasma [VIDEO] (2022) BigClive The dangers of microwave transformers [LINK] (2016) BigClive Electrocution risk of cheap USB chargers [LINK] (2020) ElectroBOOM proves 5G isn't going to give you cancer or eat your children [LINK]"},{"id":25324922,"author":"admiralspoo","month":"2020-12","story":"'Havana syndrome' likely caused by directed microwaves – US report","stance":"reference","why":"Related resonance effect in microwaves","quote":"For a related effect, see","text":"It depends on the frequency. The brain is mostly water, but there are clear differences in the dielectric constant of air, the skull (bone) and the sweet delicious gray matter inside the cavity. By tuning the frequency of the waves, you can set up resonances in the cavity. For a related effect, see [VIDEO]"},{"id":42539421,"author":"xeonmc","month":"2024-12","story":"Coupling nitrogen-vacancy center spins in diamond to a grape dimer","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"veritasium video [VIDEO] Essentially, when this gets commercialized we'll be able to make room-temperature qubits using two grapes and a diamond in a microwave oven. This opens up a whole new field of \"grapetronics\" for scalable quantum computing hardware."}]},"xxX81WmXjPg":{"wy":"Linked whenever email validation comes up as a fun illustration of how deceptively difficult it is.","q":{"x":"illustrates the difficulty of email validation well","i":31096558,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":44934352,"author":"stavros","month":"2025-08","story":"\"Email is Easy\" email address quiz","stance":"reference","why":"Author's earlier presentation on same topic.","quote":"","text":"This is fun, but it feels suspiciously close to my old presentation about the exact same thing: [VIDEO] Maybe it's just a coincidence, but a shoutout would have been nice if not."},{"id":31096558,"author":"stavros","month":"2022-04","story":"Stop Validating Email Addresses with Regex (2012)","stance":"reference","why":"Author's fun talk on email validation difficulty.","quote":"illustrates the difficulty of email validation well","text":"I gave a very fun talk on the topic in FOSDEM (VOLUME WARNING!): [VIDEO] It's more a joke than edifying, but it was very fun for me (and, I think, the audience), and illustrates the difficulty of email validation well."},{"id":26423863,"author":"sybercecurity","month":"2021-03","story":"I bought 300 emoji domain names from Kazakhstan and built an email service","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on difficulties validating email addresses.","quote":"always relevant","text":"always relevant: \"So you think you can validate email addresses\": [VIDEO] Which is also why proposals to put email certs (S/MIME, OpenPGP) in the DNS led to hashing the local part, as DNS only allows ASCII and is case insensitive."},{"id":20106429,"author":"StavrosK","month":"2019-06","story":"Firefox Monitor","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on email address validity.","quote":"Here's a talk on the topic","text":"The email standard does include the +, yes. Here's a talk on the topic: [VIDEO] But yes, that's why Fastmail supports the alternative syntax."}]},"xxYmMRxnEic":{"wy":"HN uses it to illustrate how the film's wireframe skyline effect was made with practical miniatures and as an example of retro computer-display aesthetics.","q":{"x":"it was cheaper to build a miniture New York skyline and attach fluorescent tape to the edges","i":24526507,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":37376863,"author":"morkalork","month":"2023-09","story":"Index of Aesthetics","stance":"reference","why":"Green laser grid sequence from the film.","quote":"there's an epic \"green laser grid\" sequence as he flies in to the city!","text":"I don't have anything to add to the argument just that I happened to watch Escape from New York this weekend and there's an epic \"green laser grid\" sequence as he flies in to the city! [VIDEO]"},{"id":25400245,"author":"JKCalhoun","month":"2020-12","story":"Real wireframe teapot mounted inside a Commodore Pet display (2004)","stance":"reference","why":"Clip of the glider navigation sequence.","quote":"Clip from the film","text":"\"Some sources cite [James] Cameron as the brains behind the glider navigation sequence...\" [LINK] Clip from the film: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24526507,"author":"rkachowski","month":"2020-09","story":"It was more efficient to blow up a real 747 than to use miniatures or CGI","stance":"reference","why":"Clip showing miniature skyline with wireframe effect.","quote":"it was cheaper to build a miniture New York skyline and attach fluorescent tape to the edges","text":"It seems like we have come full circle, in Escape from New York (1981) it was cheaper to build a miniture New York skyline and attach fluorescent tape to the edges rather than create a CG wireframe model for the \"computers\" in the main characters glider.[1][2] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}]}});
